gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 24, lesson_id: 25, number: 3, title: "MAIN USAGES OF GENITIVE", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.405376Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.405376Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 25, lesson_id: 25, number: 4, title: "NUMBERS ABOVE TWENTY", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.413134Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.413134Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 26, lesson_id: 2, number: 1, title: "NO INDEFINITE ARTICLE", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.305475Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.307919Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Irish has no indefinite article. 'A man' is simply expressed by <strong>fear</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 27, lesson_id: 2, number: 2, title: "THE VERB TÁ", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.311306Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.311306Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 28, lesson_id: 2, number: 3, title: "CONTRAST", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.347802Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.349968Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In Irish, contrast is expressed by a special set of pronouns, rather than by stress.</p><p><strong>Tá mise anseo ach tá sise ansin.</strong> — I am here but she is there.</p><p>Contrast forms have also some marginal functions which are dealt with in later lessons.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 29, lesson_id: 2, number: 4, title: "MEANING OF ANSIÚD", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.351062Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.353007Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ansiúd</strong> means 'there' and emphasises distance (i.e. not present or not previously mentioned), as opposed to <strong>ansin</strong> 'there' which simply points out where something is.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 30, lesson_id: 3, number: 1, title: "LENITION", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.673383Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.682777Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>When a proper noun (i.e. names of people or places, but not of languages) follows directly, in genitive relation, on another noun, its initial consonant changes, e.g. c to ch. This table shows how the consonants affected by this change called 'lenition' are replaced by a related sound:</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 31, lesson_id: 3, number: 2, title: "LENITION IN PERSONAL NAMES", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.699681Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.701628Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Often in rural areas where Irish is spoken, a person whose 'official' name is for example Máirtín Ó Bia and whose father (or some other important relation) is Seáinín, may be known as Máirtín Sheáinín (Máirtín of Seáinín, Seáinín's Máirtín).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 32, lesson_id: 4, number: 1, title: "VOCATIVE PARTICLE", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.688757Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.692421Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The vocative particle <em>a</em> //ə, which is not stressed, lenites a following noun.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 33, lesson_id: 4, number: 2, title: "GENDER", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.707057Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.709523Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In Irish a noun is either masculine or feminine. In the vocabularies in each lesson feminine (fem.) will be marked; all other nouns are masculine.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 34, lesson_id: 4, number: 3, title: "ADJECTIVES", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.727145Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.727145Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 35, lesson_id: 4, number: 4, title: "LENITION OF SLENDER L AND N", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.737716Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.739574Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Lenition of <em>l</em> or <em>n</em> is not shown in spelling but a distinction is made in pronunciation by some speakers of the dialect.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 36, lesson_id: 5, number: 1, title: "USE OF SÉ AND SÍ", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.608866Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.610698Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In general, a masculine noun is referred to by <strong>sé</strong> and a feminine noun by <strong>sí</strong>. In the case of a human, <strong>sé</strong> or <strong>sí</strong> is used according to sex, e.g. although <em>cailín</em> 'girl' is a masculine noun, <strong>sí</strong> is used. <strong>Sé</strong> is used to describe a general condition or state, e.g. <strong>Tá sé go breá</strong> 'It is fine'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 37, lesson_id: 5, number: 2, title: "THE DEFINITE ARTICLE", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.617414Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.619041Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An</strong> /NƏ/ is unstressed (the main stress is on the following noun), and the <em>n</em> is not pronounced when the article occurs between consonants, e.g. between <strong>níl</strong> and <strong>gasúr</strong>: /N'Í:1' Ə ga:su:r/.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 38, lesson_id: 5, number: 3, title: "ECLIPSIS", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.623674Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.625867Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Following almost all prepositions used with the definite article, the initial consonant of a singular noun is changed:</p><p>The <em>c</em> in <em>cloch</em> is retained in spelling but is not pronounced, so that <strong>gcloch</strong> is pronounced as though spelt <em>gloch</em>. The <em>c</em> is said to be 'eclipsed' and this type of change is called 'eclipsis'.</p><p>This table shows how the consonants affected by eclipsis change to a related sound:</p><p class=\"note\">Although <em>t</em>, <em>d</em> are not affected, e.g. <strong>ar an doras</strong>, <strong>leis an tine</strong>, in all other cases where eclipsis is the rule, <em>d</em> changes to <em>nd</em> (pronounced as though spelt <em>n</em> /N/ before a 'broad' vowel, /N'/ before a 'slender' vowel) and <em>t</em> changes to <em>dt</em> (pronounced as though spelt <em>d</em> /d/ before a 'broad' vowel, /d'/ before a 'slender' vowel).</p><p class=\"note\">As can be seen from the table, the adjective is (as is normal, see Lesson 4) lenited following a feminine noun, e.g. <strong>ar an bhfuinneoig mhór</strong> 'on the big window'; a masculine noun remains unlenited, e.g. <strong>ar an ngasúr mór</strong> 'on the big child'. The adjective is, however, lenited after a masculine proper noun, e.g. <strong>faoin Lochán Bheag</strong> 'about Lochán Beag').</p><p class=\"note\">A preposition ending in a vowel is written together with <em>an</em>, e.g. <strong>faoin bpota</strong> 'under the pot', showing the actual pronunciation (see App. 1.5). The <em>n</em>-sound in <em>an</em> is almost always pronounced broad before a 'broad' vowel, and slender before a 'slender' vowel: <strong>ar an abhainn</strong> /er'əN auN'/, but <strong>ar an éan</strong> /er'əN' e:N/.</p><p class=\"note\">In other circumstances, where eclipsis comes after words not written with a final <em>n</em>, <em>n-</em> is prefixed to the following vowel (examples see Lessons 10 and 11).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 39, lesson_id: 6, number: 1, title: "PAST TENSE OF VERB TÁ", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.854261Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.854261Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 40, lesson_id: 6, number: 2, title: "FUTURE TENSE OF TÁ", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.882653Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.882653Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 41, lesson_id: 6, number: 3, title: "LENITION AND ECLIPSIS AFTER MAIN VERBAL PARTICLES", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.900793Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.900793Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 42, lesson_id: 6, number: 4, title: "INDEPENDENT AND DEPENDENT FORMS", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.908016Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.909514Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Where there is a difference, the form used in a statement, e.g. <strong>bhí</strong>, is called the independent form and that after <strong>ní</strong>, <strong>an</strong>, <strong>go</strong>, <strong>nach</strong>, e.g. <strong>raibh</strong>, the dependent form.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 43, lesson_id: 6, number: 5, title: "RESPONSES", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.910091Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.913283Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>There is no word corresponding to English 'yes' or 'no'. The appropriate form of the verb is repeated:</p><table><tr><th>Question or statement</th><th>Response</th></tr><tbody><tr><td><strong>An bhfuil Cáit anseo?</strong> — Is Cáit here?</td><td><strong>Tá.</strong> Yes, she is.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Níl.</strong> No, she is not.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beidh muid anseo amárach.</strong> — We will be here tomorrow.</td><td><strong>Beidh.</strong> Yes, we will.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ní bheidh.</strong> No, we won't.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nach bhfuiltear sásta anseo?</strong> — Are people not content here?</td><td><strong>Táthar.</strong> Yes, they are.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Níltear.</strong> No, they are not.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In normal unstressed responses, unless the subject is included in the verb form, e.g. <strong>táthar</strong>, there is no need to repeat it, e.g. <strong>An bhfuil Cáit anseo?</strong> <strong>Tá</strong> (not <strong>Tá Cáit</strong>).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 44, lesson_id: 6, number: 6, title: "INDIRECT QUESTIONS ('IF/WHETHER ... OR NOT')", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.919127Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.921112Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl mé cinnte an bhfuil Cáit anseo nó nach bhfuil.</strong> — I am not certain whether Cáit is here or not.</p><p>The indirect question in Irish is generally exactly the same as the indirect question, e.g. <strong>An bhfuil Cáit anseo nó nach bhfuil?</strong> — 'Is Cáit here (or (is she) not)?'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 45, lesson_id: 6, number: 7, title: "EXPRESSING 'TO HAVE'", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.922566Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.923923Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá teach ag an mbean.</strong> — The woman has a house.</p><p>'Somebody has something' is expressed by saying (literally) 'There is something at somebody'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 46, lesson_id: 7, number: 1, title: "THE HABITUAL TENSES OF TÁ", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.361476Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.361476Z")
