gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 48, lesson_id: 7, number: 3, title: "SUMMARY TABLE OF VERB TÁ", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.386275Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.386275Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 49, lesson_id: 7, number: 4, title: "COMBINED FORMS", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.393466Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.393466Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 50, lesson_id: 7, number: 5, title: "ADJECTIVE MODIFYING NOUNS IN PLURAL", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.405208Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.405208Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 51, lesson_id: 7, number: 6, title: "THE VOCATIVE PLURAL", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.413241Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.413241Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 52, lesson_id: 8, number: 1, title: "IMPERATIVE OF VERB TÁ", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.464579Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.464579Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 53, lesson_id: 8, number: 2, title: "IF' CLAUSES", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.478056Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.478056Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 54, lesson_id: 8, number: 3, title: "THE DEFINITE ARTICLE (SINGULAR)", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.496608Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.496608Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 55, lesson_id: 8, number: 4, title: "USAGES OF DEFINITE ARTICLE", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.511079Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.511079Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 56, lesson_id: 9, number: 1, title: "PLURAL OF DEFINITE ARTICLE", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.782598Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.786039Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The form <em>na</em>, which is unstressed, is used with all plural nouns. It prefixes <em>h</em> to any noun beginning with a vowel.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 57, lesson_id: 9, number: 2, title: "DISJUNCTIVE PRONOUNS", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.793336Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.793336Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 58, lesson_id: 9, number: 3, title: "USE OF FEW 'SELF'", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.805135Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.805135Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 59, lesson_id: 9, number: 4, title: "COMPOUND SUBJECT OR OBJECT", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.818751Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.820422Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><em>Féin</em> is added to the pronoun where there are two or more subjects or objects. The first person comes first, e.g. <em>mé féin agus Ruairí</em>. As only the first part of a compound subject directly follows the verb, a disjunctive pronoun is used for the other(s), e.g. <em>tú féin agus é féin</em>. Contrast is expressed in the normal way by use of contrast pronouns, e.g. <em>mise agus thusa agus ise</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 60, lesson_id: 9, number: 5, title: "THE ADJECTIVES SEO, SIN, ÚD/SIÚD", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.825654Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.825654Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 61, lesson_id: 10, number: 1, title: "IF-CLAUSES TO EXPRESS A CONDITION", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.750384Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.752022Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Dhá 'if (... were)' causes eclipsis and is followed by the conditional. The dependent form is used where this distinction is made.</p><p>Mara 'if (... were) not, unless (... were)' causes eclipsis and is followed by the conditional. The dependent form is used where this distinction is made.</p><p>Dhá or similarly mara may be variously translated as 'if... were', 'if ... had been', or as 'if... should be', 'if... should have been'. It expresses a condition or hypothesis.</p><p class=\"note\">In a number of verbs where a distinction in pronunciation can be made between the conditional and a past subjunctive, older speakers may use a past subjunctive. The conditional is, however, the norm.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 62, lesson_id: 10, number: 2, title: "POSSESSIVE ADJECTIVES", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.757433Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.757433Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 63, lesson_id: 10, number: 3, title: "USE OF CEANN/ CUID TO EXPRESS 'MINE', 'YOURS', ETC.", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.772943Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.774157Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ceann (lit. 'head' or 'end') can be used to mean 'one'. 'Mine, yours' etc. when referring to a singular noun is expressed as mo, do ... ceann 'my one, mine' etc., and when referring to plurals, collective, mass or abstract nouns, as mo, do ... chuidsa 'my share, mine'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 64, lesson_id: 11, number: 1, title: "THE COPULA IS", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.398496Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.401682Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Is, traditionally called the copula, is used to link two nouns or pronouns. The appropriate disjunctive pronoun, e.g. mé/mise/mé féin, thú/thusa/thú féin etc. is used. Is is never stressed and is pronounced with a broad s /s/, except before a pronoun spelt with an initial i or e. The actual pronunciation is sometimes shown by spelling is é as 'sé, is í as 'sí, is iad as 'siad.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 65, lesson_id: 11, number: 2, title: "USES OF COPULA WITH ADJECTIVE", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.434455Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.436131Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Normally the verb tá is used with the adjectives, e.g. tá sé glic 'he is cunning', tá an leabhar spéisiúil 'the book is interesting'. The copula is, however, has a limited use with adjectives.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 66, lesson_id: 11, number: 3, title: "NUMBERS ONE TO TEN", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.443640Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.445257Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Bád amháin literally means 'only one boat'. For emphasis aon /e:N/, /e:N'/ (the n is slender before i or e) is used, e.g. aon bhád amháin.</p><p>These numbers do not stand alone. They are always followed by a noun. The numbers from aon 'one' to sé 'six' cause lenition, e.g. sé bhád 'six boats'. The numbers from seacht to deich cause eclipsis, e.g. seacht mbád 'seven boats', deich n-éan 'ten birds'.</p><p>The singular of a noun follows all these numbers. However, certain nouns, mostly denoting a measurement, take the plural (or a special form used only in this case) after numbers higher than two. In this case initial consonants are unaffected and trí 'three', ceithre 'four', sé 'six' prefix h to an initial vowel:</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 67, lesson_id: 13, number: 1, title: "DIRECT RELATIVE CLAUSES", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.332765Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.334901Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>'Who(m), that, which' are expressed by the unstressed direct relative particle <em>a</em> /ə/ which lenites all verb forms (except <em>tá</em>, which has the special relative form <em>atá</em>).</p><p>A broad <em>s</em> is added to the present habitual, e.g. <em>a bhionns</em>, and to the future, e.g. <em>a bheas</em> (from <em>beidh</em>; in spelling -idh is replaced by -(e)as) of all forms which are not combined (see Lesson 7). Combined forms do not add <em>s</em>, e.g. <em>Tá an fear a thigim go deas.</em> 'The man whom I understand is nice.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 68, lesson_id: 13, number: 2, title: "PRONOUNS STANDING ALONE", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.359833Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.365399Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The ordinary disjunctive form, e.g. <em>mé, thú, é</em>, can never stand alone. Contrast or emphatic forms are used, e.g. <em>mise, thusa, eisean</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 69, lesson_id: 13, number: 3, title: "PREFIXED ADJECTIVES", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.371865Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.374610Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ordinarily, the smaller number of adjectives which are prefixed are given the primary stress, e.g. in <em>drochbhád</em>, <em>droch-</em> takes the primary and <em>-bhád</em> the secondary stress. As is the case with all compound words, any prefix causes lenition.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 70, lesson_id: 13, number: 4, title: "FEMININE OF REFERENCE", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.395399Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.396958Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Certain nouns, though masculine, are normally or frequently referred to by <em>sí</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 71, lesson_id: 14, number: 1, title: "VERBAL NOUN", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.584145Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.586926Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The verbal noun or infinitive (it is more appropriate to look at it as a noun based on a verb) can (a) have the same form as the root, e.g. fás; (b) various endings added to the root, e.g. -(e)adh: pósadh, briseadh; -t: imirt.</p><p>There are three verbal nouns, e.g. goil 'to go', teacht/tiocht 'to come', bheith 'to be', which are preceded by an unstressed particle a /ə/ which lenites, e.g. tá mé sásta a thíocht anois 'I am content to come now'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 72, lesson_id: 14, number: 2, title: "SEO, SIN, SIÚD 'THIS HERE / THAT THERE IS'", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.611781Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.613550Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Seo, sin, siúd, corresponding to anseo, ansin, ansiúd (see Lesson 2), are used before a noun to express 'this here/that there/'that' there (person, thing) is'. When a noun is definite, that is a noun with the article, the possessive adjective, or a proper noun, e.g. an bord, mo bhord, Cáit, é, í or iad are required and they follow the usages of sé, sí, siad (see Lessons 5 and 13). Before é, í or iad, seo is pronounced with a final /w/ as though spelt seobh.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 73, lesson_id: 15, number: 1, title: "THE PREPOSITION AG WITH VERBAL NOUN", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.316937Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.322845Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ag /əg'/ 'at' which is unstressed precedes all verbal nouns (excepting bheith) in 'progressive' constructions of the sort 'the tree is growing'. The g in ag is pronounced only before a vowel. It is usually broad before a, o, u, e.g. ag ól /gə o:L/, and slender before e, i, e.g. ag imirt /əg' im'ərt'/.</p><p>A definite noun preceded by the verbal noun is in a genitive relation and therefore (see Lesson 3) lenites names of people or places, e.g. ag moladh Cháit 'praising Cáit', lit. 'at Cáit's praising'.</p><p>In some set phrases, a common noun is lenited, e.g. ag ól bhainne 'drinking milk', ag imirt chártaí 'playing cards', ag cur phrátaí 'sowing potatoes'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 74, lesson_id: 15, number: 2, title: "USE OF A (+ LENITION) WITH VERBAL NOUN", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.340324Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.343039Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The general rule that a + lenition is used before a verbal noun whose direct object precedes it (see Lesson 13) holds good, e.g. Cé (direct object)... a phósadh?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 75, lesson_id: 15, number: 3, title: "VERBAL NOUN WITH PRONOUN OBJECT", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.343789Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.345717Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>When a pronoun object, e.g. mé is required in a sentence of the sort Tá sé ag bualadh 'He is hitting', do /gə/ (a preposition meaning 'to' which is unstressed and pronounced as though spelt go) and the appropriate possessive adjective is used:</p><p>Do combines with a and ar to give dhá and dhár. Dhá, dhár and dhúr are all pronounced as though spelt dhá.</p><p>Contrast and emphasis are expressed in the normal way for possessive adjectives (see Lesson 10), e.g. tá sé do mo bhualadhsa 'he is hitting me', tá muid dhá bhualadh seisean 'we are hitting him'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 76, lesson_id: 15, number: 4, title: "FORMATION OF ADVERBS", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.356081Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.359672Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Many adjectives used adverbially are preceded by go, e.g. cúramach 'careful', go cúramach 'carefully'. Go prefixes h to vowels, e.g. go hannamh 'rarely'.</p><p>Where there are two adjectives together, go may be dropped, e.g. (go) réasúnta cúramach 'reasonably carefully'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 77, lesson_id: 15, number: 5, title: "PERSONAL NUMBERS", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.360356Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.362004Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>These nouns, which are all masculine except beirt, e.g. an bheirt 'the two people', are used when counting people. Above ten, duine is used with ordinary numbers.</p><p>With nouns denoting people, e.g. bean, sagart, múinteoir, ordinary numbers can be used: dhá bhean, trí shagart, ceithre mhúinteoir. Alternatively, personal numbers may be used followed by a plural form, e.g. beirt mná, triúr sagairt, ceathrar múinteoirí, or less frequently by the singular in such phrases as e.g. beirt fhear.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 78, lesson_id: 16, number: 1, title: "PREPOSITIONAL PRONOUNS: AG", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.303959Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.306937Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ag like most prepositions combines with mé, tú etc. to give a set of 'prepositional pronouns':</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 79, lesson_id: 16, number: 2, title: "MEANINGS OF AG", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.327699Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.327699Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 80, lesson_id: 16, number: 3, title: "A USE OF SEO/SIN WITH AGAMSA, AGADSA, ETC.", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.347156Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.349441Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an teach sin agamsa go deas.</strong> — That house of mine is nice.</p><p><strong>Tá an doras seo againn go dona.</strong> — That door of ours is bad.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín seo agaibhse tinn.</strong> — This Máirtín of yours is sick.</p><p>Seo/sin are often used with the contrast forms, e.g. 'amsa, 'adsa, to express possession. Where there is a short form, it must be used, e.g. 'ainne, not 'againne. Seo 'ainne /s'æ:N'ə/ and seo agaibhse /s'æ:gi:s'ə/ are pronounced as though spelt seainne, seagaibhse. An emphatic form may also be used with féin, but without the contrast suffix -sa/se: <strong>Tá an teach seo 'ainn féin ansin.</strong> — 'Our own house is there.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 81, lesson_id: 17, number: 1, title: "THE PREPOSITION AR", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.147283Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.147283Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 82, lesson_id: 17, number: 2, title: "AG CUR AR 'CAUSING'", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.198251Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.201049Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá ocras ar Cháit.</strong> — Cáit is hungry.</p><p><strong>Tá sé ag cur ocras ar Cháit.</strong> — It is causing Cáit to be hungry.</p><p>'It is putting hunger on C.'</p><p>Cur 'to put' can be used with most idioms containing the preposition <strong>ar</strong> to mean 'to cause to ...'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 83, lesson_id: 18, number: 1, title: "INDIRECT RELATIVE CLAUSES", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.799038Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.802290Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Unstressed <em>a</em> /ə/, which causes eclipsis and is followed by the dependent form of the verb (see Lesson 2), is used when the noun which the relative clause refers to is not the topic of the clause, e.g. <em>an bord a bhfuil an leabhar air</em> 'the table which the book is on'; where <em>an leabhar</em> 'the book' and not <em>an bord</em> 'the table' is the topic of the relative clause. In practice, this indirect relative is required:</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 84, lesson_id: 18, number: 2, title: "DOUBLE INDIRECT RELATIVE", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.816894Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.818808Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In an indirect relative clause, verbs which are normally followed by <em>go/nach</em>, e.g. <em>silim go bhfuil...</em> 'I think ...', are followed by another indirect relative, e.g. <em>an bord a silim a bhfuil sé air</em> 'the table which I think it is on'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 85, lesson_id: 18, number: 3, title: "SOME IDIOMS COMBINING AG AND AR", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.820214Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.822485Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá meas agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I have respect for Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá buíochas agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I am grateful to Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá aithne agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I am acquainted with Cáit; I know Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá cion agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I am fond of Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá ceist agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I have a question for Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá an ghráin agam ar Cháit.</strong> — I detest Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá eolas agam ar an áit seo.</strong> — I have a knowledge of (am familiar with) this place.</p><p><strong>Tá an-tóir agam ar an áit seo.</strong> — I am very keen on this place.</p><p><strong>Tá seans agam ar phost.</strong> — I have a chance of a job.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 86, lesson_id: 18, number: 4, title: "THE PREPOSITION AS", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.831291Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.833700Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tóig leabhar as bosca.</strong> — Take a book out of a box!</p><p><strong>Tóig an leabhar as an mbosca.</strong> — Take the book out of the box!</p><p>When used with the singular article <em>as</em> causes eclipsis to a following noun, e.g. <em>as an mbosca</em> 'out of the box'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 87, lesson_id: 18, number: 5, title: "QUESTIONS WITH PREPOSITIONS", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.852729Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.854893Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cé air a bhfuil an leabhar?</strong> — On what is the book?</p><p><strong>Cé aige a bhfuil an teach?</strong> — Who has the house?</p><p><em>Cé</em> is used with the third person singular masculine form of the preposition, e.g. <em>air, aige</em>, and is followed by an indirect relative clause.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 88, lesson_id: 20, number: 1, title: "RELATIVE OF COPULA", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.893703Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.893703Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 89, lesson_id: 20, number: 2, title: "FUTURE OF COPULA", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.907063Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.909704Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is fiú airgead mór amach anseo é.</strong> — It will be worth big money sometime in the future.</p><p><strong>Is deacair fanacht anseo amárach.</strong> — It will be hard to stay here tomorrow.</p><p>These forms of the copula (<em>is, ar, nach</em>, etc.) can be used with a future meaning.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 90, lesson_id: 20, number: 3, title: "THE PREPOSITION LE", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.912240Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.912240Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 91, lesson_id: 20, number: 4, title: "IDIOMATIC USES OF COPULA AND LE", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.936168Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.936168Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 92, lesson_id: 20, number: 5, title: "USE OF É IN RESPONSES", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.947519Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.949145Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is dóigh go mbeidh Cáit ag teacht.</strong> — Cáit will probably be coming.</p><p>Responses: <strong>Is dóigh go mbeidh.</strong> — Yes, she probably will.</p><p><strong>Is dóigh é.</strong> — Yes, probably so.</p><p><strong>B'fhéidir go mbeadh Máirtín ann.</strong> — Perhaps Máirtín would be there.</p><p>Responses: <strong>B'fhéidir go mbeadh.</strong> — Yes, perhaps he would.</p><p><strong>B'fhéidir é.</strong> — Yes, perhaps so.</p><p>É can be used in responses to stand for indirect speech.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 93, lesson_id: 20, number: 6, title: "USE OF DIRECTIONAL ADVERBS WITH LE", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.954126Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.956139Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Siar leat!</strong> — Back you go!</p><p><strong>Anuas libh!</strong> — Down (from up there) you (pl.) come!</p><p><strong>Síos an bóthar liom!</strong> — I went on down the road.</p><p>Adverbs expressing direction (see Lesson 19) can be used with the preposition <em>le</em> and the verb is understood. When used as a plural imperative the verbal ending -<em>aigi</em> (see Lesson 12) is sometimes added, e.g. <em>amach(aigi) libh</em> 'out you (pl.) go!'.</p><p class=\"note\">*Ab, gurb, marab before vowels, see Lesson 11.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
