gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 135, lesson_id: 36, number: 1, title: "THE SUBJUNCTIVE", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.125122Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.128115Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The present subjunctive is largely confined to set phrases expressing wishes or curses. <strong>Go</strong> /g/ə, which causes eclipsis (e.g. <strong>go gcuire</strong> ... 'that... may put') is used in the positive and <strong>nár</strong> /N :r/, which causes lenition (e.g. <strong>Nár fheice Dia an t-ádh ort</strong> 'May God not see you lucky'), in the negative. The <strong>r</strong> in <strong>nár</strong> is slender before a following <strong>i</strong> or <strong>e</strong>, e.g. <strong>nár éirí</strong> /N :r' air'i:/.</p><p>Type 1 verbs add <strong>-a!-e</strong> //ə: a to a broad consonant, e.g. <strong>go sábhála</strong> ... 'may ... save', <strong>e</strong> to a slender consonant, e.g. <strong>go gcuire</strong> ... 'may ... put'. Type 2 verbs add <strong>-(a)í</strong> /i:/: <strong>aí</strong> to a broad consonant, e.g. <strong>go mbeannaí</strong> ... 'may ... bless', <strong>í</strong> to a slender consonant, e.g. <strong>Go n-éirí go geal leat</strong> ! 'May you succeed brilliantly !'. The form of irregular verbs used is that of the habitual tenses, e.g. <strong>feiceann sé</strong> 'he sees', <strong>Nár fheice Dia an t-ádh ort</strong>! 'May God not see you lucky !'.</p><p>The present subjunctive of <strong>tá</strong> is <strong>raibh</strong>, e.g. <strong>Go raibh maith agat</strong> 'Thank you' (lit. 'May you have good'), and <strong>nár raibh</strong>. The present subjunctive of the copula is <strong>go mba</strong> /m/ə, which prefixes <strong>h</strong> to <strong>é</strong>, e.g. <strong>Go mba hé dhuit</strong> or <strong>Go mba amhlaidh dhuit</strong> 'The same to you !', and <strong>Nár ba</strong>, e.g. <strong>Nár ba fhearr a bheas tú</strong> ! 'May you not be better'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 136, lesson_id: 36, number: 2, title: "SECONDARY IMPERATIVE FORMS (CORRESPONDING TO BÍODH)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.144818Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.146514Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>All verbal endings are spelt with a 'broad' vowel, e.g. -adh, -aiodh, after a broad consonant, e.g. glan-, sal-, oscl-, and with a 'slender' vowel, e.g. -eadh, -iodh, after a slender consonant, e.g. bris-, roinn-, imr-. Although normally the future is used as a quasi-imperative, e.g. <strong>ólfaidh muid deoch</strong> 'we will have a drink', the above forms can be used with setseisean, si!sise, siad!siadsan, muid!muide; see also Lesson 8.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 137, lesson_id: 36, number: 3, title: "DIABHAL/DHEAMHAN AS EMPHATIC NEGATIVE", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.153373Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.155333Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Diabhal /d'auiJ 'devil' and dheamhan /Y'U:N/ 'demon' can be used to form emphatic negatives.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 138, lesson_id: 36, number: 4, title: "OPTIONAL VERBAL FORMS", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.183421Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.186980Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Optionally, when a verb is 'echoed' in the same utterance (e.g. <strong>Glanfaidh tú an bord, an nglanfais?</strong>) or in a response (e.g. <strong>Ar nigh tú na soithí?</strong> - <strong>Níos</strong>) special 'echo' forms may be used in the first and second person singular of the future and past, and in the second person singular of the future and past, and in the second person singular of the habitual.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
