gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 150, lesson_id: 19, number: 4, title: "NUMBERS ABOVE 10", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.498464Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.498464Z")
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 151, lesson_id: 28, number: 1, title: "Six IRREGULAR VERBS", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.617573Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.619918Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The above table contains six of the ten (excluding the verb tá) irregular verbs. Generally, irregular verbs are lenited or eclipsed just like any verb and take the same endings as Type 1 regular verbs, e.g. <strong>an dteagann tú?</strong> — 'do you come?', <strong>ní thiocfaidh mé</strong> — 'I won't come', <strong>nár tháinig siad?</strong> — 'did they not come?', <strong>ní thiocfainn</strong> — 'I would not come'.</p><p>The verb <strong>abair</strong> 'say' is somewhat exceptional:</p><p>(i) When lenition would normally be expected, the initial d is omitted, e.g. <strong>ní déarfainn</strong> — 'I wouldn't say', <strong>ar dhúirt sé?</strong> — 'did he say?'.</p><p>(ii) Initial d is not lenited after a in a direct relative, e.g. <strong>sin é an fear a déarfas é</strong> — 'that is the man who will say it', or after má in the past tenses, e.g. <strong>má dúirt sé</strong> — 'if he said'.</p><p>(iii) There is an alternative direct relative form (a deir 'says') with a present or past meaning, e.g. <strong>céard a deir tú?</strong> — 'what are you saying?', <strong>tá mé sásta, a deir sé</strong> — 'I am content, says he'.</p><p>Somewhat less frequently ní, nach, go, mara may be used in the past tense (similarly to the verb tá) instead of níor, ar, gur, marar; e.g. <strong>ar tháinig sé?</strong> / <strong>an dtáinig sé?</strong> — 'did he come?'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 152, lesson_id: 28, number: 2, title: "THE PREPOSITION IDIR", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.632618Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.634144Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Idir</strong> causes no change to a following noun, alone or with the article. The article affects the noun as normal, e.g. <strong>an bord</strong> — 'the table', <strong>idir an bord...</strong> — 'between the table ...'; <strong>an bhean</strong> — 'the woman', <strong>idir an bhean...</strong> — 'between the woman ...'; see Lesson 8.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 153, lesson_id: 28, number: 3, title: "GENITIVE WITH SLENDER CONSONANT AND IRREGULAR VOWEL CHANGE", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.644892Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.646300Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The genitive form of some dozen or so masculine nouns is formed by making the final consonant slender and by changing in spelling:</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 154, lesson_id: 28, number: 4, title: "THE VOCATIVE SINGULAR", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.666703Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.668492Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>After the vocative particle <strong>a</strong>, which is not stressed, all nouns and adjectives are lenited (see Lesson 4). In the case of masculine nouns, the noun and adjective change exactly as in the genitive singular (see Lesson 27), with the following exceptions:</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
gr = GrammarRule.create!(id: 155, lesson_id: 28, number: 5, title: "THE DIMINUTIVE SUFFIX", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.677234Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.678472Z")
gr.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The suffix -ín is used to express smallness, e.g. <strong>bád</strong> 'a boat', <strong>báidín</strong> 'a little boat'. It may also be used to express endearment, or in a pejorative sense.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gr.save!
