gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 55, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(i) Masculine nouns", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.380105Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.381952Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an búistéara anseo.</strong> — The butcher is here.</p><p><strong>Tá mac an bhúistéara anseo.</strong> — The butcher's son is here.</p><p><strong>Tá an t-iascaire anseo.</strong> — The fisherman is here.</p><p><strong>Tá mac an iascaire anseo.</strong> — The fisherman's son is here.</p><p>When the article is used with a masculine noun in the genitive, the initial consonant of that noun is lenited, e.g. <em>an búistéara</em> 'the butcher', <em>an bhúistéara</em> 'of the butcher'. Initial t and d resist lenition, e.g. <em>mac an tincéara</em> 'the tinker's son', <em>mac an druncaeir</em> 'the drunkard's son'. Initial vowels are unaffected, e.g. <em>mac an iascaire</em> 'the fisherman's son', as opposed to the nominative <em>an t-iascaire</em> 'the fisherman'; see Lesson 8.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 56, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(b) Masculine nouns beginning with s", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.382572Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.384086Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an siúinéara anseo.</strong> — The carpenter is here.</p><p><strong>Tá mac an tsiúinéara anseo.</strong> — The carpenter's son is here.</p><p>A t is prefixed to a masculine noun beginning with s followed by a vowel, l, n, or r. It takes its quality from the following vowel or consonant, e.g. slender in ... <em>an tsiúinéara</em> /ə t'u:N'e:rə/ '... of the carpenter', broad in ... <em>an tsáibhéara</em> /ə ta:w'e:rə/ 'of the sawyer'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 57, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(ii) Feminine nouns", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.384670Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.386136Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an Ghaeltacht anseo.</strong> — The Gaeltacht is here.</p><p><strong>Tá muintir na Gaeltachta anseo.</strong> — The people of the Gaeltacht are here.</p><p>Before a feminine noun the genitive of the article takes the form <em>na</em>, which is unstressed and causes no change to a consonant. <em>Muintir na Gaeltachta</em> could also be translated as 'the Gaeltacht people'. Similarly: <em>fear an bhainne</em> 'the man of the milk, the man (associated) with the milk, the milkman'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 58, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(b) Feminine nouns beginning with a vowel", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.386697Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.388141Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an oifig anseo.</strong> — The office is here.</p><p><strong>Tá doras na hoifige anseo.</strong> — The door of the office is here.</p><p><em>Na</em> prefixes h to a feminine noun with an initial vowel.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 59, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(iii) Plural nouns", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.388718Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.390231Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá na tithe anseo.</strong> — The houses are here.</p><p><strong>Tá úinéirí na dtithe anseo.</strong> — The owners of the houses are here.</p><p><strong>Tá na hiascairí anseo.</strong> — The fishermen are here.</p><p><strong>Tá bád na n-iascairí anseo.</strong> — The fishermen's boat is here.</p><p>In the genitive plural <em>na</em> causes eclipsis, e.g. <em>na dtithe</em> '... of the houses', <em>na n-iascairí</em> 'of the fishermen'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 60, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(iv) Summary table", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.390779Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.392206Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Before:</th><th>an initial consonant</th><th>an initial vowel</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>masculine nouns</td><td>an + lenition</td><td>an</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>mac an bhúistéara</td><td>mac an iascaire</td></tr><tr><td>an t (before s)</td><td>mac an tsiúinéara</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>feminine nouns</td><td>na</td><td>na h</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>muintir na Gaeltachta</td><td>doras na hoifige</td></tr><tr><td>plural nouns</td><td>na + eclipsis</td><td>na n-</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>úinéirí na dtithe</td><td>bád na n-iascairí</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 61, grammar_rule_id: 22, label: "(v) No special genitive form", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.392720Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.394342Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Except for the effects of the article (lenition, eclipsis, or prefixed h), the form of the noun in the above examples is substantially the same in the genitive. This is so in the case of:</p><p>(a) the large majority of nouns with a final //ə, e.g. <em>siúinéara</em>, <em>oifige</em>;</p><p>(b) two-syllable nouns ending in -acht, e.g. <em>Gaeltacht</em>;</p><p>(c) nouns ending in -ín, e.g. <em>cailín</em>, <em>caipín</em>;</p><p>(d) nouns ending in -(a)i e.g. <em>scéalaí</em>;</p><p>(e) personal numbers <em>triúr</em> to <em>deichniúr</em> (see Lesson 15);</p><p>(f) several other nouns ending in a consonant.</p><p class=\"note\">These are in Official Standard Irish written with a final a //ə, e.g. <em>Raidió na Gaeltachta</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 62, grammar_rule_id: 23, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.402846Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.404353Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá teach an bhúistéara anseo.</strong> — The house of the butcher is here.</p><p>Although this sentence can be translated as 'the house of the butcher', in Irish there is only one article: <em>teach an bhúistéara</em>. <em>Teach an bhúistéara</em> is felt to be a proper noun exactly like <em>Cáit</em>, <em>Bríd</em>, etc. and behaves accordingly:</p><p><em>Cáit sin</em> (see Lesson 9) <em>teach an bhúistéara sin</em> 'that butcher's house'</p><p><em>geata Cháit</em> (see Lesson 3) <em>geata theach an bhúistéara</em> 'the gate of the butcher's house'</p><p class=\"note\">These are in Official Standard Irish written with a final a //ə, e.g. <em>Raidió na Gaeltachta</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 63, grammar_rule_id: 24, label: "(i) Genitive after verbal noun", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.405890Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.407369Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá sé ag moladh Mháirtín.</strong> — He is praising Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá sí ag pósadh mhac an bhúistéara.</strong> — She is marrying the butcher's son.</p><p><strong>Tá sí ag moladh an bhúistéara.</strong> — She is praising the butcher.</p><p><strong>Tá sí ag glanadh na hoifige.</strong> — She is cleaning the office.</p><p>A verbal noun is in genitive relation with a following noun. Proper nouns are lenited, e.g. <em>ag pósadh mhac an bhúistéara</em> 'marrying the butcher's son' (see Lesson 15), and definite nouns are in the genitive, e.g. <em>ag moladh an bhúistéara</em> 'praising the butcher'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 64, grammar_rule_id: 24, label: "(ii) Genitive after certain prepositions", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.407890Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.409360Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo ar shon Mháirtín.</strong> — Cáit is here on behalf of Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá fuinneog os cionn bhord an bhúistéara.</strong> — There is a window above the butcher's table.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín anseo ar shon na n-iascairí.</strong> — Máirtín is here on behalf of the fishermen.</p><p><strong>Tá seomra eile os cionn na hoifige.</strong> — There is another room above the office.</p><p>After certain prepositions (originally containing nouns) nouns are in a genitive relation and therefore proper nouns are lenited, e.g. <em>os cionn bhord an bhúistéara</em> 'above the butcher's table', and definite nouns are in the genitive, e.g. <em>ar shon an tsáibhéara</em> 'on behalf of the sawyer'.</p><p>Possessive pronouns (mo, do, a etc.) are used with these prepositions to express pronominal objects: <em>Tá Cáit anseo ar mo shon</em> 'Cáit is here on my behalf'; <em>Tá seomra eile os do chionn</em> 'There is another room above you'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 65, grammar_rule_id: 25, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.413665Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T15:06:15.415351Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>boats</th><th>years</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>20 fiche bád</td><td>fiche bliain</td></tr><tr><td>21 bád is fiche</td><td>bliain is fiche</td></tr><tr><td>22 dhá bhád fhichead</td><td>dhá bhliain fhichead</td></tr><tr><td>23 trí bhád fhichead</td><td>trí bliana fichead</td></tr><tr><td>30 deich mbád fhichead</td><td>deich mbliana fichead</td></tr><tr><td>31 aon bhád déag is fiche</td><td>aon bhliain déag is fiche</td></tr><tr><td>32 dhá bhád déag is fiche</td><td>dhá bhliain déag is fiche</td></tr><tr><td>33 trí bhád déag is fiche</td><td>trí bliana déag is fiche</td></tr><tr><td>40 dhá fhichead bád</td><td>dhá fhichead bliain</td></tr><tr><td>41 bád is dhá fhichead</td><td>bliain is dhá fhichead</td></tr><tr><td>42 dhá bhád is dhá fhichead</td><td>dhá bhliain is dhá fhichead</td></tr><tr><td>43 trí bhád is dhá fhichead</td><td>trí bliana is dhá fhichead</td></tr><tr><td>51 aon bhád déag is dhá fhichead</td><td>aon bhliain déag is dhá fhichead</td></tr><tr><td>60 trí fichid bád</td><td>trí fichid bliain</td></tr><tr><td>61 bád is trí fichid</td><td>bliain is trí fichid</td></tr><tr><td>62 dhá bhád is trí fichid</td><td>dhá bhliain is trí fichid</td></tr><tr><td>63 trí bhád is trí fichid</td><td>trí bliana is trí fichid</td></tr><tr><td>70 deich mbád is trí fichid</td><td>deich mbliana is trí fichid</td></tr><tr><td>71 aon bhád déag is trí fichid</td><td>aon bhliain déag is trí fichid</td></tr><tr><td>80 ceithre fichid bád</td><td>ceithre fichid bliain</td></tr><tr><td>81 bád is ceithre fichid</td><td>bliain is ceithre fichid</td></tr><tr><td>82 dhá bhád is ceithre fichid</td><td>dhá bhliain is ceithre fichid</td></tr><tr><td>83 trí bhád is ceithre fichid</td><td>trí bliana is ceithre fichid</td></tr><tr><td>90 deich mbád is ceithre fichid</td><td>deich mbliana is ceithre fichid</td></tr><tr><td>91 aon bhád déag is ceithre fichid</td><td>aon bhliain déag is ceithre fichid</td></tr><tr><td>100 céad bád</td><td>céad bliain</td></tr><tr><td>101 céad is bád</td><td>céad is bliain</td></tr><tr><td>102 céad is dhá bhád</td><td>céad is dhá bhliain</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The use of numbers between twenty and a hundred can be seen in the above table. Directly following a noun, <em>fichead</em> is lenited, e.g. <em>trí bhád fhichead</em> '23 boats', <em>ceithre cinn fhichead</em> '24 (ones)', except where a plural, or special form ending in a vowel (see Lesson 11) is used, e.g. <em>trí bliana fichead</em> '23 years'.</p><p><em>Fiche</em> '20' is pronounced /f'i:/, but after <em>is</em>, e.g. <em>bád is fiche</em> '21 boats' it is pronounced with a short vowel: try. <em>Scór</em> may optionally replace <em>fiche</em>, e.g. <em>dhá scór is aon cheann déag</em> '51'.</p><p>An older system based on tens has a certain currency: <em>fiche</em>, <em>tríocha</em>, <em>daichead</em>, <em>caoga</em>, <em>seasca</em>, <em>seachtó</em>, <em>ochtó</em>, <em>nócha</em> '20... 90'. It has been reintroduced through the school system.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 66, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(i) Statement", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.313324Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.315487Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit is here.</p><p><strong>Tá fear agus gasúr anseo.</strong> — There is a man and a child here.</p><p><strong>Tá mé anseo.</strong> — I am here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 67, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(ii) Negation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.316295Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.318165Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit is not here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 68, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(iii) Questions", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.318879Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.320673Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An bhfuil Cáit anseo?</strong> — Is Cáit here?</p><p><strong>Nach bhfuil sí anseo?</strong> — Is she not here?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 69, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(iv) Indirect speech", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.321383Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.323126Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Deir sé go bhfuil Cáit anseo.</strong> — He says that Cáit is here.</p><p><strong>Deir sé nach bhfuil Cáit anseo.</strong> — He says that Cáit is not here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 70, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(v) Summary table", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.323826Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.325605Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Tá</th><th>Níl</th><th>An bhfuil</th><th>Nach bhfuil</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Cáit anseo.</td><td>Cáit anseo.</td><td>mé, mise, etc.</td><td>mé, mise, etc.</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé go</td><td>nach</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 71, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(vi) Usage", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.326310Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.328007Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The verb <strong>tá</strong> originally meant 'stands' but has come to mean 'is'. It is, however, as we shall see, not used to link two nouns or pronouns.</p><p>When a sentence with the verb <strong>tá</strong> does not contain an adjective, e.g. <strong>Tá Cáit sásta</strong> 'Cáit is content', or an adverb (adverbial phrase) of place, e.g. <strong>Tá Cáit anseo</strong> 'Cáit is here', <strong>ann</strong> is inserted: <strong>Tá fear ann</strong> 'There is a man (there)' or 'A man exists'. In the case of a noun describing an event, e.g. <strong>timpiste</strong> 'accident', an adverb of time is sufficient.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 72, grammar_rule_id: 27, label: "(vii) Autonomous form", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.328670Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:24.339941Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Táthar sásta anseo.</strong> — People are content here.</p><p>This form expresses the idea 'one is' or 'people (in general) are'. It is autonomous, that is, it stands on its own, needing no specific subject.</p><table><tr><th>Táthar</th><th>Níltear</th><th>An bhfuiltear</th><th>Nach bhfuiltear</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>sásta anseo.</td><td>sásta anseo.</td><td>sásta anseo.</td><td>sásta anseo.</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé go</td><td>nach</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 73, grammar_rule_id: 30, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.683519Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:28:51.686028Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Original</th><th>Lenited</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>c</td><td>Cháit</td></tr><tr><td>muintir Chiarraí</td><td>muintir Cháit</td></tr><tr><td>p</td><td>Pháidín</td></tr><tr><td>Peige</td><td>Pheige</td></tr><tr><td>g</td><td>Ghaoth Sáile</td></tr><tr><td>Glinsce</td><td>Ghlinsce</td></tr><tr><td>d</td><td>Donncha</td></tr><tr><td>Diarmaid</td><td>Dhiarmaid</td></tr><tr><td>b</td><td>Bhairbre</td></tr><tr><td>Bríd</td><td>Bhríd</td></tr><tr><td>m</td><td>Máire</td></tr><tr><td>Meiriceá</td><td>Mheiriceá</td></tr><tr><td>f</td><td>Fhionnasclainn</td></tr><tr><td>t</td><td>Thomáisín</td></tr><tr><td>Tír an Fhia</td><td>Thír an Fhia</td></tr><tr><td>Sasana</td><td>Shasana</td></tr><tr><td>Séamaisín</td><td>Shéamaisín</td></tr><tr><td>Seánín</td><td>Sheáinín</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Lenition is shown in spelling by writing an h. Study closely the fold-out table at the back of the book and be sure to understand the change in pronunciation.</p><p><strong>Cáit</strong> c /k/ -&gt; ch /x/</p><p><strong>Ciarraí</strong> c /k/ -&gt; ch /x/</p><p><strong>Páidín</strong> p /p/ -&gt; ph /f/ etc.</p><p>ls when followed by any consonant except l, n, r is not affected.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 74, grammar_rule_id: 32, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.693865Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.703329Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>A Cháit!</strong> — Cáit!</p><p><strong>An bhfuil tú ansin, a Bhríd?</strong> — Are you there, Bríd?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 75, grammar_rule_id: 33, label: "(i) General principles", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.711321Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.714104Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>There is no absolute rule for determining gender. There are, however, some general principles:</p><p>(a) Nouns describing males (of humans and, where the distinction is made, of animals), e.g. <strong>Máirtín</strong>, <strong>uncail</strong> 'uncle', <strong>tarbh</strong> 'bull', and occupations originally associated with males, e.g. <strong>sagart</strong> 'priest', are all masculine.</p><p>(b) Nouns describing females, e.g. <strong>Cáit</strong>, <strong>bean</strong> 'woman', <strong>cearc</strong> 'hen', and names of countries and languages, e.g. <strong>Éirinn</strong> 'Ireland', <strong>Gaeilge</strong> 'Irish', are almost all feminine.</p><p>(c) Otherwise, nouns ending in a broad consonant tend to be masculine, e.g. <strong>gasúr</strong> 'child', and those ending in a slender consonant are feminine, e.g. <strong>muintir</strong> 'people'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 76, grammar_rule_id: 33, label: "(ii) Apart from those general principles:", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.715555Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.718285Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Nouns with the following endings are consistently masculine:</p><table><tr><th>Ending</th><th>Example</th><th>Translation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>-án</td><td>cupán</td><td>cup</td></tr><tr><td>-in</td><td>cailín</td><td>girl</td></tr><tr><td>-úr</td><td>gasúr</td><td>child</td></tr><tr><td>-ún</td><td>náisiún</td><td>nation</td></tr><tr><td>-as</td><td>doras</td><td>door</td></tr><tr><td>-ar</td><td>pórtar</td><td>porter</td></tr><tr><td>-(ái)sle</td><td>coláiste</td><td>college</td></tr><tr><td>-óir/-eoir</td><td>múinteoir</td><td>teacher</td></tr><tr><td>-aire</td><td>cabaire</td><td>natterer</td></tr><tr><td>-éara</td><td>feilméara</td><td>farmer</td></tr><tr><td>-ai</td><td>scéalaí</td><td>storyteller</td></tr><tr><td>-adh</td><td>moladh</td><td>praising</td></tr><tr><td>-amh</td><td>gaineamh</td><td>sand</td></tr><tr><td>(with two syllables) -a</td><td>cóta</td><td>coat</td></tr><tr><td>(derived from noun) -ach</td><td>Éireannach</td><td>Irishman</td></tr><tr><td>(with one syllable) -cht</td><td>fuacht</td><td>cold</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">The final n of <strong>aon</strong> is not pronounced in this phrase.</p><p>(b) Nouns with the following endings are consistently feminine:</p><table><tr><th>Ending</th><th>Example</th><th>Translation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>-óig/-eoig</td><td>fuinneog</td><td>window</td></tr><tr><td>-oil</td><td>candáil</td><td>auction</td></tr><tr><td>-ail</td><td>feadaíl</td><td>whistling</td></tr><tr><td>(more than two syllables) -acht</td><td>Gaeltacht</td><td>Gaeltacht</td></tr><tr><td>(mass nouns) -ach</td><td>báisteach</td><td>rain</td></tr><tr><td>-seach</td><td>cláirseach</td><td>harp</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 77, grammar_rule_id: 34, label: "(i) Predicative use", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.727886Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.730808Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl Bríd mór.</strong> — Bríd is not big.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit agus Páidín sásta.</strong> — Cáit and Páidín are content</p><p>About eight adjectives indicating subjective judgment (when not qualified by an adverb) take the unstressed particle <em>go</em>. If the adjective begins with a vowel, <em>h</em> is prefixed:</p><p><strong>Tá sé go maith</strong> — It is good/well</p><p><strong>go breá</strong> — fine</p><p><strong>go deas</strong> — nice</p><p><strong>go dona</strong> — bad</p><p><strong>go haoibhinn</strong> — pleasant</p><p><strong>go hiontach</strong> — wonderful</p><p><strong>go hálainn</strong> — beautiful</p><p><strong>go holc</strong> — wicked</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 78, grammar_rule_id: 34, label: "(ii) Modifying and directly following nouns in the singular.", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.731486Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.733259Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá gasúr mór ansin.</strong> — There is a big child there.</p><p><strong>Tá fuinneog mhór ansin.</strong> — There is a big window here.</p><p><strong>Tá bean mhór bhreá anseo.</strong> — There is a fine big woman there.</p><p>An adjective directly following and modifying a feminine noun is lenited*. <em>Go</em> is not required with adjectives of subjective judgment in this usage.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 79, grammar_rule_id: 35, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.740259Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:26.742187Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Máire leisciúil anseo.</strong> — Lazy Máire is here.</p><p>Lenition of <em>l</em> or <em>n</em> is not shown in spelling but a distinction is made in pronunciation by some speakers of the dialect.</p><p>In the same way, lenited <em>N'</em> becomes <em>n'</em>. Study the table at the back of the book and see also phonetic exercise (c) in Lesson 1.</p><p class=\"note\">In certain expressions where a noun is used attributively (i.e. like an adjective) it is also lenited after a feminine noun, e.g. <strong>scian phóca</strong> 'pocket knife'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 80, grammar_rule_id: 36, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.611493Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.613890Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an pota ansin. Níl sé mór.</strong> — The pot is there. It is not big.</p><p><strong>Tá an chloch ansin. Níl sí mór.</strong> — The stone is there. It is not big.</p><p><strong>Tá an stail ansin. Níl sé mór.</strong> — The stallion is there. It is not big.</p><p><strong>Tá an cailín ansin. Níl sí mór.</strong> — The girl is there. She is not big.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 81, grammar_rule_id: 37, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.619766Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.621944Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an fear ansin.</strong> — The man is there.</p><p><strong>Níl an gasúr anseo.</strong> — The child is not here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 82, grammar_rule_id: 38, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.626572Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:29:56.628207Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Pronounced as:</th></tr><tbody><tr><td><strong>cloch ar an gcloch</strong> — gloch</td></tr><tr><td><strong>pota faoin bpota</strong> — bota</td></tr><tr><td><strong>gasúr leis an ngasúr mór</strong> — ngasúr /rj/</td></tr><tr><td><strong>bád ar an mbád</strong> — mad</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fuinneoig ar an bhfuinneoig mhór</strong> — bhuinneoig</td></tr><tr><td><strong>fear leis an bhfear</strong> — bhear</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 83, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(i) Statement", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.856068Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.859642Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí Cáit anseo inné.</strong> — Cáit was here yesterday.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 84, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(ii) Negation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.860864Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.864018Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ní raibh Cáit anseo inné.</strong> — Cáit was not here yesterday.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 85, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(iii) Questions", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.865032Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.867678Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An raibh Cáit anseo inné?</strong> — Was Cáit here yesterday?</p><p><strong>Nach raibh Cáit anseo inné?</strong> — Was Cáit not here yesterday?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 86, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(iv) Summary table", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.868571Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.870901Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Bhí</td><td>Bhí mise anseo inné.</td></tr><tr><td>Ní</td><td>Ní raibh mise anseo inné.</td></tr><tr><td>An</td><td>An raibh mise anseo inné?</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>Nach raibh mise anseo inné?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 87, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(v) Autonomous form", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.872057Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.874154Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhíodh siad sásta anseo inné.</strong> — People were content here yesterday.</p><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Bhíodh</td><td>Bhíodh siad sásta anseo inné.</td></tr><tr><td>Ní</td><td>Ní raibh siad sásta anseo inné.</td></tr><tr><td>An</td><td>An raibh siad sásta anseo inné?</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>Nach raibh siad sásta anseo inné?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 88, grammar_rule_id: 39, label: "(vi) Meaning of past tense", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.875217Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.877340Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit was here.</p><p>In the verb tá there is no distinction between a preterite 'was' and a pluperfect 'had been'. A perfect meaning can be expressed by using the present, e.g. <strong>Tá Cáit anseo seachtain anois</strong> — 'Cáit is / has been here a week now'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 89, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(i) Statement", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.883400Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.885190Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo amárach.</strong> — Cáit will be here tomorrow.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 90, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(ii) Negation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.885934Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.887709Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ní bheidh Cáit anseo amárach.</strong> — Cáit will not be here tomorrow.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 91, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(iii) Questions", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.888409Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.890078Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An mbeidh Cáit anseo amárach?</strong> — Will Cáit be here tomorrow?</p><p><strong>Nach mbeidh Cáit anseo amárach?</strong> — Will Cáit not be here tomorrow?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 92, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(iv) Summary table", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.890744Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.892417Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Beidh</td><td>Beidh mise anseo amárach.</td></tr><tr><td>Ní bheidh</td><td>Ní bheidh mise anseo amárach.</td></tr><tr><td>An</td><td>An mbeidh mise anseo amárach?</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>Nach mbeidh mise anseo amárach?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 93, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(v) Autonomous forms", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.893072Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.894775Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Beifear sásta anseo amárach.</strong> — People will be satisfied here tomorrow.</p><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Beifear</td><td>Beifear sásta anseo amárach.</td></tr><tr><td>Ní bheifear</td><td>Ní bheifear sásta anseo amárach.</td></tr><tr><td>An</td><td>An mbeidh siad sásta anseo amárach?</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>Nach mbeidh siad sásta anseo amárach?</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 94, grammar_rule_id: 40, label: "(vi) Meaning of future tense", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.895395Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.896979Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo seachtain amárach.</strong> — Cáit will be / have been here a week tomorrow.</p><p>In Irish no distinction is made between a future, e.g. 'will be' and a future perfect, e.g. 'will have been'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 95, grammar_rule_id: 41, label: "(i) Lenition", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.901446Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.903050Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ní always causes lenition: <strong>ní bheidh</strong> = ní + beidh</p><p><strong>Níl</strong> (a contracted form of <strong>ní fhuil</strong>) = ní + bhfuil</p><p><strong>Raibh</strong> does not change as r is not affected by lenition.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 96, grammar_rule_id: 41, label: "(ii) Eclipsis", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.903641Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:30:39.907002Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An</strong>, <strong>nach</strong>; <strong>go</strong>, <strong>nach</strong> always cause eclipsis:</p><p><strong>An mbeidh ... ?</strong> — an + mbeidh</p><p><strong>An bhfuil... ?</strong> — an + bhfuil</p><p><strong>Nach mbeidh... ?</strong> = nach + mbeidh</p><p><strong>Nach bhfuil... ?</strong> = nach + bhfuil</p><p><strong>...go mbeidh</strong> = go + mbeidh</p><p><strong>... go bhfuil</strong> = go + bhfuil</p><p><strong>Raibh</strong> does not change as r is not affected by eclipsis.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 97, grammar_rule_id: 46, label: "(i) Present habitual", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.363173Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.368203Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bíonn Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit is normally here / is wont to be here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 98, grammar_rule_id: 46, label: "(i) Past habitual", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.369498Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.372828Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhíodh Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit used to be here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 99, grammar_rule_id: 47, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.381370Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.383601Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bheadh Cáit anseo.</strong> — Cáit would be / would have been here.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 100, grammar_rule_id: 48, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.387247Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.390501Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>is</th><th>was</th><th>will be</th><th>normally is</th><th>used to be</th><th>would be</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>tá mé</td><td>bhí mé</td><td>beidh mé</td><td>bím</td><td>bhínn</td><td>bheinn</td></tr><tr><td>tá tú</td><td>bhí tú</td><td>beidh tú</td><td>bíonn tú</td><td>bhíteá</td><td>bheifeá</td></tr><tr><td>tá sé</td><td>bhí sé</td><td>beidh sé</td><td>bíonn sé</td><td>bhíodh sé</td><td>bheadh sé</td></tr><tr><td>tá sí</td><td>bhí sí</td><td>beidh sí</td><td>bíonn sí</td><td>bhíodh sí</td><td>bheadh sí</td></tr><tr><td>tá muid</td><td>bhí muid</td><td>beidh muid</td><td>bíonn muid</td><td>bhíodh muid</td><td>bheadh muid</td></tr><tr><td>tá sibh</td><td>bhí sibh</td><td>beidh sibh</td><td>bíonn sibh</td><td>bhíodh sibh</td><td>bheadh sibh</td></tr><tr><td>tá siad</td><td>bhí siad</td><td>beidh siad</td><td>bíonn siad</td><td>bhídís</td><td>bheidís</td></tr><tr><td>táthar</td><td>bhíodh</td><td>beifear</td><td>bítear</td><td>bhítí</td><td>bheifí</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The statement form in the past, habitual past and conditional is always lenited. A final ch-sound in verbal endings, except in autonomous forms, is replaced by a t /t / sound before a slender s, e.g. sé, sise, sibh, Seán.</p><p><strong>Bhíodh muid anseo.</strong> /w'i:x/ — We used to be here.</p><p><strong>Bheadh sé go deas.</strong> /w'et s'e:/ — It would be nice.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 101, grammar_rule_id: 49, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.394230Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.397141Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>bíonn + mé/mise</strong> combine as <strong>bím(se)</strong> /b'i:m'/ — I (normally) am</p><p><strong>bhíodh + mé/mise</strong> <strong>bhínn(se)</strong> /W'Í:N'/ — I used to be</p><p><strong>bheadh + mé/mise</strong> <strong>bheinn(se)</strong> /w'eN'/ — I would be</p><p><strong>bhíodh + tú/tusa</strong> <strong>bhíteá(sa)</strong> /w'i:t' :/ — you used to be</p><p><strong>bheadh + tú/tusa</strong> <strong>bheifeá(sa)</strong> /w'ea:/ — you would be</p><p><strong>bhíodh + siad/siadsan</strong> <strong>bhídís(ean)</strong> /w'i:d'i:s'/ — they used to be</p><p><strong>bheadh + siad/siadsan</strong> <strong>bheidís(ean)</strong> /w'ed'i:s'/ — they would be</p><p>All verbs have combined forms in the above persons and tenses. Contrast forms always add -se /s'ə/ (1.Sg.), -sa /sə/ (2. Sg.) and -sean /s'Nə / (3. Pl.).</p><p>The combined form is used in a response:</p><p><strong>An mbeifeá ansin ?</strong> — <strong>Bheinn.</strong> Would you be there ? — Yes.</p><p><strong>Bhídísean sásta.</strong> — <strong>Bhídís.</strong> They used to be content. — Yes, they used to.</p><p>The contrast forms, e.g. bhídísean, are never used in these responses.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 102, grammar_rule_id: 50, label: "(i) After most nouns", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.405932Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.407783Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>fuinneogaí móra</strong> — big windows</p><p><strong>daoine glice</strong> — cunning people</p><p>An unstressed vowel /ə/ is added. This is spelt a after a broad consonant, e.g. mór, and e after a slender consonant, e.g. glic.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 103, grammar_rule_id: 50, label: "(ii) After nouns with plurals spelt with a final consonant", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.408673Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.410320Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>gasúir bheaga</strong> — little children</p><p><strong>francaigh mhóra</strong> — big rats</p><p>The adjective is also lenited.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 104, grammar_rule_id: 51, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.413868Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:18.415931Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>A riffneachaí!</strong> — (You) ruffians!</p><p><strong>A chearca!</strong> — Hens!</p><p><strong>A dhaoine uaisle!</strong> — Ladies and gentlemen! (lit. noble people)</p><p><strong>A ghasúir bheaga!</strong> — (You) little children!</p><p><strong>A riffneachaí bradacha!</strong> — You thieving ruffians!</p><p>The vocative particle a /ə/ always causes lenition (see Lesson 4). Adjectives follow the normal rules for the plural.</p><p>There are a few nouns which have a special vocative plural form, e.g. créatúr 'poor thing', créatúir 'poor things', <strong>a chréatúireachaí!</strong> '(you) poor things!'; fear 'man', fir 'men', <strong>a fheara!</strong> '(you) men!'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 105, grammar_rule_id: 52, label: "(i) Positive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.465859Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.469321Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bí anseo amárach!</strong> — Be here tomorrow!</p><p><strong>Bígí anseo amárach!</strong> — Be (pl.) here tomorrow!</p><p><strong>Bíodh Cáit anseo amárach!</strong> — Have Cáit here tomorrow! Let Cáit be here tomorrow!</p><p><strong>Bídís anseo amárach!</strong> — Have them here tomorrow!</p><p>Bíodh can also be used with sé, sí and muid; concerning pronunciation before slender s, see Lesson 7.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 106, grammar_rule_id: 52, label: "(ii) Negative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.470241Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.472484Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ná bí anseo amárach!</strong> — Don't be here tomorrow!</p><p>Ná is used to negate all forms of the imperative.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 107, grammar_rule_id: 53, label: "(i) Positive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.478907Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.481067Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Má bhíonn Máirtín ann, bíonn Cáit sásta.</strong> — If Máirtín is there, Cáit is (normally) pleased.</p><p>Má 'if' lenites all verb forms (except tá, e.g. má tá...)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 108, grammar_rule_id: 53, label: "(ii) Negative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.481881Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.483730Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Mura bhfuil tusa sásta, ní bheidh mise sásta.</strong> — If you are not satisfied, I will not be satisfied.</p><p>Mura 'if... not, unless' causes eclipsis and is followed by the dependent form.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 109, grammar_rule_id: 53, label: "(iii) Future", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.484540Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.488681Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Má bhíonn Máirtín ann, beidh Cáit sásta.</strong> — If Máirtín will be there, Cáit will be pleased.</p><p><strong>Mura mbeidh Máirtín ann, ní bheidh Cáit sásta.</strong> — If Máirtín will not be there, Cáit will not be pleased.</p><p>After má, the future is expressed by using the habitual present, e.g. bíonn.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 110, grammar_rule_id: 53, label: "(iv) In responses", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.490395Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.492468Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ach tá mé sásta!</strong> — But I am satisfied!</p><p><strong>Bhuel, má tá!</strong> — Well, if that is so (that is alright).</p><p>Má/mura can be used in a response with the usual deletion of the subject pronoun (see Lesson 6) to express 'if that is (not) so, then that is alright'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 111, grammar_rule_id: 54, label: "(i) Before a feminine noun", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.497512Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.499762Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an chloch anseo.</strong> — The stone is here.</p><p><strong>Tá an fhuinneog mhór ansin.</strong> — The big window is there.</p><p>The article lenites a following feminine noun, except t, d which tend to resist lenition after n e.g. an tine 'the fire', an deoch 'the drink', and s.</p><p><strong>Bhí an tseachtain go maith.</strong> — The week was good.</p><p><strong>Tá an tsráid bheag ansin.</strong> — The small street is there.</p><p>The article prefixes a t to a feminine noun beginning with s followed by a vowel or l, n, r. The s is then not pronounced. Any prefixed t takes its quality from the following vowel or consonant, e.g. slender in an tseachtain, broad in an tsráid. The t is prefixed even when the article follows a preposition:</p><p><strong>ar an tsráid</strong> — on the street</p><p><strong>faoin tsúil</strong> — under the eye</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 112, grammar_rule_id: 54, label: "(ii) Before a masculine noun beginning with a vowel", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.500439Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.502104Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an t-úll anseo.</strong> — The apple is here.</p><p><strong>Tá an t-éan ansin.</strong> — The bird is there.</p><p>The article prefixes a t to a masculine noun beginning with a vowel.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 113, grammar_rule_id: 54, label: "(iii) Summary table", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.502867Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.504437Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Before</th><th>with initial consonant</th><th>with initial vowel</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>masc. noun</td><td>an</td><td>an t-</td></tr><tr><td>fem. noun</td><td>an + lenition</td><td>an</td></tr><tr><td>fem. noun</td><td>an t before sl, sn, sr, s + vowel</td><td>an tseachtain</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 114, grammar_rule_id: 55, label: "(i) Nouns used in a general sense", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.512596Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.514497Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an óige go maith.</strong> — Youth is good.</p><p><strong>Ní bhíonn an tÉireannach leisciúil.</strong> — An Irishman is not (normally) lazy.</p><p>The article is used when speaking in a general way.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 115, grammar_rule_id: 55, label: "(ii) Expressing a relationship between an entity and its parts", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.516969Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:31:56.518703Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an teach go deas ach tá an doras go dona.</strong> — The house is nice, but its door is bad.</p><p><strong>Tá mé go maith ach tá an chois tinn.</strong> — I am well but my foot is sore.</p><p>When there is no doubt about what or who the possessor is, the article can be used. This is usually the way of expressing possession where an inanimate noun is referred to, e.g. teach.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 116, grammar_rule_id: 56, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.787270Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.790474Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá na heochracha anseo ar na boird.</strong> — The keys are here on the tables.</p><p><strong>Tá na héanacha beaga ansin ar na clocha.</strong> — The little birds are there on the stones.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 117, grammar_rule_id: 57, label: "(i) Usage", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.794273Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.796884Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá tú ansin. Feicim anois thú.</strong> — You are there. I see you now.</p><p><strong>Tá sé ansin. Feicim anois é.</strong> — He is there. I see him now.</p><p><strong>Tá sí ansin. Feicim anois í.</strong> — She is there. I see her now.</p><p><strong>Tá siad ansin. Feicim anois iad.</strong> — They are there. I see them now.</p><p>When used disjunctively, that is, not as a subject directly following on a verb form, <em>thú, é, í, iad</em> or <em>thusa, eisean, ise, iadsan</em> are used. <em>Mé/mise, muid/muide, sibh/sibhse</em> remain the same.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 118, grammar_rule_id: 57, label: "(ii) Word order", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.797684Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.800298Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cloisim anois thú.</strong> — I hear you now.</p><p><strong>Tigim thusa anois ach ní thigim iad sin.</strong> — I understand you now, but I don't understand those fellows.</p><p>When the ordinary form of a pronoun, e.g. <em>thú</em>, is used an adverb or adverbial phrase, e.g. <em>anois</em>, precedes it.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 119, grammar_rule_id: 58, label: "(i) For emphasis (mé féin)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.805989Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.808141Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé féin sásta anseo.</strong> — I myself am content here.</p><p><strong>Feicim féin an sagart anois.</strong> — I myself see the priest now.</p><p><em>Féin</em> /he:n'/ is added to the pronoun, e.g. <em>mé féin, tú féin</em>, etc., or simply to the verb, where the pronoun is combined with it, e.g. <em>feicim</em> 'I see', <em>feicim féin</em> 'I myself see'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 120, grammar_rule_id: 58, label: "(ii) For emphasis after a contrast form or after a noun (mise mé féin)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.808885Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.810897Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An bhfuil tusa thú féin sásta ansin?</strong> — Are you yourself content here?</p><p><strong>Tá an múinteoir é féin anseo anois.</strong> — The teacher himself is here now.</p><p>After a contrast form or after a noun, <em>mé féin, thú féin, é féin</em>, etc. are added.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 121, grammar_rule_id: 58, label: "(iii) Reflexive use", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.811572Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.813244Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá scáthán ansin agus feicim mé féin.</strong> — There is a mirror there and I see myself.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 122, grammar_rule_id: 59, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.821165Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.822880Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé féin agus Ruairí sásta.</strong> — Ruairí and I are content.</p><p><strong>Tá tú féin agus é féin anseo.</strong> — You and he are here.</p><p><strong>Tá mise agus thusa agus ise anseo.</strong> — She and you and I are here.</p><p><strong>Ní fheicim thusa ná iadsan.</strong> — I see neither you nor them.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 123, grammar_rule_id: 60, label: "(i) Use with nouns", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.826277Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.827952Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an fear seo sásta.</strong> — This man is satisfied.</p><p><strong>Tá an chloch mhór sin go deas.</strong> — That big stone is nice.</p><p><strong>Níl na fir úd sásta.</strong> — 'Those' men are not satisfied.</p><p>The definite article is used, e.g. <em>an fear seo</em> 'this man', before any common noun followed by the invariable adjectives <em>seo, sin,</em> or <em>úd</em>. Names do not normally have the article, e.g. <em>Máirtín sin</em> 'that Máirtín'. These adjectives correspond to the adverbs <em>anseo, ansin, ansiúd</em>. The form <em>úd</em> (rather than <em>siúd</em>) is most often used with a noun.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 124, grammar_rule_id: 60, label: "(ii) With third person", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.828564Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:32:33.830200Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá sé seo go maith.</strong> — This (fellow/thing) is good.</p><p><strong>Bhí sí sin go deas.</strong> — That (woman/thing (fem.)) was nice.</p><p><strong>Ní héidís siúd sásta.</strong> — 'They' would not be satisfied.</p><p><em>Seo, sin, siúd</em> can be used with the ordinary third person pronouns to refer to people or things. Contrast forms, e.g. <em>seisean</em>, are not used. The form <em>siúd</em> (rather than <em>úd</em>) is used with a pronoun or combined form.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 125, grammar_rule_id: 61, label: "(i) Positive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.752657Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.754032Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Dhá mbeadh Máirtín anseo, bheadh Cáit sásta</strong> — If Máirtín were here, Cáit would be content.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 126, grammar_rule_id: 61, label: "(ii) Negative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.754582Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.755919Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Mara mbeadh Máirtín anseo, ní bheadh Cáit sásta</strong> — If Máirtín were not here, Cáit would not be content.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 127, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.757991Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.759470Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Before a consonant</th><th>Before a vowel</th><th>Contrast</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>mo dhoras /mə/ my door</td><td>m'uncail /mə/ my uncle</td><td>mo chótasa my coat</td></tr><tr><td>do dhoras /də/ your door</td><td>d'uncail /də/ your uncle</td><td>do chótasa your coat</td></tr><tr><td>a dhoras /ə/ his door</td><td>a uncail /ə/ his uncle</td><td>a chóta seisean his coat</td></tr><tr><td>a doras /ə/ her door</td><td>a huncail /hə/ her uncle</td><td>a cóta sise her coat</td></tr><tr><td>ar ndoras /ə/ our door</td><td>ar n-uncail /ə/ our uncle</td><td>ar gcóta muide our coat</td></tr><tr><td>bhur ndoras /ə/ your door</td><td>bhur n-uncail /ə/ your uncle</td><td>bhur gcóta sibhse your coat</td></tr><tr><td>a ndoras /ə/ their door</td><td>a n-uncail /ə/ their uncle</td><td>a gcóta siadsan their coat</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Note that -sa is suffixed, e.g. mo chótasa 'my coat'; seisean, sise etc. are written separately, e.g. a bhordseisean 'his table'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 128, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(ii) Pronunciation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.760020Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.761319Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The possessive adjectives are invariable. They are all pronounced with a neutral vowel /ə/. Mo 'my' and do 'your' are written m' and d' showing the pronunciation before a vowel; m' and d' are pronounced broad or slender according to the following vowel. The adjectives ar 'our' and bhur 'your (pl.)', which in Official Standard Irish are spelt ár and bhur, are in pronunciation reduced to /ə/.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 129, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(iii) Lenition / Eclipsis", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.761854Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.763153Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In all cases mo, do, a 'his/its' lenite; ar, bhur, a 'their' cause eclipsis.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 130, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(iv) Use with seo, sin, siúd", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.763712Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.765076Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí sé ag a gheata sin</strong> — He was at that fellow's gate.</p><p>Seo 'this', sin 'that', siúd 'that' can be used with a 'his', 'her', 'their' to mean 'this/that person's', 'these/those people's'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 131, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(v) Use with féin", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.765606Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.766901Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mo bhord féin anseo</strong> — My own table is here.</p><p>'Own' is expressed by féin.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 132, grammar_rule_id: 62, label: "(vi) Use of cuid", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.767400Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.769116Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) General use</p><p><strong>Tá mo chuid leabhar anseo</strong> — My books are here.</p><p><strong>Tá do chuid bainne ansin</strong> — Your milk is there.</p><p><strong>Tá a chuid Gaeilge go maith</strong> — His Irish is good.</p><p>Cuid literally means 'a share, a portion'. It is normally used before a noun in the plural, e.g. mo chuid leabhar 'my books'; a collective or mass noun, e.g. mo chuid bainne 'my milk'; or before an abstract noun, e.g. mo chuid Gaeilge 'my Irish'. It is, however, generally omitted where the noun in question 'inalienably' belongs to the possessor, e.g. mo chosa 'my legs'.</p><p>(b) Use in contrast and with féin</p><p><strong>Tá mo chuid leabharthasa anseo</strong> — My books are here.</p><p><strong>Tá do chuid Gaeilge féin go maith</strong> — Your own Irish is good.</p><p>The contrast endings -sa, seisean, etc. and féin 'own', are added to the noun, not to the word cuid.</p><p>(c) Before a noun in genitive relation</p><p><strong>Tá cuid leabhartha Cháit anseo</strong> — Cáit's books are here.</p><p>As well as after mo, do etc., cuid can optionally be used in similar circumstances, e.g. cuid leabhartha Cháit 'Cáit's books', cuid Gaeilge Cháit 'Cáit's Irish', before a noun in genitive relation.</p><p class=\"note\">This may also mean 'its'. For use of article with inanimate nouns, see also Lesson 8.</p><p class=\"note\">This is an exception to the rule given in Lesson 9 that seisean, sise, siadsan are only used directly after verbs.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 133, grammar_rule_id: 63, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.774725Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:33:11.776019Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mo bhordsa anseo agus tá do cheannsa ansin</strong> — My table is here and yours is there.</p><p><strong>Tá do chuid leabharthasa anseo agus tá mo chuidsa ansin</strong> — Your books are here and mine are there.</p><p><strong>Tá a chuid Gaeilge seisean go maith ach tá do chuidsa go maith freisin</strong> — His Irish is good, but yours is also good.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 134, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(i) Usage", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.402898Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.405717Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé anseo agus tá mé go maith.</strong> — I am here and I am well.</p><p><strong>Is mé Máirtín.</strong> — I am Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Is thusa an dochtúir.</strong> — You are the doctor.</p><p><strong>Is í sin mo bhean.</strong> — That woman is my wife.</p><p><strong>Is é féin an múinteoir.</strong> — He himself is the teacher.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 135, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(ii) Definite noun as subject", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.406562Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.409332Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is é an fear sin an sagart.</strong> — That man is the priest.</p><p><strong>Is í Cáit mo bhean.</strong> — Cáit is my wife.</p><p><strong>Is iad Bríd agus Máirtín na gasúir.</strong> — Bríd and Máirtín are the children.</p><p>In any copula sentence, where the topic (i.e. the first noun, in these examples) is a definite noun, e.g. an fear sin 'that man', Cáit, or Bríd agus Máirtín, it is preceded by the appropriate pronoun é, í, iad.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 136, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(iii) é, í, iad as subject", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.410032Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.412275Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is é an dochtúir é.</strong> — He is the doctor.</p><p><strong>Is í an múinteoir í.</strong> — She is the teacher.</p><p><strong>Is iad na gasúir iad.</strong> — They are the children.</p><p>When the topic is é/í/iad, it must be repeated, e.g. is é an dochtúir é.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 137, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(iv) Table of forms of the copula", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.413038Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.415320Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Is</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Ní</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Ar</td><td>thú/thusa</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé gur</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Mura</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr><tr><td>Más</td><td>mé/mise</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">1 Tú/tusa may also be used, though for other pronouns only the disjunctive forms are allowed.</p><p class=\"note\">2 An /ə/, although the Official Standard Irish form, is less common.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 138, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(v) Table of forms of copula before third person pronouns", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.416110Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.418128Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Ní</td><td>hé/heisean, hí/hise, hiad/hiadsan</td></tr><tr><td>Is</td><td>é/eisean</td></tr><tr><td>Ab</td><td>í/ise</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>é/eisean</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé gurb</td><td>iad/iadsan</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>iad/iadsan</td></tr><tr><td>Mura</td><td>iad/iadsan</td></tr><tr><td>Más</td><td>iad/iadsan</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Más is pronounced with a final slender s /s'/ before third person pronouns.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 139, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(vi) Responses", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.418862Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.420987Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ar thusa an dochtúir?</strong> — <strong>Is mé.</strong> — Are you the doctor? — Yes.</p><p><strong>Ab í Cáit an múinteoir?</strong> — <strong>Ní hí.</strong> — Is Cáit the teacher? — No.</p><p>The pronoun, which takes the stress, is repeated with the copula. Note that if the ordinary form of a disjunctive pronoun is used in responses, contrast forms are not used, e.g. Ar thusa ...? Is mé. (not Is mise)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 140, grammar_rule_id: 64, label: "(vii) Omission of is", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.421751Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.423655Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>(Is) mise an dochtúir.</strong> — I am the doctor.</p><p><strong>(Is) í sin an múinteoir.</strong> — That woman is the teacher.</p><p>Is may be understood before mise, mé féin, mise mé féin; thusa, thú féin, thusa thú féin, etc. or é (í, iad) seo (sin, siúd), but not before the ordinary forms mé, thú, é, í, muid, sibh, iad.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 141, grammar_rule_id: 65, label: "(i) Certain adjectives", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.436867Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.438460Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is ionann iad</strong> — They are the same.</p><p>With a certain few adjectives the copula is normally used, e.g. Is ionann iad; is mar a chéile iad 'they are the same'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 142, grammar_rule_id: 65, label: "(ii) Exclamatory use", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.439086Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.440886Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Nach maith é!</strong> — Isn't he good?</p><p><strong>Is deas é!</strong> — It is nice!</p><p><strong>Is deas í do léine!</strong> — Your shirt is nice!</p><p>The copula is optionally used with some very few adjectives describing a permanent quality and expressing emotional estimation in an exclamation.</p><p>In responses the adjective is repeated, e.g. Is ionann iad. - Is ionann. 'They are the same.' 'Yes, they are.'</p><p class=\"note\">1 Ionann /a: NNƏ / is pronounced as though spelt anann.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 143, grammar_rule_id: 66, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.445936Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:34:00.447840Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Irish</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>bád amháin</td><td>/bɑːd əˈwɑːnʲ/</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>dhá bhád</td><td>/ɣɑː vɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>trí bhád</td><td>/tʲɾʲiː vɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>ceithre bhád</td><td>/ˈcɛhɾʲə vɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>cúig bhád</td><td>/kuːɡʲ vɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>sé bhád</td><td>/ʃeː vɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>seacht mbád</td><td>/ʃaxt mɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>ocht mbád</td><td>/oxt mɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>naoi mbád</td><td>/niː mɑːd/</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>deich mbád</td><td>/dʲɛx mɑːd/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The forms clocha, slata are used only in the sense of 'stoneweight' or 'yards': trí chloch, trí shlat mean 'three stones', 'three rods'.</p><p>Chúig lenites a few plural forms, e.g. chúig bhliana 'five years', chúig phingine 'five pence'.</p><p>Aon 'one' optionally prefixes t to s, e.g. aon tsagart amháin 'one priest only'.</p><p>Probably due to the use of parts of the body as units of measurement, e.g. ceann, this usage of a plural form spreads so that the plural of cois 'leg' is used, e.g. trí cosa 'three legs', and of teanga 'tongue, language', in the set expression na seacht dteangacha 'seven (= several) languages'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 144, grammar_rule_id: 67, label: "(i) Positive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.335789Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.338618Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Feicim an fear atá sásta.</strong> — I see the man who is satisfied.</p><p><strong>a bhionns sásta.</strong> — (normally) is satisfied,</p><p><strong>a bheas sásta.</strong> — will be satisfied,</p><p><strong>a bhí sásta.</strong> — was satisfied,</p><p><strong>a bhíodh sásta.</strong> — used to be satisfied,</p><p><strong>a bheadh sásta.</strong> — would be satisfied.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 145, grammar_rule_id: 67, label: "(ii) Negative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.339689Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.344278Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Feicim an fear nach bhfuil sásta.</strong> — I see the man who is not content.</p><p><strong>Feicim an fear nach mbíonn sásta.</strong> — I see the man who is not (normally) content.</p><p>'Who(m), which, that ... not' is expressed by the negative direct relative particle <em>nach</em> which causes eclipsis and is followed by a dependent form of the verb, e.g. <em>nach bhfuil</em>, <em>nach raibh</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 146, grammar_rule_id: 67, label: "(iii) Double direct relative", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.345226Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.348570Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Feicim an fear a shílim a bheadh sásta.</strong> — I see the man that I think would be content.</p><p><em>Nór'</em> often in fast speech.</p><p>In a direct relative clause verbs which are normally followed by <em>go/nach</em>, e.g. <em>Sílim go mbeadh an fear sásta</em> 'I think that the man would be content', are followed by another relative, e.g. <em>an fear a shílim a bheadh...</em> 'the man who I think would be ...'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 147, grammar_rule_id: 67, label: "(iv) Usage", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.349418Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.352183Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cén bhean a bhí anseo inné?</strong> — What woman (is it who) was here yesterday?</p><p><strong>Bím sásta chomh luath is a fheicim an dochtúir.</strong> — I am normally satisfied as soon as I see the doctor.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit go díreach mar a bhí sí inné.</strong> — Cáit is exactly as she was yesterday.</p><p><strong>Beidh sise anseo nuair a bheas tusa ansin.</strong> — She will be here when you will be there.</p><p>A direct relative is used after questions with <em>cé/céard</em> which contain the copula (<em>cén</em> is a contraction of <em>cé</em> 'who/what is' and <em>an</em> 'the'), after conjunctions containing <em>is</em>, e.g. <em>chomh luath is</em> 'as soon as', <em>mar</em> 'like', and after certain other conjunctions, e.g. <em>nuair</em> 'when'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 148, grammar_rule_id: 68, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.366569Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.368658Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cé atá ansin?</strong> — <strong>Mise.</strong> — Who is there? — Me.</p><p><strong>Cé thusa?</strong> — Who are you?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 149, grammar_rule_id: 69, label: "(i) Adjectives given primary stress", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.375510Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.379253Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá drochbhád agus seanchapall ag Máirtín.</strong> — Máirtín has a bad boat and an old horse.</p><p><strong>Tá an bád ró-shean agus tá an capall ró-óg.</strong> — The boat is too old and the horse is too young.</p><p><strong>Tá droch-sheanbhád eile ansin.</strong> — There is another bad old boat there.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 150, grammar_rule_id: 69, label: "(ii) Double stress", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.379936Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.382501Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit an-mhór.</strong> — Cáit is very big.</p><p><strong>Tá an-charr ag Cáit.</strong> — Cáit has an excellent car.</p><p><strong>Tá an carr sin fíormhaith.</strong> — That car is really good.</p><p><strong>Níl aon charr ag Máirtín.</strong> — Máirtín has no car.</p><p>Aon 'any' and the following noun get equal stress.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 151, grammar_rule_id: 69, label: "(iii) Spelling", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.383127Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.384990Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Generally, the prefix is written together with the following word, e.g. <em>drochbhád</em>, <em>ró-shean</em>. However, two vowels, two identical consonants or two prefixes are separated by a hyphen, e.g. <em>ró-óg</em>, <em>droch-chapall</em>, <em>droch-sheanbhád</em>. The prefixes <em>an-</em> and <em>dea-</em> are written with a hyphen, and <em>aon</em> as a separate word.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 152, grammar_rule_id: 69, label: "(iv) Adjectives ending in n", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.385625Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.388806Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Nach bhfuil aon teach ag an seanfhear sin?</strong> — Has that old man no house?</p><p><strong>Tá seanteach ag an bhfear sin ach níl aon solas ann.</strong> — That man has an old house but there is no light there.</p><p>All adjectives ending in <em>n</em> which come before the noun behave like <em>an</em> 'the' (see Lessons 5 and 8) in that:</p><p>(a) the <em>n</em> takes its quality from the vowel which follows it in spelling, e.g. <em>n</em> in <em>seanfhear</em> and <em>aon fhear</em> is pronounced /N'/.</p><p>(b) <em>d, t</em> resist lenition, e.g. <em>seanteach</em>, <em>seandoras</em>, <em>aon teach</em>.</p><p>An <em>s</em> also resists lenition, e.g. <em>aon solas</em>, although a <em>t</em> is frequently inserted, e.g. <em>aon tsolas</em> /'e:N'toləs/.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 153, grammar_rule_id: 70, label: "(i) Modes of conveyance, machines, containers", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.398073Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.399621Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá carr ag Máirtín agus tá sí an-mhór.</strong> — Máirtín has a car and she/it is very big.</p><p>Other nouns are <em>bád</em>, <em>bus</em>, <em>leoraí</em>, <em>eitleán</em>, <em>meaisín</em>, <em>rásúr</em>, <em>soitheach</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 154, grammar_rule_id: 70, label: "(ii) Certain animals", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.400198Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.403109Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá capall ansin agus tá sí fíormhór.</strong> — There is a horse there and it is really big.</p><p>Another example is <em>francach</em> 'rat'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 155, grammar_rule_id: 70, label: "(iii) Certain garments", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.403714Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.405207Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá caipín Máirtín anseo ach tá sí róbheag.</strong> — Máirtín's cap is here but it is too small.</p><p>Another example is <em>geansaí</em> 'jersey'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 156, grammar_rule_id: 70, label: "(iv) Leabhar 'book'", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.406197Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:35:39.407602Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl an leabhar sin ag Cáit. An bhfuil sí ag Máirtín?</strong> — Cáit has not got that book. Has Máirtín got it?</p><p><em>Leabhar</em> is also exceptional in that, although it is masculine, a following adjective may be optionally lenited, e.g. <em>leabhar mhór</em> or <em>leabhar mór</em> 'a big book'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 157, grammar_rule_id: 71, label: "(i) Positive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.588834Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.591110Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl an crann in ann fás.</strong> — The tree cannot grow.</p><p><strong>Tá mé sásta siúl.</strong> — I am content to walk.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit sásta pósadh.</strong> — Cáit is willing to marry.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 158, grammar_rule_id: 71, label: "(ii) Negative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.592825Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.595202Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé sásta gan siúl.</strong> — I am content not to walk.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit sásta gan é a phósadh.</strong> — Cáit is willing not to marry him.</p><p>The word gan 'without', which is unstressed, is used before the verbal noun to form a negative.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 159, grammar_rule_id: 71, label: "(iii) Direct object", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.597913Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.600194Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé sásta míle a shiúl.</strong> — I am satisfied to walk a mile.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit sásta gan é a phósadh.</strong> — Cáit is willing not to marry him.</p><p>A direct object, e.g. míle, é, precedes the verbal noun. There is a general rule in Irish that when the direct object comes before the verbal noun a /ə/ (+lenition) is used, e.g. a shiúl, a phósadh.</p><p class=\"note\">The verbal noun of every new verb is given in the vocabularies of each lesson. The formation of the verbal noun, however, is more fully dealt with in Appendix II.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 160, grammar_rule_id: 71, label: "(iv) Usage as ordinary noun", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.601080Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.604315Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an siúl go deas.</strong> — Walking is nice.</p><p><strong>Níl an fás go maith anseo.</strong> — The growth is not good here.</p><p><strong>Bíonn pósaithe go leor anseo.</strong> — There are plenty of marriages here.</p><p>Verbal nouns behave like ordinary nouns, are masculine or feminine and may have a plural form.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 161, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(i) Statement", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.614930Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.616712Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Seo bord agus sin cathaoir.</strong> — This here is a table and that there is a chair.</p><p><strong>Seo é an bord agus sin í an chathaoir.</strong> — This here is the table and that there is the chair.</p><p><strong>Seo í Cáit, sin é Máirtín agus siúd iad na daoine eile.</strong> — This here is Cáit, that there is Máirtín, and those there are the other people.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 162, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(ii) Negation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.617364Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.619499Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ní heo é an bord.</strong> — This here is not the table.</p><p>Apart from the statement form, 'seo, 'sin, 'siúd are used. Ní prefixes a h.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 163, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(iii) Questions", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.620133Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.622407Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ab eo é an bord?</strong> — Is this (thing) here the table?</p><p><strong>Nach 'eo é an bord?</strong> — Is this (thing) here not the table?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 164, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(iv) Indirect speech", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.623042Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.625436Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Deir sé gurb 'eo é an bord.</strong> — He says that this here is the table.</p><p><strong>Deir sé nach 'eo é an bord.</strong> — He says that this here is not the table.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 165, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(v) 'If' clauses", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.626034Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.627554Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Mas 'eo é an bord, tá mé sásta.</strong> — If this here is the table, I am satisfied.</p><p><strong>Marab 'eo é an bord, níl mé sásta.</strong> — If this here is not the table, I am not satisfied.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 166, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(vi) Some particular expressions", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.628150Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.630243Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In certain expressions, e.g. <strong>sin é mo bharúil</strong> 'that is my opinion'; <strong>sin é mo thuairim</strong> 'that is my way of thinking'; <strong>sin é an chaoi</strong> 'that is the way (it is)', é is used before a feminine noun. It can be thought of as referring to what has just been or is about to be said, rather than to the noun itself.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 167, grammar_rule_id: 72, label: "(vii) Summary table", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.630840Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:36:20.633110Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Seo (sin, siúd)</td><td>Seo é an bord.</td></tr><tr><td>Ní heo (hin, hiúd)</td><td>Ní heo é an bord.</td></tr><tr><td>Ab</td><td>Ab eo é an bord?</td></tr><tr><td>Nach</td><td>Nach 'eo é an bord?</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé gurb</td><td>Deir sé gurb 'eo é an bord.</td></tr><tr><td>nach</td><td>Deir sé nach 'eo é an bord.</td></tr><tr><td>Mas</td><td>Mas 'eo é an bord, tá mé sásta.</td></tr><tr><td>Marab</td><td>Marab 'eo é an bord, níl mé sásta.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The use of ní, ab, nach, etc. before 'eo, 'in, 'iúd is the same as the special forms of is used before third person pronouns (see Lesson 11).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 168, grammar_rule_id: 73, label: "(i) In progressive construction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.324198Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.328769Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an crann ag fás.</strong> — The tree is growing.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag pósadh.</strong> — Cáit is getting married.</p><p><strong>Bhí siad ag ól fíon agus ag imirt cluichí.</strong> — They were drinking wine and playing games.</p><p><strong>Bhí Máirtín ag moladh Cháit.</strong> — Máirtín was praising Cáit.</p><p class=\"note\">It may help to think of the obsolete English construction 'the tree is a'growing'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 169, grammar_rule_id: 73, label: "(ii) After certain verbs", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.329716Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.333284Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Feicim fear ag siúl ar an tsráid.</strong> — I see a man walking on the street.</p><p><strong>Beidh Cáit ag teacht anseo ag foghlaim Gaeilge.</strong> — Cáit will be coming here to learn Irish.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín ag goil ag foghlaim Spáinnise.</strong> — Máirtín is going to learn Spanish.</p><p><strong>Beidh sé ag tosú ag foghlaim Spáinnise amárach.</strong> — He will be starting to learn Spanish tomorrow.</p><p class=\"note\">Ag + verbal noun can be used after certain verbs of perception, e.g. feicim 'I see', cloisim 'I hear'; of motion, e.g. teacht 'coming', goil 'going'; and certain verbs marking the beginning or duration of an action, e.g. tosú 'beginning', goil 'going to', 'about to'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 170, grammar_rule_id: 75, label: "(i) General usage", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.347220Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.349146Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Pronoun</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>mé</td><td>do mo bhualadh /gə mə/</td></tr><tr><td>tú</td><td>do do bhualadh /gə də/</td></tr><tr><td>é</td><td>dhá bhualadh /y a:/</td></tr><tr><td>í</td><td>dhá bualadh /ya:/</td></tr><tr><td>muid</td><td>dhár mbualadh /ya:/</td></tr><tr><td>sibh</td><td>dhúr mbualadh /y a:/</td></tr><tr><td>iad</td><td>dhá mbualadh /ya:/</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Tá sé do mo bhualadh.</strong> — He is hitting me. (lit. He is to my hitting.)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 171, grammar_rule_id: 75, label: "(ii) Passive construction", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.350485Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:04.352363Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>This construction can also be used in a passive way:</p><p><strong>Tá an teach dhá thógáil.</strong> — The house is being built. (lit. .. .is to its building)</p><p><strong>Tá an balla dhá phéinteáil.</strong> — The wall is being painted.</p><p><strong>Tá an fhuinneog dhá briseadh.</strong> — The window is being broken.</p><p class=\"note\">When used in this reflexive or passive way dhá is written as á in Official Standard Irish. In this dialect it may alternatively be pronounced as dhá or á.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 172, grammar_rule_id: 78, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.308178Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.311236Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí an dochtúir ag Cáit inné.</strong> — The doctor was at (i.e. visited) Cáit yesterday.</p><p><strong>Bhí an dochtúir ag an mbean eile freisin.</strong> — The doctor was at the other woman too.</p><p><strong>Bhí an dochtúir agam inné.</strong> — The doctor was at me yesterday.</p><p>Ordinary form Contrast Emphasis Emphatic contrast</p><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Contrast</th><th>Emphasis</th><th>Emphatic contrast</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>agam /a:gmə/</td><td>agamsa</td><td>agam féin</td><td>agamsa mé féin</td></tr><tr><td>'am /a:m/</td><td>'amsa</td><td>'am féin</td><td>'amsa mé féin</td></tr><tr><td>agat /a:gdə/</td><td>agatsa</td><td>agat féin</td><td>agatsa thú féin</td></tr><tr><td>'ad /a:d/</td><td>adsa</td><td>'ad féin</td><td>'adsa thú féin</td></tr><tr><td>aige /eg'ə/</td><td>aigesan</td><td>aige féin</td><td>aigesan é féin</td></tr><tr><td>aice /ek'ə/</td><td>aicese</td><td>aice féin</td><td>aicese í féin</td></tr><tr><td>againn /a:gNə'/</td><td>againne</td><td>againn féin</td><td>againne muid féin</td></tr><tr><td>'ainn /æ:N'/</td><td>'ainne</td><td>'ainn féin</td><td>'ainne muid féin</td></tr><tr><td>agaibh /a:gi:/</td><td>agaibhse</td><td>agaibh féin</td><td>agaibhse sibh féin</td></tr><tr><td>acu /a:kbə/</td><td>acusan</td><td>acu féin</td><td>acusan iad féin</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Second person plural pronouns pronounced with two syllables have a final long i, e.g. agaibh /ægi:/; those pronounced with one syllable have a final slender b, e.g. sibh /s'ib'/. Third person plural prepositional pronouns are pronounced with a final broad b which is not shown in spelling, e.g. acu /a:kob/. The forms ag, aige, aice have an initial e sound and a slender consonant as though spelt oig, oige, oice. The contrast forms (corresponding to mise, etc., see Lesson 2) are the same for almost all prepositions. The first and second singular add -sa to a broad consonant, e.g. agamsa, agatsa; -se is added to a final slender consonant (an example will be seen later in Lesson 22).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 173, grammar_rule_id: 78, label: "(ii) Usage", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.314656Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.316930Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí an dochtúir aige seo inné.</strong> — The doctor was at this fellow yesterday.</p><p>Aige, aice, acu (the ordinary form of the third person prepositional pronouns) can be used with seo, sin, siúd, e.g. aige seo 'at this fellow/thing', acu siúd 'at those people/things'. The choice of aige or aice, as in the case of all pronouns, follows the rules given for sé/sí (see Lessons 5 and 10).</p><p><strong>Tá carr agam féin agus ag Cáit.</strong> — Cáit and I have a car.</p><p>As is usual where there is a compound subject or object (see Lesson 9), the ordinary forms (agam, agat, etc.) are not used.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 174, grammar_rule_id: 78, label: "(iii) Word order", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.319599Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.321717Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí an dochtúir inné agam.</strong> — The doctor was at me yesterday.</p><p>After the verb tá, when the ordinary form of a prepositional pronoun is used, an adverb or short adverbial phrase may precede it, e.g. inné agam, but agamsa inné, agam féin inné, etc.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 175, grammar_rule_id: 79, label: "(i) Basic meaning 'at'", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.328420Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.331508Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag an ngeata.</strong> — Cáit is at the gate.</p><p>Idiomatically, ag expresses 'to have something' (see Lesson 6) or 'to have an ability, quality or knowledge':</p><p><strong>Tá Spáinnis agam.</strong> — I have Spanish. I know Spanish.</p><p><strong>Tá caint agam.</strong> — I have talk. I can talk.</p><p><strong>Tá ceol agam.</strong> — I have music. I can make music.</p><p><strong>Tá foighid agam.</strong> — I have patience. I have patience.</p><p><strong>Tá ciall agam.</strong> — I have sense. I have sense.</p><p><strong>Tá an ceart agam.</strong> — I have the right. I am right / have the right.</p><p><strong>Tá súil agam.</strong> — I have an eye. I hope.</p><p><strong>Tá a fhios agam.</strong> — I have its knowledge. I know.</p><p><strong>An bhfuil a fhios agat é?</strong> — 'Do you know it?'. <strong>Tá a fhios.</strong> — 'Yes, I do.' It is usual to repeat a fhios in a response.</p><p><strong>Níl a fhios cé atá ann.</strong> — 'People don't know who is there'; <strong>Tá súil go mbeidh Cáit anseo amárach.</strong> — 'People hope that Cáit will be here tomorrow'; a certain few idioms can be used without any preposition to mean 'people do...', 'one does ...', corresponding to the autonomous form of the verb; see Lesson 2.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 176, grammar_rule_id: 79, label: "(ii) Some other meanings", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.332186Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:37:51.335025Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) 'to' (a person or place)</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag goil ag an dochtúir.</strong> — Cáit is going to the doctor.</p><p class=\"note\">*In this sense ag has supplanted an older chuig which survives elsewhere and is still often written.</p><p>(b) 'of' (a person), 'for' (a person or thing)</p><p><strong>Tá sé go deas ag Cáit a ghoil ansin.</strong> — It is nice of Cáit to go there.</p><p>Similarly go dona 'bad', náireach 'shameful'.</p><p><strong>Tá sé éasca ag Cáit a ghoil ansin.</strong> — It is easy for Cáit to go there.</p><p>Similarly nádúrthach 'natural', fánach 'pointless', go maith 'good'.</p><p>(c) quantity (of persons or things)</p><p><strong>Tá go leor acu anseo.</strong> — There are plenty of them here.</p><p>Similarly roinnt 'some', neart 'lots of'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 177, grammar_rule_id: 81, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.149784Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.154306Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá bainne ar an mbord.</strong> — There is milk on the table.</p><p><strong>Bhí cóta ar chathaoir anseo inné.</strong> — There was a coat on a chair here yesterday.</p><p><strong>Bhí hata orm inné.</strong> — I had a hat on yesterday.</p><p>Ar /er'/ 'on' causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>ar chathaoir</strong> 'on a chair', except in certain adverbial phrases, e.g. <strong>ar fad</strong> 'all together', <strong>ar cuairt</strong> 'visiting', <strong>ar ball</strong> 'in awhile'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 178, grammar_rule_id: 81, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.155792Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.159277Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary forms</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>orm</td><td>/ormə/</td></tr><tr><td>ort</td><td>/ort/</td></tr><tr><td>air</td><td>/er'/</td></tr><tr><td>uirthi</td><td>/orə/</td></tr><tr><td>orainn</td><td>/orəN'/</td></tr><tr><td>oraibh</td><td>/ori:/</td></tr><tr><td>orthu</td><td>/orbə/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both <strong>ar</strong> and <strong>air</strong> are pronounced with an e-sound and palatalized r, as though spelt <em>oir</em>. The contrast forms and general usage is the same as that of <strong>ag</strong> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>orm, ormsa, orm féin, ormsa mé féin</strong> (see Lesson 15).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 179, grammar_rule_id: 81, label: "(iii) Meanings of ar", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.160371Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:38:30.166370Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Basic meaning 'on':</p><p><strong>Tá bainne ar an mbord.</strong> — There is milk on the table.</p><p>Idiomatically <strong>ar</strong> expresses:</p><p>(a) to wear, have on:</p><p><strong>Tá hata agus fáinní orm.</strong> — I am wearing a hat and rings.</p><p>Similarly: <strong>cóta</strong> 'a coat', and any other item of dress.</p><p>(b) to have a natural aspect, feature or appearance:</p><p><strong>Tá féasóg, lámha fada agus cuma thuirseach ar an múinteoir.</strong> — The teacher has a beard, long arms and a tired appearance.</p><p>Similarly: <strong>éadan deas</strong> 'a nice face', <strong>strainc</strong> 'a grimace', <strong>dath buí</strong> 'a yellow colouring', <strong>droch-chosúlacht</strong> 'a bad appearance'. Also <strong>tá báisteach, sneachta etc. air</strong> 'it looks like rain (snow etc.)'.</p><p>(c) an emotional or physical state (incl. disease) or estimation:</p><p>literal meaning:</p><p><strong>Céard atá orm?</strong> — What is on me? What is the matter with me?</p><p><strong>Tá brón orm (go/nach)</strong> — There is sorrow on me. I am sorry (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá áthas orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am glad (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá imní orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am worried (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá faitíos orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am afraid (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá náire orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am ashamed (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá aiféala orm (go/nach)</strong> — I regret (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá iontas orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am amazed (that... (not))</p><p><strong>Tá amhras orm (go/nach)</strong> — I am doubtful / suspicious ...</p><p><strong>Tá ocras orm.</strong> — I am hungry.</p><p><strong>Tá tart orm.</strong> — I am thirsty.</p><p><strong>Tá fonn orm</strong> — I desire to ...</p><p><strong>Tá drogall orm</strong> — I am reluctant to ...</p><p><strong>Tá deifir orm.</strong> — I am in a hurry.</p><p><strong>Tá moill orm.</strong> — I am delayed.</p><p><strong>Tá fearg orm.</strong> — I am angry.</p><p><strong>Tá clú orm.</strong> — I am famous.</p><p><strong>Tá slaghdán (etc.) orm.</strong> — I have a cold.</p><p class=\"note\">Note that a certain few idioms can be used impersonally (corresponding to the autonomous form of the verb).</p><p><strong>Tá faitíos nach mbeidh Cáit ann.</strong> — People are afraid that Cáit won't be there.</p><p><strong>Tá amhras go mbeidh sé tinn.</strong> — People suspect that he will be sick.</p><p><strong>Níl a fhios cé mhéad atá ansin.</strong> — People don't know how much there is there. There is a great deal there.</p><p>(d) to have a name, a price:</p><p><strong>Máirtín atá orm.</strong> — Máirtín is my name.</p><p><strong>Punt atá ar an leabhar seo.</strong> — (It is) a pound that book costs.</p><p>(The word order here will be explained in Lesson 34). - Somewhat similarly: <strong>Tá Máirtín sásta é a dhéanamh ar phunt.</strong> 'Máirtín is willing to do it for a pound.'</p><p>(e) to have to do something:</p><p><strong>Tá orm bainne a ól.</strong> — I have to drink milk.</p><p>(f) to have or do something to someone's disadvantage:</p><p><strong>Tá sé ag briseadh fuinneoga orm.</strong> — He is breaking windows 'on me'.</p><p><strong>Tá airgead ag Cáit orm.</strong> — I owe Cáit money. ('C. has money to my disadvantage')</p><p><strong>Bhí spóirt ag Cáit orm.</strong> — Cáit had fun at my expense.</p><p>(g) (adverbially) to describe a state:</p><p><strong>Tá sé ar cuairt anseo.</strong> — He is visiting here.</p><p><strong>Tá sí ar an gcoláiste.</strong> — She is attending the college.</p><p>(h) to strengthen the imperative of verbs expressing continued action:</p><p><strong>Fan ort!</strong> — Wait (will you)!</p><p><strong>Foighid ort!</strong> — Have patience!</p><p>A few verbs expressing continued action (and the quasi-imperatives of the sort in the second example above) use <strong>ort/oraibh</strong> to strengthen an imperative.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 180, grammar_rule_id: 83, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.803771Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.806324Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Feicim an bord atá ansin.</strong> — I see the table which is there.</p><p><strong>Feicim an bord a bhfuil an leabhar air.</strong> — I see the table which the book is on.</p><p><strong>Feicim an bhean atá ansin.</strong> — I see the woman who is there.</p><p><strong>Feicim an bhean a bhfuil an teach aici.</strong> — I see the woman who has the house.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 181, grammar_rule_id: 83, label: "(ii) Pronunciation", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.807236Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.810454Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(i) if the clause contains a preposition: <strong>an bord a bhfuil an leabhar air</strong></p><p>(ii) if the clause is in genitive relation ('whose'): <strong>an fear a bhfuil a mhac anseo</strong> — 'the man whose son is here'.</p><p>(iii) if the clause refers to:</p><p>(a) time: <strong>Sin é an t-am a raibh Cáit anseo.</strong> — That is the time that (at which) Cáit was here.</p><p>Similarly: <em>an lá</em> 'the day', <em>an tseachtain</em> 'the week', <em>an uair</em> 'the occasion', etc.</p><p>(b) a reason: <strong>Sin é an fáth a mbeidh Cáit anseo.</strong> — That is (the reason) why Cáit will be here.</p><p>Similarly: following <em>an t-údar</em> 'the grounds for, the reason'.</p><p>(c) the word <em>áit</em> 'place': <strong>Sin é an áit a bhfuil Cáit.</strong> — That is where (the place at which) Cáit is.</p><p>In the same way <em>cén t-am</em> 'at what time', <em>cén fáth</em> 'why', <em>cén áit</em> 'where', are followed by an indirect relative, e.g. <strong>Cén t-am a raibh tú anseo?</strong> — 'What time were you here at?'</p><p class=\"note\">1 An older construction <em>an bord ar a bhfuil an leabhar</em> 'the table on which the book is' is still often used in writing, and more rarely in speech.</p><p class=\"note\">2 <em>An uair</em> 'the occasion, the time' may take either a direct or indirect clause: <em>an uair a bhí tú anseo</em> or <em>an uair a raibh tú anseo</em> 'the time you were here'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 182, grammar_rule_id: 86, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.834451Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.836908Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>asam</td><td>/a:smə/</td></tr><tr><td>asat</td><td>/æsdə/</td></tr><tr><td>as</td><td>/a:s/</td></tr><tr><td>aisti</td><td>/æ:s't'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>asainn</td><td>/a:sNə'/</td></tr><tr><td>asaibh</td><td>/a:si:/</td></tr><tr><td>astu</td><td>/a:stob/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage is the same as that of <em>ag</em> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <em>asam, asamsa, asam féin, asamsa mé féin</em> (see Lesson 16).</p><p><em>Asat, asainn, asaibh</em> have an alternative pronunciation /a:sddə/, /a:sdNə'/, /a:sdi:/ resp.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 183, grammar_rule_id: 86, label: "(iii) Meaning of as", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.837553Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.840591Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Basic meaning 'out of'</p><p><strong>Tóig leabhar aníos as an mála!</strong> — Take a book (up) out of the bag!</p><p>More idiomatically <em>as</em> is used:</p><p>(a) to denote 'out of a material': <strong>Tá Cáit ag déanamh sciorta as an éadach.</strong> — Cáit is making a skirt out of the cloth.</p><p>(b) to express 'out of', as a result of a state of mind: <strong>Bíonn Cáit á dhéanamh as fearg.</strong> — Cáit (normally) does it out of anger.</p><p>Similarly: <em>as taidh/taid</em> 'in a fit of temper'.</p><p>(c) to express 'from' (a town, county, country, island): <strong>Beidh Cáit ag teacht as Meiriceá amárach.</strong> — Cáit will be coming from America tomorrow.</p><p>Similarly: <em>as Baile Átha Cliath</em> 'from Dublin', <em>as an Spáinn</em> 'from Spain', etc.</p><p>(d) to express 'off, from, out of' (smell, noise, movement): <strong>Tá boladh as an madadh sin.</strong> — That dog smells; there is a smell off that dog.</p><p>Similarly: <em>torann as</em> 'noise from', <em>cor as</em> 'movement from'.</p><p>(e) to express 'out, off' (of fire, machines, etc.): <strong>Tá an tine as.</strong> — The fire is out.</p><p><strong>Cas as an raidió!</strong> — Turn off the radio!</p><p>(f) with <em>féin</em> to mean 'alone': <strong>Tá Máirtín as féin anois.</strong> — Máirtín is alone now.</p><p>(g) in certain adverbial expressions: <strong>Tá sé as cuma.</strong> — It is out of shape.</p><p>Similarly: <em>as marc</em> 'off target, wrong'.</p><p>(h) with <em>a chéile</em> to express 'consecutive', 'in a row', 'asunder', 'apart': <strong>Bhí Cáit anseo trí lá as a chéile.</strong> — Cáit was here three days in a row.</p><p><strong>Tá an teach ag titim as a chéile.</strong> — The house is falling apart.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 184, grammar_rule_id: 86, label: "(iv) Idiomatic use of a with copula", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.841203Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:39:15.843329Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cé as Cáit?</strong> — Is as Gaillimh í. — Where is Cáit from? She is from Galway.</p><p>The copula (is, ní, ar, nach, etc., see Lesson 11) is used with <em>as</em> to mean 'is from', 'comes from', e.g. <em>Is as Baile Átha Cliath é</em> 'He is from Dublin'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 185, grammar_rule_id: 88, label: "(i) Direct relative", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.894993Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.897605Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Sin é an rud is féidir a dhéanamh.</strong> — That is the thing that can be done.</p><p>The positive direct relative is the same as the statement form, e.g. <em>is féidir...</em> 'that/who can ...'</p><p><strong>Sin é an rud nach féidir a dhéanamh.</strong> — That is the thing that cannot be done.</p><p>The negative direct relative is expressed by using <em>nach</em> 'that... not', e.g. <em>nach féidir...</em> 'that/who cannot'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 186, grammar_rule_id: 88, label: "(ii) Indirect relative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.899405Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.901779Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Sin é an fear ar maith leis an bord.</strong> — That is the man who likes the table.</p><p>The positive indirect relative is expressed by using <em>ar</em> 'that, who', e.g. <em>ar maith le...</em> 'that likes ...', <em>ar leis...</em> 'that owns ...'.</p><p><strong>Sin é an fear nach maith leis an bord.</strong> — That is the man who does not like the table.</p><p>The negative indirect relative is expressed by using <em>nach</em> 'that... not', e.g. <em>nach maith le...</em> 'that does not like ...'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 187, grammar_rule_id: 90, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.913003Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.914974Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo leis an mbosca.</strong> — Cáit is here with the box.</p><p><strong>Tá mé ag scríobh le peann mór.</strong> — I am writing with a big pen.</p><p><strong>Níl Cáit sásta le hÚna.</strong> — Cáit is not satisfied with Úna.</p><p><em>Le</em> 'with' prefixes a h (which is not normally pronounced) to a directly following noun which begins with a vowel, e.g. <em>le hÚna</em> /l'u:Nə/ 'with Úna'. Before the singular article <em>le</em> takes the form <em>leis</em> (see Lesson 5).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 188, grammar_rule_id: 90, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.916011Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.917924Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>liom</td><td>/l'um/</td></tr><tr><td>leat</td><td>/l'æ:t/</td></tr><tr><td>leis</td><td>/l'es'/</td></tr><tr><td>léi</td><td>/l'e:/</td></tr><tr><td>linn</td><td>/l'in'/</td></tr><tr><td>libh</td><td>/l'iv'/</td></tr><tr><td>leo</td><td>/l'o:/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The preposition <em>le</em> and all its forms are pronounced with an initial lenited l. (Prepositions tend to be permanently lenited.)</p><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as that of <em>ag</em> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <em>liom, liomsa, liomsa mé féin</em> (see Lesson 16). Note, however, that <em>leis</em> has the contrast form <em>leisean</em>, i.e. -<em>sean</em> rather than -<em>son</em> is added.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 189, grammar_rule_id: 90, label: "(iii) Basic meaning 'with'", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.918584Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.921394Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The basic meaning of <em>le</em> is accompaniment or instrumentality.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo le Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit is here with Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín ag obair le spáid.</strong> — Máirtín is working with a spade.</p><p>More idiomatically <em>le</em> expresses:</p><p>(a) 'with the motion of'</p><p><strong>Tá an bád ag imeacht le sruth.</strong> — The boat is going with the current.</p><p>Similarly: <em>leis an ngaoth</em> 'with the wind'</p><p>(b) 'at the same time as'</p><p><strong>Bhí Cáit anseo leis an lá.</strong> — Cáit was here at daybreak. (lit. 'with the day')</p><p>Similarly: <em>leis an oíche</em> 'at nightfall'</p><p>(c) 'with', 'as a result of'</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit tinn leis an tuirse.</strong> — Cáit is sick with the tiredness.</p><p>Similarly: <em>tinn le himní/le hól</em> 'sick with worry / drink', <em>spalptha leis an tart</em> 'parched with the thirst'.</p><p>(d) 'away', 'continuing'</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag obair léi.</strong> — Cáit is working away / working on / continuing to work.</p><p>Similarly: any verbal noun.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 190, grammar_rule_id: 90, label: "(iv) Secondary meaning: 'to'", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.922373Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.924880Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) 'to', 'for the purpose'</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo le balla a phéinteáil.</strong> — Cáit is here to paint a wall.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo le é a phéinteáil.</strong> — Cáit is here to paint it.</p><p>(b) 'to', 'due to be'</p><p><strong>Tá balla ansin le péinteáil.</strong> — There is a wall there to paint.</p><p>In this usage <em>le</em> normally prefixes <em>n</em> to an initial vowel, e.g. <strong>Tá neart le n-ithe agus le n-ól.</strong> — 'There is plenty to eat and to drink.'</p><p>More idiomatically it expresses:</p><p>(a) 'to', 'towards'</p><p><strong>Bhí Cáit go deas le Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit was nice to Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá fearg ar Cháit le Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit is angry with Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá foighid ag Cáit le Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit has patience with Máirtín.</p><p>Similarly: <em>go dona</em> 'bad', <em>múinte</em> 'polite', <em>lach</em> 'pleasant', <em>gnaiúil</em> 'decent, generous', <em>gránna</em> 'horrible'; <em>Tá deifir</em> ('hurry') <em>ar Cháit le ...</em> <em>Tá loighe</em> ('an inclination')/ <em>súil</em> ('is expecting')/ <em>éad</em> ('jealousy') <em>ag Cáit le ...</em></p><p>(b) 'to', 'against'</p><p><strong>Tá droim Cháit leis an mballa.</strong> — Cáit's back is to the wall.</p><p>Similarly: <em>leis an doras</em> 'to the door', <em>leis an gclai</em> 'to the ditch / stonewall'.</p><p>(c) duration</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo le seachtain.</strong> — Cáit is here a week.</p><p>Similarly: <em>mí</em> 'month', <em>bliain</em> 'year', <em>cúpla lá</em> 'a few days'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 191, grammar_rule_id: 91, label: "(i) To express 'it seems'", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.936777Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.938855Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is maith le Cáit bainne.</strong> — Milk seems good to Cáit. / C. likes milk.</p><p><strong>Ní miste liom é.</strong> — I don't mind it.</p><p>The copula is used with <em>le</em> in certain idiomatic expressions and has the general meaning of 'it seems'. Further examples are:</p><p><strong>Is cuma le Cáit.</strong> — It seems all the same to Cáit.</p><p><strong>Ní fearr le Cáit.</strong> — It does not seem better to Cáit. Cáit doesn't prefer.</p><p><strong>Ar ionadh le Cáit?</strong> — Did it seem amazing to Cáit? Was Cáit amazed?</p><p><strong>Nach fiú le Cáit?</strong> — Does it not seem worthwhile to Cáit?</p><p><strong>Deir sé gur dóigh le Cáit.</strong> — He says that it seems likely to Cáit. ...that Cáit supposes</p><p><strong>Deir sé nach cuimhne le Cáit.</strong> — He says that Cáit doesn't remember.</p><p><strong>Más féidir le Cáit...</strong> — If Cáit can.</p><p><strong>Mara miste le Cáit...</strong> — If Cáit doesn't mind.</p><p><em>Is cuma le ...</em> 'it seems all the same to ...' is generally only used positively, e.g. <em>is cuma le...</em>, <em>ar cuma le...</em>, <em>gur cuma le...</em></p><p>To a limited extent <em>is ... le</em> can be used with other adjectives, e.g. <em>is deacair liom é a chreidiúint</em> 'I find it hard to believe'. These are generally adjectives implying a relative estimation, e.g. <em>réidh</em>, 'easy', <em>ard</em> 'high', <em>íseal</em> 'low'.</p><p>Some of these idioms can be used impersonally (with no prepositional pronoun): <em>Is fearr a bheith sásta</em> 'it is better to be satisfied'. Similarly: <em>is cuma faoi</em> 'it seems all the same about it', 'it doesn't matter about it'; <em>is dóigh go mbeidh tú anseo</em> 'it is probable that you will be here', 'you will probably be here'; <em>is féidir Gaeilge a fhoghlaim</em> 'it is possible to learn Irish'. Note also: <em>is deacair a rá</em> 'it is difficult to say'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 192, grammar_rule_id: 91, label: "(ii) To express ownership", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.939424Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:01.940982Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is le Cáit an teach seo.</strong> — Cáit owns this house.</p><p><strong>An leatsa an leabhar sin nó cé leis é?</strong> — Do you own this book or who owns it?</p><p><strong>Ní liom. Níl a fhios agam cé leis é.</strong> — No. I don't know who owns it.</p><p>The copula is used with <em>le</em> to express ownership. In responses the ordinary form of the prepositional pronoun (<em>liom, leat</em>, etc.) is repeated with the copula.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 193, grammar_rule_id: 94, label: "(i) Form used with noun or pronoun (corresponding to beidh)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.156120Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.159501Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glanfaidh Cáit an bord agus nífidh sí na soithí.</strong> — Cáit will clear the table and she will wash the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léifidh Cáit an leabhar ach ní bhrisfidh sí an chathaoir.</strong> — Cáit will read the book but she will not break the chair.</p><p><strong>Coinneoidh sé an cóta agus salóidh sé é.</strong> — He will keep the coat and he will dirty it.</p><p><strong>Osclóidh tusa an geata agus imreoidh muide cluichí.</strong> — You will open the gate and we will play games.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>adds -faidh/-fidh /ə/</td><td>adds -óidh/-eoidh /o:/</td></tr><tr><td>glan + faidh = glanfaidh</td><td>sal(aigh) + óidh = salóidh</td></tr><tr><td>bris + fidh = brisfidh</td><td>coinn(igh) + eoidh = coinneoidh</td></tr><tr><td>léi(gh) + fidh = léifidh</td><td>osc(ai)l + óidh = osclóidh</td></tr><tr><td>ní(gh) + fidh = nífidh</td><td>im(i)r + eoidh = imreoidh</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A final gh is dropped and a short syllable is lengthened; see Lesson 12.</p><p>All verbal endings are spelt with a 'broad' vowel, e.g. -faidh/-óidh after a broad consonant, e.g. glan-, sal-, oscl-), and with a 'slender' vowel, e.g. -fidh/-eoidh after a slender consonant, e.g. bris-, coinn-, imr- (see Lesson 12).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 194, grammar_rule_id: 94, label: "(ii) Autonomous form (corresponding to beifear)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.160358Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.163156Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -far /ar/</td><td>adds -ófar/-eofar /air/</td></tr><tr><td>glan + far = glanfar</td><td>sal(aigh) + ófar = salófar</td></tr><tr><td>bris + fear = brisfear</td><td>coinn(igh) + eofar = coinneofar</td></tr><tr><td>léi(gh) + fear = léifear</td><td>osc(ai)l + ófar = osclófar</td></tr><tr><td>ní(gh) + fear = nífear</td><td>im(i)r + eofar = imreofar</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Although in pronunciation the ending is /a:r/ in all cases, type 1 and type 2 are spelt differently. The pronunciation /(o:)frə / is, however, usually heard in neighbouring and other dialects.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 195, grammar_rule_id: 94, label: "(iii) Pronunciation of verbs with roots ending in b, d, g, bh, mh", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.164153Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.167831Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>A root-final</p><p>b (scuab) is pronounced as p (scuabfaidh, scuabfar)</p><p>d (goid) t (goidfidh, goidfear)</p><p>g (póg) c (pógfaidh, pógfar)</p><p>bh (scríobh) f (scríobhfaidh, scríobhfar)</p><p>mh (snámh) f (snámhfaidh, snámhfar)</p><p>before any verbal ending spelt with an initial f</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 196, grammar_rule_id: 94, label: "(iv) Direct relative", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.168777Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.170931Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Future</td><td>Future relative ('that will...')</td><td>Future</td><td>Future relative ('that will...')</td></tr><tr><td>glanfaidh</td><td>a ghlanfas</td><td>salóidh</td><td>a shalós</td></tr><tr><td>brisfidh</td><td>a bhrisfeas</td><td>coinneoidh</td><td>a choinneos</td></tr><tr><td>léifidh</td><td>a léifeas</td><td>osclóidh</td><td>a osclós</td></tr><tr><td>nífidh</td><td>a nifeas</td><td>imreoidh</td><td>a imreos</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In type 1 verbs, -(a)idh is dropped and -(e)as is added to any uncombined form; in type 2 verbs, -idh is dropped and -s is added to any uncombined form.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 197, grammar_rule_id: 95, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.176076Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.178578Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an pota faoin mbord.</strong> — The pot is under the table.</p><p><strong>Tá an leabhar faoi bhosca.</strong> — The book is under a box.</p><p><strong>Tá an chathaoir fum.</strong> — The chair is under me.</p><p>Faoi 'under' causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. faoi chathaoir 'under a chair'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 198, grammar_rule_id: 95, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.179307Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.181603Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>fum</td><td>under me /fu:m/</td></tr><tr><td>fut</td><td>under you /fu:t/</td></tr><tr><td>faoi</td><td>under him/it /fi:/</td></tr><tr><td>fuithi</td><td>under her /fu:/</td></tr><tr><td>fuinn</td><td>under us /fu:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>fuibh</td><td>under you (pl.) /fu:b'/</td></tr><tr><td>futhu</td><td>under them /fu:b/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as those of ag and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. fóm, fumsa, fám féin, fumsa mé féin; see Lesson 16.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 199, grammar_rule_id: 95, label: "(iii) Meanings of faoi", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.182311Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.185028Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá leabhar faoin gcathaoir.</strong> — There is a book under the chair.</p><p>Idiomatically faoi is used:</p><p>(a) to express intent</p><p><strong>Tá fúm Cáit a phósadh.</strong> — I intend to marry Cáit.</p><p>(b) (with bualadh) to express impact</p><p><strong>Bíonn na carranna ag bualadh faoi bhallaí.</strong> — The cars (as a rule) (are) hit(ting) against walls.</p><p>(c) to express motion (in certain phrases)</p><p><strong>Tá siúl faoi.</strong> — lit. There is motion under him. He is going fast.</p><p><strong>Tá fuadar faoi.</strong> — There is a flurry under him. He is in a hurry.</p><p><strong>Tá fás faoi.</strong> — There is growth under him. It is growing.</p><p>(d) in some common adverbial phrases</p><p><strong>faoi smacht</strong> — under control</p><p><strong>faoi bhláth</strong> — in bloom, flourishing</p><p><strong>faoi lánseol</strong> — under full sail, in full swing</p><p><strong>faoi bhealach</strong> — under way</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 200, grammar_rule_id: 95, label: "(iv) secondary meaning 'around, about'", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.185660Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.188111Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In some particular usages, faoi has taken over the function of an older preposition um meaning 'around, about, concerning'.</p><p><strong>Tá mé ag caint faoin mbean sin.</strong> — I am talking about that woman.</p><p>Similarly: <strong>Tá imní/áthas/brón/aiféala orm faoi</strong> — 'I am worried/glad/sorry/regretful about it', and in the adverbial phrases:</p><p><strong>faoi Nollaig</strong> — around Christmas</p><p><strong>faoi seo</strong> — by now</p><p><strong>faoi Cháisc</strong> — around Easter</p><p><strong>faoi láthair</strong> — at present</p><p><strong>faoi Bhealtaine</strong> — around May</p><p><strong>faoi dheireadh</strong> — at last</p><p><strong>faoi Shamhain</strong> — around Hallowtide</p><p><strong>faoin tír</strong> — in the country</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 201, grammar_rule_id: 96, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.195386Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.198254Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag dul go dtí an áit chéanna.</strong> — Cáit is going to the same place.</p><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo go dtí an lá sin.</strong> — Cáit will be here until that day.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín ag dul go Sasana.</strong> — Máirtín is going to England.</p><p>Go dtí /gə'd'i:/ 'to, as far as, up to' is used with definite nouns, e.g. go dtí an áit 'to the place', go dtí Déardaoin 'until Thursday', go dtí Máirtín 'to Máirtín'. However, go (which prefixes h to a following vowel) is used with place-names without the article, e.g. go Sasana 'to England', go Meiriceá 'to America', go hÉirinn 'to Ireland', go hAlbain 'to Scotland', and in certain adverbial phrases, e.g. go maidin 'until morning', lán go béal 'full to the (lit. mouth) brim'.</p><p>Go is generally omitted before certain place-names beginning with a consonant, e.g. ag dul Baile Átha Cliath 'going to Dublin', ag dul Gaillimh 'going to Galway'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 202, grammar_rule_id: 97, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.200923Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:41:56.202544Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Gan /gNə / 'without', go dtí /g'əd'i:/ 'to', seachas /s'æ:xsə / 'besides, except for' do not combine with following personal pronouns, e.g. gan mé, gan mise, gan mise mé féin 'without me'.</p><p>Generally these prepositions cause no change to a noun following directly, e.g. gan bróga 'without shoes', seachas Cáit 'besides Cáit'. Used following these prepositions, the article follows its normal rules, e.g. gan an bhean 'without the woman', go dtí an fhuinneog 'to the window', seachas an bord 'besides the table'.</p><p class=\"note\">Gan 'without' normally lenites a directly following abstract noun which does not begin with d, l, s, f e.g. gan mhoill 'without delay', gan mhaith 'without good, no good', gan mhairg 'without regret', gan mhothú 'without feeling, unconscious', gan phósadh 'unmarried'.</p><p class=\"note\">Gan sometimes prefixes a t to a following s, e.g. dall gan tsúil 'blind without an eye'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 203, grammar_rule_id: 98, label: "(i) Form used with noun or pronoun (corresponding to bheadh)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.305306Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.309187Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ghlanfadh Cáit an bord agus nífeadh sí</strong> — Cáit would clear the table and she would wash the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léifeadh Cáit an leabhar agus ní bhrisfeadh sí an chathaoir.</strong> — Cáit would read the book but she would not break the chair.</p><p><strong>Choinneodh sé an cóta agus shalódh sé é.</strong> — He would keep the coat and he would dirty it.</p><p><strong>D'osclódh sibhse an geata ach ní imreodh muide cluiche.</strong> — You (pl.) would open the gate but we wouldn't play games.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>glan + fadh</td><td>ghlanfadh</td></tr><tr><td>sal(aigh) + ódh</td><td>shalódh</td></tr><tr><td>bris + feadh</td><td>bhrisfeadh</td></tr><tr><td>coinn(igh) + eodh</td><td>choinneodh</td></tr><tr><td>léi(gh) + feadh</td><td>léifeadh</td></tr><tr><td>osc(ail) + ódh</td><td>d'osclódh</td></tr><tr><td>ní(gh) + feadh</td><td>nífeadh</td></tr><tr><td>im(igh) + eodh</td><td>d'imreodh</td></tr><tr><td>fliuch + fadh</td><td>d'fhliuchfadh</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The verb is lenited and adds -fadh, -feadh /xə/. A final gh is dropped and a short syllable is lengthened; see Lesson 12.</p><p>All verbal endings are spelt with a 'broad' vowel, e.g. -fadh, -ódh, after a broad consonant, e.g. glan-, sal-, oscl-, and with a 'slender' vowel, e.g. -fidh, -eodh, after a slender consonant, e.g. bris-, coinn-, imr-; see Lesson 12.</p><p>The basic form is always lenited, e.g. <strong>ghlanfadh sé</strong> 'he would clean'; where a verb has an initial vowel or f, d' is prefixed, e.g. <strong>d'osclódh sé</strong> 'he would open', <strong>d'fhliuchfadh sé</strong> 'he would wet'. The d' is normally pronounced broad or slender according to the following vowel.</p><p>Concerning the pronunciation of endings with a final -dh, see Lesson 7.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 204, grammar_rule_id: 98, label: "(ii) Combined forms (corresponding to bheinn, bheifeá, bheidís)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.310019Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.313673Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) First person singular</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -fainn /NƏ '/</td><td>adds -oinn, -eoinn /O:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanfainn</td><td>I would clean</td></tr><tr><td>shalóinn</td><td>I would dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisfinn</td><td>I would break</td></tr><tr><td>choinneoinn</td><td>I would keep</td></tr><tr><td>léifinn</td><td>I would read</td></tr><tr><td>d'osclóinn</td><td>I would open</td></tr><tr><td>nífinn</td><td>I would wash</td></tr><tr><td>d'imreoinn</td><td>I would play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchfainn</td><td>I would wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p>(b) Second person singular</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -feá / :/</td><td>adds -ofá, -eofá / :/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanfá</td><td>you would clean</td></tr><tr><td>shalófá</td><td>you would dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisfeá</td><td>you would break</td></tr><tr><td>choinneofá</td><td>you would keep</td></tr><tr><td>léifeá</td><td>you would read</td></tr><tr><td>d'osclófá</td><td>you would open</td></tr><tr><td>nífeá</td><td>you would wash</td></tr><tr><td>d'imreofá</td><td>you would play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchfá</td><td>you would wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Although in pronunciation the ending is /u:/ in all cases, type 1 and type 2 are spelt differently.</p><p>(c) Third person plural</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -faidís /əd'i:s'/</td><td>adds -oidís, -eoidís /o:d'i:s'/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanfaidís</td><td>they would clean</td></tr><tr><td>shalóidís</td><td>they would dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisfidís</td><td>they would break</td></tr><tr><td>choinneoidís</td><td>they would keep</td></tr><tr><td>léifidís</td><td>they would read</td></tr><tr><td>d'osclóidís</td><td>they would open</td></tr><tr><td>nídís</td><td>they would wash</td></tr><tr><td>d'imreoidís</td><td>they would play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchfaidís</td><td>they would wet</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 205, grammar_rule_id: 98, label: "(iii) Autonomous form (corresponding to bheifí)", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.314479Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.317291Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -faí /i:/</td><td>adds -ófaí, -eofaí /i:/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanfaí</td><td>one would clean</td></tr><tr><td>shalófaí</td><td>one would dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisfí</td><td>one would break</td></tr><tr><td>choinneofaí</td><td>one would keep</td></tr><tr><td>léifí</td><td>one would read</td></tr><tr><td>d'osclófaí</td><td>one would open</td></tr><tr><td>nífí</td><td>one would wash</td></tr><tr><td>d'imreofaí</td><td>one would play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchfaí</td><td>one would wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">Although in pronunciation the endings are usually /i:/ in all cases, type 1 and type 2 are spelt differently. (A less common pronunciation of type 2 endings is, in fact, /o:f'i:/.)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 206, grammar_rule_id: 98, label: "(iv) Pronunciation of verbs with roots ending in b d, g, bh, mh", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.318017Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.320978Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>A root-final</p><p>b (scuab) is pronounced as p (scuabfadh)</p><p>d (goid) t (goidfeadh)</p><p>g (póg) c (pógfadh)</p><p>bh (scríobh) f (scríobhfadh)</p><p>mh (snamh) f (snamhfadh)</p><p>before any verbal ending spelt with an initial f.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 207, grammar_rule_id: 99, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.327220Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.329710Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá litir anseo ó Mháirtín.</strong> — There is a letter here from Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Tá bainne ansin ón mbó.</strong> — There is milk there from the cow.</p><p>The preposition ó 'from' lenites a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>ó Mháirtín</strong> 'from Máirtín'. When used with the singular article it causes eclipsis to a following noun, e.g. <strong>ón mbó</strong> 'from the cow'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 208, grammar_rule_id: 99, label: "(ii) Prepositional Pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.330361Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.332808Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>uaim</td><td>from me /wem'/</td></tr><tr><td>uait</td><td>from you /wet'/</td></tr><tr><td>uaidh</td><td>from him/it /wai/</td></tr><tr><td>uaithi</td><td>from her/it /wo:/</td></tr><tr><td>uainn</td><td>from us /weN'/</td></tr><tr><td>uaibh</td><td>from you (pl.) /web'/</td></tr><tr><td>uaithu</td><td>from them /wo:b'/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as those of ag and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. uaim, uaimse, uaim féin, uaimse mé féin; see Lesson 16.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 209, grammar_rule_id: 99, label: "(iii) Meanings of ó", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.333433Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:42:40.336449Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Basic meaning 'from, since':</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ar an mbealach ar ais ón bposta.</strong> — Cáit is on the way back from the post office.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit anseo ón Nollaig.</strong> — Cáit is/has been here since Christmas.</p><p>More idiomatically ó is used:</p><p>(a) to express desire</p><p><strong>Níl uaidh ach spóirt.</strong> — He only wants fun.</p><p>(b) as a conjunction, to express 'from the time, since'</p><p><strong>Níl Cáit go maith ó bhí sí tinn.</strong> — Cáit is not well since she was sick.</p><p>(c) in some common adverbial phrases</p><p><strong>ó dhúchas</strong> — Tá Fraincis ó dhúchas ag Cáit, Cáit has French as a mother tongue.</p><p><strong>ó bhroinn</strong> — Tá marc ó bhroinn ar Cháit, Cáit has a mark from birth.</p><p><strong>ó rath</strong> — Tá na fataí ó rath. The potatoes are ruined.</p><p><strong>ó shin</strong> — Tá Cáit anseo ó shin. Cáit is here since.</p><p><strong>ó mhaidin</strong> — Tá Cáit anseo ó mhaidin. Cáit is here since morning.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 210, grammar_rule_id: 101, label: "(i) Form used with noun or pronoun (corresponding to bíonn)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.796953Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.800447Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glanann Cáit an bord agus níonn sí na soithí</strong> — Cáit clears the table and she washes the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léann Cáit an leabhar ach ní bhriseann sí an chathaoir.</strong> — Cáit reads the book but she does not break the chair.</p><p><strong>Coinníonn sé an cóta agus salann sé é.</strong> — He keeps the coat and he dirties it.</p><p><strong>Osclaíonn tusa an geata agus imríonn muide cluichí.</strong> — You open the gate and we play games.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>adds -(e)ann, -onn /ƏN/</td><td>adds -(a)íonn /Í:N/</td></tr><tr><td>glan + ann = glanann</td><td>sal(aigh) + aíonn = salann</td></tr><tr><td>bris + eann = briseann</td><td>coinn(igh) + íonn = coinníonn</td></tr><tr><td>lé(igh) + eann = léann</td><td>osc(ail) + aíonn = osclaíonn</td></tr><tr><td>ní(gh) + onn = níonn</td><td>im(ir) + íonn = imríonn</td></tr></tbody></table><p>After a long vowel gh is dropped; after a short vowel gh is dropped before an ending with an initial vowel; and the syllable is lengthened; see Lesson 12. After í, the ending is spelt -onn, cf. bí + onn: bíonn. All verbal endings are spelt with an initial 'broad' vowel, e.g. -ann, -aíonn, after a broad consonant, e.g. glan-, sal-, oscl-, and with an initial 'slender' vowel, e.g. -eann, -íonn, after a slender consonant, e.g. bris-, coinn-, imr-; see Lesson 12.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 211, grammar_rule_id: 101, label: "(ii) Combined form (corresponding to bím)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.801430Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.809317Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(a)im /mə '/</td><td>adds -(a)im /i:m'/</td></tr><tr><td>glanaim — I clean, clear</td><td>salaím — I dirty</td></tr><tr><td>brisim — I break</td><td>coinním — I keep</td></tr><tr><td>léim — I read</td><td>osclaím — I open</td></tr><tr><td>níom — I wash</td><td>imrím — I play</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 212, grammar_rule_id: 101, label: "(iii) Autonomous form (corresponding to bítear)", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.810177Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.813507Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -t(e)ar /trə/, /t'rə /</td><td>adds -(a)ítear /i: t'rə /</td></tr><tr><td>glantar — one cleans, clears</td><td>salaítear — one dirties</td></tr><tr><td>bristear — one breaks</td><td>coinnítear — one keeps</td></tr><tr><td>léitear — one reads</td><td>osclaítear — one opens</td></tr><tr><td>nítear — one washes</td><td>imrítear — one plays</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Before an ending with an initial consonant a short syllable can optionally remain short, e.g. nitear. The last syllable is always lost before an ending with an initial vowel; see Lesson 12.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 213, grammar_rule_id: 101, label: "(iv) Direct relative", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.814255Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.816859Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>S is added to any non-combined form (see Lesson 13): <strong>a ghlananns</strong> 'that cleans', <strong>a shalaionns</strong> 'that dirties', etc.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 214, grammar_rule_id: 102, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.821572Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.824137Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá muid ag tabhairt airgead do Cháit.</strong> — We are giving money to Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá muid ag tabhairt airgead don bhean eile.</strong> — We are giving money to the other woman.</p><p>Do /gə/ causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. do Cháit 'to Cáit'. Before a vowel it is written d', e.g. d'Úna 'to Úna'. When used with the singular article it is written as don /gNə / and a following noun is lenited, e.g. don bhean 'to the woman'.</p><p class=\"note\">As normal after a preposition, the singular article prefixes t to feminine nouns beginning with s and followed by a vowel or l, r, n, e.g. don tsúil 'to the eye'; see Lesson 8. After don, t may be optionally prefixed to similar masculine nouns, e.g. don tsagart 'to the priest'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 215, grammar_rule_id: 102, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.824819Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.827643Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>dhom</td><td>to me /yum/</td></tr><tr><td>dhuit</td><td>to you /yit'/</td></tr><tr><td>dhó</td><td>to him/it /yo:/</td></tr><tr><td>dhi</td><td>to her/it /y'i/, /yi:/</td></tr><tr><td>dhúinn</td><td>to us /yu:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>dhaoibh</td><td>to you (pl.) /yi:b'/</td></tr><tr><td>dhóibh</td><td>to them /yo:b'/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as those of ag and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. dhom, dhomsa, dhom féin, dhomsa mé féin; see Lesson 16. Often the initial dh is dropped in pronunciation, e.g. *om /um/, *uit /it'/, *ó /o:/, and less frequently it is unlenited, e.g. dom /dum/, duit /dit'/, dó /do:/. We have already seen in Lesson 15 how do is used with mo, do, etc. (do mo, do do, dhó, dho'ur).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 216, grammar_rule_id: 102, label: "(iii) Meaning of do", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.828285Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.830279Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Basic meaning 'to' or 'for'</p><p><strong>Tá mé ag tabhairt leabhar do Cháit.</strong> — I am giving a book to Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá mé ag scríobh litir do Cháit.</strong> — I am writing a letter for Cáit.</p><p>Do is used in a giving or dative sense; ag (see Lesson 16) is in most cases used of 'direction to' or 'movement to', e.g. <strong>Tá mé ag scríobh litir ag Cáit</strong> 'I am writing a letter to Cáit.' Exceptionally do expresses motion with certain place-names which are used with the article, e.g. don Lochán Bheag 'to Lochán Beag'.</p><p>More idiomatically do is used in:</p><p>(a) Thar éis do:</p><p><strong>Thar éis do na daoine imeacht, ghlan Cáit an bord.</strong> — After the people had gone, Cáit cleared the table.</p><p>(b) Hóbair do:</p><p><strong>Hóbair do Cháit titim.</strong> — Cáit almost fell.</p><p>In both these idioms the subject, e.g. na daoine, Cáit, follows do and a verbal noun is used.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 217, grammar_rule_id: 103, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.837450Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:43:27.840120Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is fiú dhuit imeacht.</strong> — It is worth it for you to go off.</p><p><strong>Ní gá dhuit fanacht.</strong> — It is not necessary for you to stay.</p><p>The copula (see Lesson 11) is used with do in certain idiomatic expressions. Further examples are:</p><p><strong>Is breá /b'r' / do Cháit...</strong> — It is fine for Cáit</p><p><strong>Ní gá /g a:/</strong> — It isn't necessary</p><p><strong>Ar leor /L'o:r/</strong> — Is it enough</p><p><strong>Nach furasta /frustə/</strong> — Isn't it easy</p><p><strong>Deir sé gur fearr /f' :r/</strong> — He says that it is better</p><p><strong>Deir sé nach ceart /k'æ:rt/</strong> — He says that it isn't right</p><p><strong>Más fíor /f'i:rə /</strong> — If it is true</p><p><strong>Mara cuma /kumə/</strong> — If it is not all the same</p><p>There are some idioms which are only used in a negative, e.g. <strong>ní foláir /f'l'ctr/ do Cháit imeacht</strong> 'Cáit must go off.</p><p>Some of these idioms can be used impersonally (with no prepositional pronoun), e.g. <strong>ní gá imeacht</strong> 'it is not necessary to go off', <strong>is fiú a theacht</strong> 'it is worth coming', <strong>is leor sin</strong> 'that is enough', <strong>is fíor sin</strong> 'it is true'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 218, grammar_rule_id: 104, label: "(i) Form used with noun or pronoun (corresponding to bhíodh)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.182980Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.186286Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ghlanadh Cáit an bord agus níodh sí na soithí.</strong> — Cáit used to clean the table and she used to wash the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léadh Cáit an leabhar agus ní bhriseadh sí an chathaoir.</strong> — Cáit used to read the book and she used not to break the chair.</p><p><strong>Choinníodh sé an cóta agus shalaíodh sé é.</strong> — He used to keep the coat and he used to dirty it.</p><p><strong>D'osclaíodh sibhse an geata ach ní imríodh muide cluichí.</strong> — You (pl.) used to open the gate but we used not to play games.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>is lenited and adds -(e)adh /xə/</td><td>is lenited and adds -(a)iodh /i:x/</td></tr><tr><td>glan + adh → ghlanadh</td><td>sal(aigh) + aiodh → shalaíodh</td></tr><tr><td>bris + eadh → bhriseadh</td><td>coinn(igh) + iodh → choinníodh</td></tr><tr><td>lé(igh) + adh → léadh</td><td>osc(ai)l + aiodh → d'osclaíodh</td></tr><tr><td>ní(gh) + odh → níodh</td><td>im(i)r + iodh → d'imríodh</td></tr><tr><td>fliuch + adh → d'fhliuchadh</td></tr></tbody></table><p>After a long vowel gh is dropped; after a short vowel gh is dropped before an ending with an initial vowel; and the syllable is lengthened; see Lesson 12. After i, the ending is spelt -odh, cf. bhíodh.</p><p>The basic form is always lenited, e.g. <strong>ghlanadh sé</strong> 'he used to clean'; where a verb has an initial vowel or f, d' is prefixed, e.g. <strong>d'osclaíodh sé</strong> 'he used to open', <strong>d'fhliuchadh sé</strong> 'he used to wet'. The d' is normally pronounced broad (/d/) or slender (/d'/) according to the following vowel.</p><p>Concerning the pronunciation of final dh, see Lesson 7.</p><p>All verbal endings are spelt with an initial 'broad' vowel, e.g. -adh, -aiodh, after a broad consonant, e.g. glan, fliuch, sal-, oscl-, and with an initial 'slender' vowel, e.g. -eadh, -iodh, after a slender consonant, e.g. bris, coinn, imr-; see Lesson 12.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 219, grammar_rule_id: 104, label: "(ii) Combined forms (corresponding to bhinn, bhiteá, bhidis)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.189850Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.193399Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) First person singular</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(a)inn /əN'/</td><td>adds -(a)inn /i:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanainn — I used to clean</td><td>shalainn — I used to dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisinn — I used to break</td><td>choinninn — I used to keep</td></tr><tr><td>léinn — I used to read</td><td>d'osclainn — I used to open</td></tr><tr><td>níonn — I used to wash</td><td>d'imrinn — I used to play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchainn — I used to wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p>(b) Second person singular</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -t(e)á /t:/, /t'a:/</td><td>adds -(a)íteá /i:t'a:/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlantá — you used to clean</td><td>shalaíteá — you used to dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhristeá — you used to break</td><td>choinníteá — you used to keep</td></tr><tr><td>léiteá — you used to read</td><td>d'osclaíteá — you used to open</td></tr><tr><td>níteá — you used to wash</td><td>d'imriteá — you used to play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchtá — you used to wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p>(c) Third person plural</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(a)idís /əd'i:s'/</td><td>adds -(a)ídís /i:d'i:s'/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanaidís — they used to clean</td><td>shalaídís — they used to dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhrisidís — they used to break</td><td>choinnídís — they used to keep</td></tr><tr><td>léidís — they used to read</td><td>d'osclaídís — they used to open</td></tr><tr><td>nídís — they used to wash</td><td>d'imrídís — they used to play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchaidís — they used to wet</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 220, grammar_rule_id: 104, label: "(iii) Autonomous form", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.194231Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.196393Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -t(a)í /ti:/, /t'i:/</td><td>adds -(a)ítí /i:t'i:/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlantaí — one used to clean</td><td>shalaítí — one used to dirty</td></tr><tr><td>bhristí — one used to break</td><td>choinnítí — one used to keep</td></tr><tr><td>léití — one used to read</td><td>d'osclaítí — one used to open</td></tr><tr><td>nítí — one used to wash</td><td>d'imrítí — one used to play</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchtaí — one used to wet</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Before an ending pronounced with an initial consonant a short syllable can remain, e.g. <strong>nítí</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 221, grammar_rule_id: 105, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.201899Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.204056Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bain an caipín de Máirtín.</strong> — Take the cap off Máirtín!</p><p><strong>Tóig do chois den chathaoir.</strong> — Lift your leg off the chair!</p><p>De /gə/ causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>de Cháit</strong> 'off Cáit'. Before a vowel it is written <strong>d'</strong>, e.g. <strong>d'Úna</strong> 'off Úna'. When used with the definite article it is written as <strong>den</strong> /gNə/ and a following noun is lenited, e.g. <strong>den chathaoir</strong> 'off the chair'.</p><p>As normal after a preposition, the singular article prefixes t to feminine nouns beginning with s and followed by a vowel or l, r, n, e.g. <strong>den tsráid</strong> 'off the street'; see Lesson 8. After <strong>den</strong> (or <strong>don</strong>, see Lesson 23) t may be optionally prefixed to similar masculine nouns, e.g. <strong>den tsagart</strong> 'off the priest'.</p><p class=\"note\">Pronounced as though spelt go. (In pronunciation, before nouns, de is exactly like do.)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 222, grammar_rule_id: 105, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.205356Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.207433Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>dhiom</td><td>off me /y'i:m/</td></tr><tr><td>dhiot</td><td>off you /y'i:t/</td></tr><tr><td>dhe</td><td>off him/it /y'e/</td></tr><tr><td>dhi</td><td>off her/it /y'i/</td></tr><tr><td>dhinn</td><td>off us /y'i:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>dhíbh</td><td>off you (pl.) /y'i:b'/</td></tr><tr><td>dhiobh</td><td>off them /y'i:b'/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as those of <strong>ag</strong> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>dhiom, dhíomsa, dhíom féin, dhíomsa mé féin</strong>; see Lesson 16.</p><p>Similarly to <strong>do</strong> (see Lesson 23) the initial dh is often dropped in pronunciation, e.g. <strong>'iom</strong> /i:m/, <strong>'iot</strong> /i:t'/, and less frequently it is unlenited, e.g. <strong>díom</strong> /d'i:m/, <strong>díot</strong> /d'i:t/. On account of its general similarity with the preposition <strong>do</strong>, the third person pronouns may often be replaced by those of <strong>do</strong>, e.g. <strong>dho</strong> for <strong>dhe</strong>.</p><p><strong>Do</strong> is used with <strong>mo, do</strong>, etc. in a similar way to <strong>do</strong>, e.g. <strong>de mo</strong> 'off my', <strong>de do</strong> 'off your', <strong>dhó</strong> 'off his, her, its, their', <strong>dhár</strong> /ya:/ 'off our', <strong>dhe 'ur</strong> /ya:/ 'off your (pl.)'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 223, grammar_rule_id: 105, label: "(iii) Basic meaning of de 'off'", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.208791Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.210505Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an cóta ag titim den chathaoir.</strong> — The coat is falling off the chair.</p><p><strong>Bain dhiot do chóta.</strong> — Take off your coat!</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 224, grammar_rule_id: 105, label: "(iv) Secondary meaning of de 'of'", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.211177Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.214228Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá cuid de na daoine sásta.</strong> — Some of the people are content.</p><p>After certain words used partitively <strong>de</strong> corresponds to 'of', e.g. <strong>cuid</strong> den bhalla 'a part of the wall', <strong>píosa de lá</strong> 'a portion (bit) of a day', <strong>go leor de na rudaí</strong> 'a lot of the things', <strong>ceachtar den bheirt</strong> 'either of the two people', <strong>níos mó den am</strong> 'more of the time'.</p><p>Somewhat similarly: <strong>buille de mhaide</strong> 'a blow of a stick', <strong>pictiúr de dhuine</strong> 'a picture of a person', <strong>a leithéide de dhuine</strong> 'his type of a person, such a person'.</p><p>Ag is usually used instead of <strong>de</strong> to refer to the plural, e.g. <strong>go leor dhe</strong> 'a lot of it', but <strong>go leor acu</strong> 'a lot of them'; see Lesson 16.</p><p>(b) <strong>Níl ann ach amadán de dhuine.</strong> — He is a fool-like person. (lit. a fool of a person).</p><p>Similarly: <strong>giinn d'fhear</strong> 'a wedge of a man, i.e. a stocky, well-built man', <strong>gliogar de chathaoir</strong> 'a crock of a chair, i.e. a rickety, unstable chair'.</p><p>(c) <strong>Níl sé d'am agam caint léi.</strong> — I have not (the amount of) time (required) to talk to her.</p><p><strong>De</strong> is used with certain abstract nouns to express 'the required amount of, the necessary, by way of'. Similarly: <strong>d'fhoighid</strong> 'the necessary patience', <strong>de spás</strong> 'the necessary period of time', <strong>d'ádh</strong> 'the necessary luck'.</p><p>(d) <strong>Tá mé tuirseach den chaint.</strong> — I am tired of the talk.</p><p><strong>De</strong> corresponds to 'of' after certain adjectives. Similarly: <strong>bréan de</strong> 'fed up', <strong>cinnte de</strong> 'certain of', <strong>lán de</strong> 'full of'.</p><p><strong>Tá siad ag déanamh amadán de Mháirtín.</strong> — They are making a fool of Máirtín.</p><p><strong>De</strong> corresponds to 'of' after certain verbs. Similarly: <strong>Síleann siad a domhan de Mháirtín</strong> 'they think the world of Máirtín'.</p><p>(f) <strong>Bhí sé ag goil ag pósadh de léim.</strong> — He was going to marry suddenly. (lit. 'of a jump').</p><p><strong>De</strong> is used in some adverbial phrases to express a sudden movement or sound. Similarly: <strong>de phlump</strong> 'with a bang', <strong>d'iarraidh amháin</strong> 'in one go'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 225, grammar_rule_id: 106, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.222069Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:44:15.224565Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Number</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>1 a haon</td><td>/hi:N/</td></tr><tr><td>2 a dó</td><td>/do:/</td></tr><tr><td>3 a trí</td><td>/t'r'i:/</td></tr><tr><td>4 a ceathair</td><td>/k'æ:r'/</td></tr><tr><td>5 a cúig</td><td>/ku:g'/</td></tr><tr><td>6 a sé</td><td>/s'e:/</td></tr><tr><td>7 a seacht</td><td>/s'æ:xt/</td></tr><tr><td>8 a hocht</td><td>/hoxt/</td></tr><tr><td>9 a naoi</td><td>/Ni:/</td></tr><tr><td>10 a deich</td><td>/d'e/</td></tr><tr><td>11 a haon déag</td><td>/hi:N'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>12 a dó dhéag</td><td>/do:y'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>13 a trí déag</td><td>/t'r'i: d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>14 a ceathair déag</td><td>/k'æ:r d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>15 a cúig déag</td><td>/ku:g' d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>16 a sé déag</td><td>/s'e: d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>17 a seacht déag</td><td>/s'æ:x d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>18 a hocht déag</td><td>/hox d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>19 a naoi déag</td><td>/Ni: d'e:g/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These numbers are written with a /ə/ (which prefixes h to vowels), although a is not normally pronounced, except between two consonants.</p><p>Usage:</p><p>(a) For counting, e.g. <strong>a haon, a dó, a trí...</strong> 'one, two, three ...', or where numbers are used abstractly, e.g. <strong>a dó agus a dó, sin a ceathair</strong> 'two and two are four', or in a telephone number.</p><p><strong>Dhó</strong> /yo:/ is used in expressions of the sort <strong>leabhar nó dhó</strong> 'a book or two', <strong>bord nó dhó</strong> 'a table or two'.</p><p>(b) With <strong>de</strong> /gə/, often reduced to /</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 226, grammar_rule_id: 107, label: "(i) Form used with noun or pronoun (corresponding to bhí)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.579104Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.582630Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ghlan Cáit an bord agus nigh sí na soithí</strong> — Cáit cleaned the table and she washed the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léigh Cáit an leabhar agus bhris sí an chathaoir.</strong> — Cáit read the book and she broke the chair.</p><p><strong>Choinnigh siadsan an cóta agus shalaigh seisean é.</strong> — They kept the coat and he dirtied it.</p><p><strong>D'oscail tusa an geata agus d'imir muide cluichí.</strong> — You opened the gate and we played games.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>is lenited</td><td>is lenited</td></tr><tr><td>ghlan cleaned</td><td>shalaigh dirtied</td></tr><tr><td>bhris broke</td><td>choinnigh kept</td></tr><tr><td>léigh read</td><td>d'oscail opened</td></tr><tr><td>nigh washed</td><td>d'imir played</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuch wet</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Where the verb has an initial vowel or 'f', d' is prefixed, e.g. <strong>d'oscail sé</strong> — he opened, <strong>d'fhliuch sé</strong> — he wet. The 'd' is normally pronounced broad (/d/) or slender (/d'/) according to the following vowel.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 227, grammar_rule_id: 107, label: "(ii) Optional combined forms (corresponding to bhíodar)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.583509Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.586395Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(a)dár, -odar /də rə/</td><td>adds -(a)iodar /i:drə/</td></tr><tr><td>ghlanadar they cleaned</td><td>shalaiodar they dirtied</td></tr><tr><td>bhriseadar they broke</td><td>choinniodar they kept</td></tr><tr><td>léadar they read</td><td>d'osclaiodar they opened</td></tr><tr><td>níodar they washed</td><td>d'imriodar they played</td></tr><tr><td>d'fhliuchadar they wet</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">After a long vowel gh is dropped; The last syllable is always lost after a short vowel gh is dropped before an ending with an initial vowel; and the syllable is lengthened; see Lesson 12. After í, the ending is spelt -odar, cf. bhíodar, see Lesson 7. For the lenition of l, n, see Lesson 1, phonetic exercise 1 (c).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 228, grammar_rule_id: 107, label: "(iii) Autonomous form (corresponding to bhíothadh)", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.587181Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.589324Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(e)adh /u:/</td><td>adds -(a)íodh /i:w/</td></tr><tr><td>glanadh one cleaned</td><td>salaiodh one dirtied</td></tr><tr><td>briseadh one broke</td><td>coinniodh one kept</td></tr><tr><td>léadh one read</td><td>hosclaiodh one opened</td></tr><tr><td>níodh one washed</td><td>himriodh one played</td></tr><tr><td>fliuchadh one wet</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">After í, the ending is spelt -odh. The past tense autonomous form is never lenited (even after particles) and no d' is prefixed. Where the verb has an initial vowel, h is prefixed (even after particles).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 229, grammar_rule_id: 107, label: "(iv) Use of particles", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.590501Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.592566Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>A regular verb in the past uses nior /N'i:r/ (in place of ní), ar /rə/ (in place of an), nar /Na:r/ (in place of nach), gur /grə/ (in place of go), marar /ma:rrə/ (in place of mara), and ar /rə/ (in place of the indirect relative particle a). All of these particles cause lenition as shown in this table:</p><table><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Nior</td><td><strong>Nior ghlan Cáit an geata.</strong> — Cáit did not clean the gate.</td></tr><tr><td>Ar</td><td><strong>Ar oscail sí an geata?</strong> — Did she open the gate?</td></tr><tr><td>Nar</td><td><strong>Nar ghlan sí an geata?</strong> — Did she not clean the gate?</td></tr><tr><td>Gur</td><td><strong>Deir sé gur oscail sí an geata.</strong> — He says that she opened the gate.</td></tr><tr><td>Marar</td><td><strong>Marar ghlan sí an geata.</strong> — If she did not clean the gate.</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">As pointed out in (iii) above the autonomous form is not lenited after particles, e.g. nior glanadh, ar glanadh?, and h is prefixed to a vowel irrespective of particles, e.g. hosclaiodh, nior hosclaiodh, ar hosclaiodh?</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 230, grammar_rule_id: 107, label: "(v) Meaning of past tense", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.593383Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.594809Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Léigh sé an leabhar.</strong> — He (had) read the book.</p><p><strong>Léigh sé an leabhar an lá sin.</strong> — He (had) read the book that day.</p><p>Generally in Irish no distinction is made between a preterite, e.g. 'he read', a perfect, e.g. 'he has read', and a pluperfect, e.g. 'he had read'. (The verb tá is slightly exceptional, see Lesson 6).</p><p class=\"note\">Nár /Na:r/, which is the Official Standard Irish form, is also used, though, it would seem, less frequently.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 231, grammar_rule_id: 108, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.605350Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.607460Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá na leabhartha i mbosca.</strong> — The books are in a box.</p><p><strong>Tá na leabhartha sa mbosca.</strong> — The books are in the box.</p><p>The preposition i/ə/ causes eclipsis to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>i mbosca</strong> — 'in a box'. Before a vowel it is written in, e.g. <strong>in Éirinn</strong> — 'in Ireland'. When used with the article it combines with it and is <strong>sa</strong> /sə/ before a consonant, and <strong>san</strong> /SNƏ/ before a vowel. Sa causes eclipsis to a following noun, e.g. <strong>sa mbosca</strong> — 'in the box'. <strong>Sna</strong> /SNƏ/, causing no change, is used in the plural.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 232, grammar_rule_id: 108, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.608235Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.610342Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>ionam</td><td>in me /uNmə/</td></tr><tr><td>ionat</td><td>in you /uNdə/</td></tr><tr><td>ann</td><td>in him/it /a:N/</td></tr><tr><td>inti</td><td>in her/it /i:N't'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>ionainn</td><td>in us /UNNƏ'/</td></tr><tr><td>ionaibh</td><td>in you (pl.) /UNÍ:/</td></tr><tr><td>iontu</td><td>in them /uNtbə/</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">The contrast forms and general usage are the same as those of ag and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. ionam, ionamsa, ionam féin, ionamsa mé féin; see Lesson 16.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 233, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(i) Basic meaning 'in'", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.613128Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.614716Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá uisce sa bpota.</strong> — There is water in the pot.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 234, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(ii) to express 'to' (an indefinite place)", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.617037Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.618667Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag goil in áit éicint.</strong> — Cáit is going somewhere.</p><p>Somewhat similarly: <strong>ag goil sna sagairt</strong> — 'going to (join) the priests, to become a priest'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 235, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(iii) to express possession of a quality or power", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.619263Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.620792Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá sláinte sa ngrian.</strong> — The sun has a healthgiving quality. ('There is health in the sun.').</p><p><strong>Níl maith ar bith sa leabhar seo.</strong> — This book is no good. ('There is no good in this book.')</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 236, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(iv) in some common adverbial phrases", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.621380Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.623387Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>in ann</strong> /ƏN a: N/ — able, capable of</p><p><strong>in am</strong> /ƏN :m/ — in time</p><p><strong>i bhfad</strong> /ə wa:dJ — far, a long way</p><p><strong>i gcónaí</strong> /gə u:Ni:/ — always</p><p><strong>i bhfolach</strong> /ə wa:lxə/ — in hiding, hidden</p><p><strong>i gceist</strong> /ə g'es't'/ — in question, under consideration</p><p><strong>i ngrá</strong> /ə rjra/ — in love</p><p><strong>inéindí</strong> /Nə e:N'd'i:/ — together, suddenly</p><p><strong>in aonturas</strong> /ƏN e: Ntorsə/ — on purpose</p><p><strong>in áirde</strong> /ƏN a:rd'ə/ — (high) up</p><p><strong>in uachtar</strong> /əN u:xə trə/ — on top</p><p><strong>in íochtar</strong> /ƏN 'i: xə trə/ — at the bottom</p><p><strong>i dteannta</strong> /ə d' a :Ntə/ — in a fix/dilemma</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 237, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(v) with possessive pronouns (mo, do, etc.) to express a state", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.623972Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.625702Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an seomra ina phraiseach.</strong> — The room is in a mess. (lit. 'in its mess')</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ina múinteoir anois.</strong> — Cáit is a teacher now. (lit. 'in her teacher')</p><p><strong>Tá sé ina shamhradh.</strong> — It is summer; summer has arrived. (lit. 'in its summer')</p><p>This idiom, at least to a certain extent, emphasised the state of having become something; 'I am a teacher' can be expressed by <strong>Is múinteoir mé</strong> (see Lesson 11) or by <strong>Tá mé i mo mhúinteoir</strong>. On the other hand, 'I am an Irishman' is normally <strong>Is Éireannach mé</strong>, as this is felt as a permanent state.</p><p><strong>Tá sé ina mhúinteoir mhaith</strong> — 'he is a good teacher'; in this construction after the third person singular masculine an adjective may be lenited after a masculine noun.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 238, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(vi) an alternative way of expressing this idiom", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.626275Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.627982Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Múinteoir atá ionam.</strong> — I am a teacher. (lit. 'it is a teacher that is in me')</p><p>In this construction <strong>in</strong> (causing no change) may be used with personal names, e.g. <strong>Múinteoir atá in Bríd</strong> — 'Bríd is a teacher'. Also <strong>ansin</strong> is used, e.g. <strong>Siúinéara atá ansin</strong> — 'that fellow is a carpenter', rather than <strong>ann sin</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 239, grammar_rule_id: 109, label: "(vii) with possessive pronouns, instead of ag before a certain few verbal nouns to describe a state", position: 7, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.628665Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:04.630385Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá mé i mo chodladh.</strong> — I am sleeping/asleep, (lit. 'in my sleep')</p><p>Similarly: <strong>i mo dhúiseacht</strong> — 'awake', <strong>i mo sheasamh</strong> — 'standing', <strong>i mo luí</strong> — 'lying', <strong>i mo shuí</strong> — 'sitting up, out of bed', <strong>i mo chónaí</strong> — 'living, standing idle', <strong>i mo rith</strong> — 'running'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 240, grammar_rule_id: 110, label: "(i) Formation", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.577227Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.586424Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an bord glanta agus tá na soithí nite.</strong> — The table is cleared and the dishes are washed.</p><p><strong>Tá an leabhar léite agus tá an chathaoir briste.</strong> — The book is read and the chair is broken.</p><p><strong>Beidh an cóta coinnithe go maith agus ní bheidh sé salaithe.</strong> — The coat will be kept well and it will not be dirtied.</p><p><strong>Bhí an geata osclaithe agus an cluiche imrithe.</strong> — The gate was opened and the game played.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>(root with one syllable)</td><td>(root with two syllables)</td></tr><tr><td>adds -ta/-te /t/ə, /t'ə/</td><td>adds -(a)ithe /i:/</td></tr><tr><td>glanta cleaned</td><td>salaithe dirtied</td></tr><tr><td>briste broken</td><td>coinnithe kept</td></tr><tr><td>léite read</td><td>osclaithe opened</td></tr><tr><td>nite washed</td><td>imrithe played</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A final gh is dropped; Verbs with roots in l, n, r may alternatively add -te /t'ə/: oscailte, imirte. In this case the second syllable of the root is not dropped as the ending does not have an initial vowel; see Lesson 12.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -tha/-the /i:/ to roots ending in b, p, t, d, g, c, bh, mh</td></tr><tr><td>scuabtha swept</td></tr><tr><td>ceaptha thought</td></tr><tr><td>fágtha left</td></tr><tr><td>goite, goidthe stolen</td></tr><tr><td>scríobhtha written (now spelt scríofa)</td></tr><tr><td>snámhtha swum (now spelt snáfa)</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Lár is often dropped.</p><p>All verbal endings are spelt with a 'broad' vowel, e.g. -ta, -aithe, after a broad consonant, e.g. glan, sal-, oscl-, and with a 'slender' vowel, e.g. -te, -ithe, after a slender consonant, e.g. bris, coinn-, imr-; see Lesson 12.</p><p>Some type 1 verbs with roots ending in r, e.g. bearr 'shave', gearr 'cut', scar 'separate', comhair 'count', add -tha, -the /ə/, e.g. bearrtha 'shaved', gearrtha 'cut', scartha 'separated', comhairthe 'counted'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 241, grammar_rule_id: 110, label: "(ii) Pronunciation of verbs with roots ending in b, d, g, bh, mh", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.588222Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.591957Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>b scuab is pronounced p scuabtha</p><p>d goid t goite, goidthe</p><p>g fág c fágtha</p><p>bh scríobh f scríobhtha/scríofa</p><p>mh snámh f snámhtha/snámha</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 242, grammar_rule_id: 110, label: "(iii) Meaning of verbal adjective", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.593929Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.598305Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The verbal adjective describes a state resulting from an action.</p><p><strong>Tá an bord briste.</strong> — The table is (in the state of having been) broken.</p><p>Usually, it can be used attributively like any other adjective:</p><p><strong>Tá an bord briste thall ansin.</strong> — The broken table is over there.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 243, grammar_rule_id: 111, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.605769Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.607641Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an ghloine sobhriste.</strong> — The glass is easily breakable.</p><p><strong>Tá an obair sin dodhéanta.</strong> — That work is impossible to do.</p><p><strong>Níl sé sin inite.</strong> — That is not edible.</p><p>The prefixes so- 'easily possible', do- 'impossible' and in- 'possible' are prefixed to a form of the verb which is the same as the verbal adjective, e.g. briste, déanta, ite. So is pronounced /su/ before a broad consonant or vowel, and /si/ before a slender consonant. Similarly, do is pronounced /du/ or /di/. In is pronounced /Nə /. So- and do- are given primary stress while the verbal form takes the secondary stress, e.g. sobhriste /'si.w'r'is't'ə/ 'breakable', so-ólta /'su,o:Ltə/ 'easily drinkable', dobhriste /'di.w'r'is't'ə/ 'unbreakable', dothóigthe /'du,ho:k'i:/' impossible to raise'. In is not stressed, e.g. inite /Nə it'ə/ 'edible'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 244, grammar_rule_id: 112, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.610895Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.613268Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Irish</th><th>English</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>bád</td><td>boat</td></tr><tr><td>caiptín an bháid</td><td>the captain of the boat</td></tr><tr><td>Peadar</td><td>Peadar</td></tr><tr><td>cóta Pheadair</td><td>Peadar's coat</td></tr><tr><td>doras /o/</td><td>door</td></tr><tr><td>lámh an doiris</td><td>the door handle</td></tr><tr><td>éan</td><td>bird</td></tr><tr><td>nead an éin</td><td>the bird's nest</td></tr><tr><td>airgead</td><td>money</td></tr><tr><td>mála an airgid</td><td>the money bag</td></tr><tr><td>aonach</td><td>fair</td></tr><tr><td>lá an aonaigh</td><td>the fair day</td></tr><tr><td>cogadh</td><td>war</td></tr><tr><td>aimsir an chogaidh</td><td>the time of war</td></tr><tr><td>clog /o/</td><td>clock</td></tr><tr><td>éadan an chloig</td><td>the face of the clock</td></tr><tr><td>poll /au/</td><td>hole</td></tr><tr><td>béal an phoill /ai/</td><td>the mouth of the hole</td></tr><tr><td>cnoc /u/</td><td>hill</td></tr><tr><td>barr an chnoic</td><td>the top of the hill</td></tr><tr><td>arm /a:/</td><td>army</td></tr><tr><td>lucht an airm /æ:/</td><td>the people of the army</td></tr><tr><td>iasc /i:ə/</td><td>fish</td></tr><tr><td>mála an éisc /e:/</td><td>the fish bag</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In pronunciation all these forms are in accordance with the rules in Appendix I. Where the vowel changes, this is shown in the table above.</p><p>The majority of masculine nouns ending in a broad consonant are made slender in the genitive singular, e.g. bád - an bháid /bɑːd/ - /wɑːd'/ 'boat - of the boat'. In a few words, e.g. doras, arm, the medial consonant is also made slender, e.g. doras - an doiris /dɔrəs/ - /d'er'əs'/ 'door - of the door', arm - an airm /aːrmə/ - /æːr'əm'/ 'army - of the army'. When the final consonant is made slender, ia /iːə/ becomes éi /eː/, e.g. iasc - éisc /iːsk/ - /eːs'k'/ 'fish - of the fish'. A final (e)ach /əx/ becomes -(a)igh //ə, aonach - aonaigh /iːNxə/ - /iːNə/ 'fair-of the fair'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 245, grammar_rule_id: 113, label: "(i) Usage with definite noun", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.620929Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.622900Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Definite noun i.e. a noun following the article, or a possessive pronoun; or a proper noun</p><table><tr><th>noun</th><th>verbal noun</th><th>genitive preposition</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>ainm...</td><td>(ag) moladh...</td><td>ar shon ...</td></tr><tr><td>... na hoifige</td><td>... mo bháid</td><td>... Chait, Pheadair</td></tr><tr><td>... teach an bhúistéara</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>After a noun, verbal noun or genitive preposition, (a) a definite noun, e.g. an bhúistéara, mo bháid, Peadar, Cait, in the genitive, (b) all proper nouns, e.g. Peadar, Gaillimh, teach an bhúistéara, are lenited.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 246, grammar_rule_id: 113, label: "(ii) Usage with indefinite noun", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.623457Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.625525Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Indefinite noun</p><table><tr><th>noun</th><th>verbal noun</th><th>genitive preposition</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>ainm ...</td><td>(ag) moladh</td><td>ar shon ...</td></tr><tr><td>... gasúr</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Generally after a noun, verbal noun or genitive preposition, the nominative is used with an indefinite noun (one which neither comes after an, na; mo, do etc. nor is a proper noun). However, a genitive is used in certain set expressions:</p><p>(a) after a noun, e.g. éadach boird 'tablecloth', deoch phórtair 'a drink of stout';</p><p>(b) after a verbal noun, e.g. ag cur allais 'sweating', ag fáil bháis 'dying';</p><p>(c) after a preposition taking the genitive, e.g. ar feadh tamaill 'for a while'.</p><p class=\"note\">We have already seen examples of this genitive formation after Dé in Dé Domhnaigh (Domhnach /duːNəx/), Dé Luain (Luan /luːən/), Dé Sathairn (Satharn /saːrNə/). Less frequently Dé can be dropped and the article used with the days of the week, e.g. ar an Domhnach 'on Sunday(s)'; or in certain phrases without the article, e.g. ó Dhéardaoin go Domhnach 'from Thursday to Sunday'.</p><p class=\"note\">In certain cases, the use of the genitive is optional, particularly among younger speakers of the dialect. However, it is felt that the rules given here are a reasonable compromise.</p><p class=\"note\">Concerning the lenition see Lessons 9 and 15.</p><p class=\"note\">Particularly where the second noun is used as an adjective ('attributively') and the two nouns felt as one lexical unit.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 247, grammar_rule_id: 113, label: "(iii) Usage following chun or partitive words", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.626084Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.628659Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) <strong>Tá sé ag dul chun feabhais.</strong> — He is improving (lit. going towards excellence).</p><p>Chun /UN/ is generally followed by a genitive, e.g. nom. feabhas, gen. feabhais. Other examples are: chun báis 'to death', chun deiridh 'backwards', chun bealaigh 'away'.</p><p>(b) <strong>Tá neart airgid anseo.</strong> — There is plenty of money here.</p><p>Partitive nouns such as neart 'plenty of' (in statements), mórán 'much of' (in questions or negatives), go leor 'lots of', beagán 'little of', roinnt 'some', leath 'half', ceathrú 'quarter', cuid 'share/portion of etc., are normally followed by a genitive.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 248, grammar_rule_id: 114, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.631037Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:46:59.632520Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>barr an gheata bhuí</strong> — the top of the yellow gate</p><p><strong>muintir an bhaile mhóir</strong> — the people of the town</p><p><strong>ainm an leabhair bháin</strong> — the name of the white book</p><p>An adjective following a masculine noun in the genitive is lenited and in the case of adjectives having one syllable in spelling (and not ending in ll, nn, rr, m), the final consonant is made slender, e.g. mór 'big', ... an bhaile mhóir 'of the town'. Adjectives ending in -(e)ach /xə/ change to -(a)igh //ə, e.g. obair an duine bhradaigh 'the work of the dishonest person'. The following forms are somewhat exceptional: uasal 'noble', mac an duine uasail 'the gentleman's son'; beag 'little', mac Shéamais Bhig 'Séamas Beag's son'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 249, grammar_rule_id: 115, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.050446Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.053543Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an fear tinn ag an ngrian.</strong> — The man is sick as a result of the sun.</p><p><strong>Tá an bord scoilte ag an ngrian.</strong> — The table is split as a result of the sun.</p><p><strong>Tá an bord scoilte ag Máirtín.</strong> — The table is split as a result of M.'s action.</p><p><strong>Tá an bord briste agam.</strong> — The table is broken as a result of my action. (= I have the table broken.)</p><p><strong>Tá an teach dhá thógáil agam.</strong> — The house is being built as a result of my action. (= I am in the process of building the house.)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 250, grammar_rule_id: 116, label: "(i) Formation and examples", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.059518Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.062218Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Nominative</th><th>Genitive</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>cloch</td><td>ceathrú cloiche</td></tr><tr><td>sceach</td><td>bláth na sceiche</td></tr><tr><td>cuach</td><td>ubh na cuaiche</td></tr><tr><td>tuaith</td><td>muintir na tuaithe</td></tr><tr><td>uaigh</td><td>dúnadh na huaighe</td></tr><tr><td>cisteanach</td><td>bord na cisteanaí</td></tr><tr><td>baintreach</td><td>mac na baintrí</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">All these forms are pronounced regularly according to the table in Appendix I. In nouns of more than one syllable, a final -(e)ach /xə/ is replaced by -(a)í /i:/, e.g. cisteanach 'kitchen', bord na cisteanaí 'the kitchen table'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 251, grammar_rule_id: 116, label: "(ii) Slender consonant with -e /ə/ and irregular vowel change", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.062956Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.065879Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The genitive of some few feminine nouns is formed by making the final consonant slender, by adding -e /ə/ and by changing the spelling from:</p><p>(a) ea to i</p><p><strong>cearc</strong> — ubh na circe</p><p>(b) ea to e</p><p><strong>fearg</strong> — le teann feirge</p><p>Similar to fearg are: nead 'nest', ubh neide 'nest egg'; leac 'flat stone, slab', faoi bhun na leice 'underneath the slab'; speal 'scythe', cois na speile 'the scythe handle'.</p><p>(c) a to o</p><p><strong>slat</strong> — lúbadh na sloite</p><p><strong>clann</strong> — ag iompar cloinne</p><p>Similar to slat is: glac 'a fist(ful)', as cúl mo ghloice (lit. out of the back of my fist), 'out of my hand'. Note also the noun scian 'knife', cois na seine 'the knife handle', which is irregular.</p><p class=\"note\">All these forms are pronounced regularly according to the table in Appendix I.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 252, grammar_rule_id: 117, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.072543Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.074412Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Often when an adjective follows a feminine noun the use of the genitive is avoided or ignored:</p><p><strong>barr an fhuinneoig mhór</strong> — the top of the big window</p><p>In certain set phrases (and normally in writing) the noun and adjective are in the genitive. The form of the adjective is similar to the comparative:</p><p><strong>oíche na gaoithe móire</strong> — the night of the big wind</p><p><strong>le haghaidh na coise tinne</strong> (lit. for the sore leg), for a rainy day, for an emergency</p><p><strong>ar thaobh na láimhe deise</strong> — on the right-hand side</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 253, grammar_rule_id: 118, label: "(i) Formation and examples", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.078107Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.081294Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>moch</td><td>níos/is moiche</td></tr><tr><td>fliuch</td><td>níos fliche</td></tr><tr><td>buíoch</td><td>buíche</td></tr><tr><td>aisteach</td><td>níos aistí</td></tr><tr><td>faillíoch</td><td>níos faillí</td></tr><tr><td>seasmhach</td><td>seasmhaí</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">All these forms are pronounced regularly according to the table in Appendix I. In adjectives of more than one syllable, final -(e)ach, -(a)íoch /(i:)xə/ is replaced by ~(a)í /i:/, e.g. aisteach 'peculiar', níos aistí 'more peculiar'.</p><p>Salach 'dirty' and amplach 'greedy' are somewhat irregular: níos soilche /s'el'i:/, níos ampliaí /a:mpLi:/.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 254, grammar_rule_id: 118, label: "(ii) Special comparative forms with irregular vowel change", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.081894Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:49:49.084173Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The comparative forms of a limited number of adjectives is formed by making the final consonant slender, by adding -e /n/, and by changing the spelling from:</p><p>(a) ea to i</p><p><strong>sean</strong> — níos sine</p><p><strong>gearr</strong> — níos giorra</p><p>Similar to sean is geal 'bright', níos gile 'brighter'.</p><p>(b) ea to ei</p><p><strong>deas</strong> — níos deise</p><p><strong>leathan</strong> — níos leithne</p><p>Similar to deas are: ceart 'right', níos ceirte 'lighter'; dearg 'red', níos deirge 'redder'; searbh 'sour', níos seirbhe 'sourer'.</p><p>(c) a to o</p><p><strong>lag</strong> — níos laige</p><p><strong>glan</strong> — níos glaine</p><p><strong>mall</strong> — níos moille</p><p><strong>gann</strong> — níos gainne</p><p>Similar to lag are: gar 'near', níos gaire 'nearer'; glas 'green', níos glaise 'greener'; garbh 'rough', níos gairbhe 'rougher'; dall 'blind', níos doille 'blinder'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 255, grammar_rule_id: 119, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.789946Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.795207Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Ba mé/mise</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Ní ba thú/thusa</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>An</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Nach 'eo/'in/'iúd é an dochtúr</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé go</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>nach mba mhaith leis</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Dá chuma leis</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Mara etc.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Má ba</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table><p>It will be noticed that the verbal particles ní, an, go etc. used with ba are those used before the conditional of any verb. Ba, however, is not lenited.</p><p>The Official Standard Irish forms are:</p><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>níor</td><td>/N'i:r/ (+lenition)</td></tr><tr><td>ar</td><td>/rə/ an mba</td></tr><tr><td>nár</td><td>/Na:r/ nach mba</td></tr><tr><td>gur</td><td>/grə/ go mba</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Although these forms do occur in the dialect, they are, with the exception of nár, probably less frequent than those in the table above.</p><p>Ba generally causes lenition, e.g. <strong>ba chuma le...</strong> 'it would be all the same for ...'. A following d, t, g, or s, however, is very often not lenited, e.g. <strong>ba dochtúr é</strong> 'he was a doctor'.</p><p>There is a strong tendency for a dh /y/ to be pronounced before é/eisean, í/ise, iad/iadsan, ea, and before 'eo, 'in, 'iúd, e.g. <strong>ba é sin an dochtúr</strong> /bəy 'e: .../ 'that was the doctor'.</p><p>Dá mba is used in the conditional, e.g. <strong>dá mba chuma dhuit...</strong> 'if you should ...', but má ba is used in the past, e.g. <strong>má ba chuma leat...</strong> 'if it was all the same to you ...'.</p><p class=\"note\">1. Nár /Na:r/ rather than nár /Na:r/ in this dialect.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 256, grammar_rule_id: 120, label: "(i) Direct relative", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.801905Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.805341Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Positive</p><p><strong>Sin é an rud ba cheart a dhéanamh.</strong> That is the thing which should be done / should have been done.</p><p>The positive direct relative is the same as the statement form, e.g. <strong>... ba cheart ...</strong> '... that should ...'.</p><p>(b) Negative</p><p><strong>Sin é an rud nach mba cheart a dhéanamh.</strong> That is the thing which should not be done / should not have been done.</p><p>The negative direct relative is expressed in the same way as in the conditional of the regular verb (see Lesson 12) by using <strong>nach</strong> 'that not' which causes eclipsis, e.g. <strong>... nach mba cheart...</strong> '... that should not...'.</p><p>Alternatively <strong>nár</strong> /Na:r/ which lenites may be used, e.g. <strong>nár cheart...</strong> '... that should not...'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 257, grammar_rule_id: 120, label: "(ii) Indirect relative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.806088Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.809032Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Positive</p><p><strong>Sin é an fear a mba mhaith leis an bord.</strong> That is the man who would like the table.</p><p>The positive indirect relative is expressed in the same way as in the conditional of the regular verb by using <strong>a</strong> 'that not' which causes eclipsis, e.g. <strong>... a mba mhaith leis...</strong> '... who(masc.) would like ...'.</p><p>Alternatively <strong>ar</strong> which lenites may be used, e.g. <strong>ar mhaith leis...</strong> '... who would like ...'.</p><p>(b) Negative</p><p><strong>Sin é an fear nach mba mhaith leis an bord.</strong> That is the man who would not like the table.</p><p>The negative indirect relative is expressed in the same way as in the conditional of the regular verb by using <strong>nach</strong> 'that not' which causes eclipsis, e.g. <strong>nach mba mhaith leis ...</strong> '... who would not like...'.</p><p>Alternatively <strong>nár</strong> /Na:r/ which lenites may be used, e.g. <strong>... nár mhaith leis...</strong> '... who would not like ...'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 258, grammar_rule_id: 121, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.813515Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.816380Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Ba</td><td>fhearr</td></tr><tr><td>Níorbh</td><td>fheáidir le Cáit, do Chait</td></tr><tr><td>Arbh</td><td>fhiú</td></tr><tr><td>Nárbh</td><td>fhóldir</td></tr><tr><td>Deir sé gurbh</td><td>fhíor</td></tr><tr><td>narbh</td><td>fhurasta do Chait</td></tr><tr><td>Dá mb'</td><td>éigean</td></tr><tr><td>Mara mb'</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 259, grammar_rule_id: 121, label: "(ii) Relative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.817038Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:50:30.819027Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Direct positive</p><p><strong>Sin é an rud a b'fhearr le Cáit.</strong> That is the thing that Cáit would prefer.</p><p>(b) Direct negative</p><p><strong>Sin é an rud narbh fhearr le Cáit.</strong> That is the thing that Cáit would not prefer.</p><p>(c) Indirect positive</p><p><strong>Sin é an fear arbh fhearr leis imeacht.</strong> That is the man who would prefer to go off.</p><p>(d) Indirect negative</p><p><strong>Sin é an fear narbh fhearr leis imeacht.</strong> That is the man who would not prefer to go off.</p><p>The examples (a) - (d) show the past conditional forms of the copula when used in idioms of the sort <strong>is fiú liom</strong> 'it seems worthwhile to me' (see Lesson 20), or <strong>is fiú dhom</strong> 'it is worthwhile for me' (see Lesson 23), before a word beginning with /, or before éigean. Although very frequent, these forms are optional in this dialect.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 260, grammar_rule_id: 123, label: "(i) Fronted sentences", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.487972Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.490754Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an fear ag caint le Cáit anois.</strong> — The man is talking to Cáit now.</p><p>Fronting:</p><p>(a) <strong>Is é an fear atá ag caint le Cáit anois.</strong> — It is the man who is talking to Cáit now.</p><p>(b) <strong>Is ag caint le Cáit atá an fear anois.</strong> — The man is talking to Cáit now. (lit. It is talking to C. that the man is now.)</p><p>(c) <strong>Is le Cáit atá an fear ag caint anois.</strong> — It is to Cáit the man is talking now.</p><p>(d) <strong>Is anois atá an fear ag caint le Cáit.</strong> — It is now that the man is talking to Cáit.</p><p>The appropriate pronoun (é, í, iad) is used before a definite noun, e.g. <strong>Is é an fear atá ...</strong> 'It is the man who is ...'; <strong>Is í Cáit atá ...</strong> 'It is Cáit who is ...'; <strong>Is iad mo mhuintir atá ...</strong> 'It is my people who are ...'; see Lesson 14.</p><p><strong>Ní bhíonn sé anseo go minic.</strong> — He is not often here.</p><p><strong>Ní minic a bhíonn sé anseo.</strong> — It is not often that he is here.</p><p>When an adverb or adjective with go (e.g. go minic, go maith) is fronted, no go is required following the copula.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 261, grammar_rule_id: 123, label: "(ii) Responses", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.493914Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.496583Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) <strong>Ab é an fear atá ag caint le Cáit?</strong> — Is é. /s'e:/ Ní hé. /N'i: he:/</p><p>Is it the man who is talking to Cáit? Yes. No.</p><p>In responses the appropriate pronoun is repeated with the required form of the copula (see Lesson 14).</p><p>(b) <strong>Ar ag caint le Cáit atá an fear?</strong> — Is ea. /s'æ:/ Ní hea. /N'i: hæ:/</p><p>Is it talking to Cáit that the man is? Yes. No.</p><p>Is the man talking to Cáit?</p><p>(c) <strong>Ar le Cáit atá an fear ag caint?</strong> — Is it to Cáit that the man is talking?</p><p>(d) <strong>Ar anois atá an fear ag caint le Cáit?</strong> — Is it now that the man is talking to Cáit?</p><p>(e) <strong>Ar cúthal atá an fear ag caint le Cáit?</strong> — Is it shy that the man is talking to Cáit? Is the man who is talking to Cáit shy?</p><p>When a preposition, adverb or adjective is fronted, ea is used in responses with the required form of the copula. For a more emphatic response a prepositional pronoun or an adjective can be repeated, e.g. <strong>Ar le Cáit atá ...?</strong> Is léi. <strong>Ar dearg atá ...?</strong> Is dearg.</p><p>In all the above fronted sentences the copula is often simply understood, e.g. <strong>ag caint le Cáit atá ...?</strong> Is ea.</p><p class=\"note\">This type of sentence is common in English as spoken in Ireland.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 262, grammar_rule_id: 123, label: "(iii) Fronted copula sentences", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.497331Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.499760Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ba cheart duit a theacht.</strong> — You should come.</p><p><strong>Thusa a ba cheart a theacht.</strong> — You should come.</p><p>The topics in copula sentences are not normally fronted in statements except in the phrases <strong>ba cheart</strong>, <strong>ba chóir</strong> 'you should ...'. In this case the contrast form of the personal pronoun, e.g. <strong>thusa</strong>, corresponding to the form of <strong>do</strong>, e.g. <strong>duit</strong>, in the basic sentence is fronted.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 263, grammar_rule_id: 124, label: "(i) Is é an chaoi", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.510779Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.512673Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhris sé an bord.</strong> — He broke the table.</p><p><strong>Is é an chaoi ar bhris sé an bord.</strong> — Actually, he broke the table.</p><p><strong>An nglanann Cáit an bord?</strong> — Does Cáit clean the table?</p><p><strong>Ab é an chaoi a nglanann Cáit an bord?</strong> — Does Cáit actually clean the table?</p><p>The verb can be emphasised by using <strong>is é an chaoi...</strong> /s'e: xi:/ or <strong>ab é an chaoi...?</strong> /əb'e: xi:/ in questions. Alternatively <strong>is amhlaidh ...</strong> /əs auL/ə, <strong>ar amhlaidh ...?</strong> /ər auLə/ can be used with a direct relative clause.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 264, grammar_rule_id: 124, label: "(ii) Is éard", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.513484Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.515157Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Dúirt sé go raibh sé ag imeacht.</strong> — He said he was going off.</p><p><strong>Is éard a dúirt sé go raibh sé ag imeacht.</strong> — What he said is that he was going off.</p><p><strong>Is éard</strong> /s'e:rd/, which is followed by a direct relative (see Lesson 13), is used to emphasise following indirect speech. The forms of the copula which are employed before <strong>é/í/iad</strong> (see Lesson 11) are used, e.g. <strong>Ab éard...?</strong> <strong>Nach éard?</strong></p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 265, grammar_rule_id: 125, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.519770Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.521706Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Pronunciation</th><th>Genitive</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>bean</td><td>/b'æ: N/</td><td>mná</td><td>cóta na mná</td></tr><tr><td>deirfiúr</td><td>/d'r'aur/</td><td>deirféar</td><td>fear mo dheirféar</td></tr><tr><td>lá</td><td>/L :/</td><td>lae</td><td>solas an lae</td></tr><tr><td>teach</td><td>/t'æ:x/</td><td>tí</td><td>doras an tí</td></tr><tr><td>leaba</td><td>/L'æ:bə/</td><td>leapa</td><td>bun na leapa</td></tr><tr><td>mí</td><td>/m'i:/</td><td>míosa</td><td>deireadh na míosa</td></tr><tr><td>trá</td><td>/tra:/</td><td>trá</td><td>bóthar na trá</td></tr><tr><td>dair</td><td>/dæ:r'/</td><td>daraí</td><td>crann daraí</td></tr><tr><td>talamh</td><td>/ta:Lə/</td><td>talún</td><td>tomhais na talún</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The genitive of <strong>trá</strong> is pronounced with a final /w/. The genitive of <strong>talamh</strong> may be either <strong>an talaimh</strong> or <strong>na talún</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 266, grammar_rule_id: 126, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.523126Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.524753Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>In set phrases, certain verbal nouns have a genitive form which is similar to the verbal adjective. The following are some examples:</p><table><tr><th>Verbal Noun</th><th>Genitive</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>caitheamh</td><td>caite</td><td>cead caite</td></tr><tr><td>pósadh</td><td>pósta</td><td>fáinne pósta</td></tr><tr><td>téamh</td><td>téite</td><td>deis téite</td></tr><tr><td>suí</td><td>suite</td><td>áit suite</td></tr><tr><td>luí</td><td>luite</td><td>áit luite</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 267, grammar_rule_id: 127, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.525940Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.529193Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá na báid anseo.</strong> — The boats are here.</p><p><strong>Tá úinéaraí na mbád anseo.</strong> — The owners of the boats are here.</p><p>Normally there is only one plural form (see Lesson 25). However, in some set phrases (mostly concerning animals or parts of the human body) an older genitive plural is still used:</p><table><tr><th>Nom. Pl.</th><th>Gen. Pl.</th><th>Example</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>éan</td><td>éanacha</td><td>ceol na n-éan</td></tr><tr><td>cearc</td><td>cearca</td><td>teach na gcearc</td></tr><tr><td>bos</td><td>bosa</td><td>ag bualadh bos</td></tr><tr><td>ronnach</td><td>ronnacha</td><td>ag iascach ronnach</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In form, the old genitive plural is generally the same as the nominative singular, e.g. <strong>éan</strong> 'bird', <strong>ceol na n-éan</strong> 'the song of the birds'. However, in the case of a feminine noun like <strong>bos</strong> it is the same as the old nominative (ending in a broad consonant), e.g. <strong>bos</strong> 'palm of hand', <strong>bualadh bos</strong> 'lit. hitting of hands, clapping'; <strong>muic</strong> 'pig', <strong>cuid na muc</strong> 'the food of the pigs, pig food'.</p><p>In the case of nouns which add a consonant in the genitive singular, e.g. <strong>lacha</strong> 'duck', gen. <strong>lachan</strong> (see Lesson 33), the old plural genitive is the same as the genitive singular, e.g. <strong>dá lachan</strong> 'a brood of ducks'.</p><p><strong>Radharc na súl</strong> (from <strong>súil</strong> 'eye') 'the sight of the eyes, eyesight' is exceptional.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 268, grammar_rule_id: 128, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.531543Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.533413Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>dheacracht</strong> — difficult</p><p><strong>dheirgeacht</strong> — red</p><p><strong>Dhá bharúlacht</strong> — amusing é, beidh Cáit sásta.</p><p><strong>shlachtmhaire</strong> — handsome</p><p><strong>chruacha</strong> — hard</p><p><strong>fheabhas</strong> — good, excellent</p><p><strong>Dhá</strong> /y :/, / :/, which is not stressed, causes lenition and is followed by a form which is the same as the abstract noun (see Lesson 33), e.g. <strong>dheacracht</strong> 'difficulty', <strong>dhá dheacracht</strong> 'however difficult'. Adjectives ending in -(e)ach, e.g. <strong>aisteach</strong> 'strange', or in -mhar e.g. <strong>slachtmhar</strong> 'handsome', or adjectives pronounced with a final vowel, e.g. <strong>crua</strong> 'hard', take a form the same as the comparative, e.g. <strong>níos aisti</strong> 'stranger', <strong>dhá aisti</strong> 'however strange', <strong>níos slachtmhaire</strong> 'handsomer', <strong>dhá shlachtmhaire</strong> 'however handsome', <strong>níos cruacha</strong> 'harder', <strong>dhá chruacha</strong> 'however hard'.</p><p>A sentence of the type 'the bigger, the better' is expressed by <strong>dhá mhéad é is ea is fearr</strong>. This use of <strong>is ea</strong> reflects a usage (which is now most infrequent) where it followed an adverbial phrase which was fronted, e.g. <strong>ar an mbóthar is ea a chonaic mé í</strong> 'on the road I saw her'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 269, grammar_rule_id: 129, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.534683Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:53:20.536107Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is iontach a fheabhas is atá sé.</strong> — It is amazing how good he is.</p><p><strong>A</strong> //ə, which is not stressed and causes lenition, takes the same form of an adjective as follows <strong>dhá</strong> 'however' (see above). This construction precedes <strong>is</strong> and a direct relative clause (see Lesson 13).</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 270, grammar_rule_id: 130, label: "(i) Optional replacement of go/nach clauses", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.289218Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.292548Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ba mhaith liom an carr a bheith ag Cáit</strong> — I would like Cáit to have the car.</p><p><strong>Ní miste gan airgead a bheith ag Máirtín</strong> — It doesn't matter that Máirtín has no money.</p><p>After prepositional phrases with the copula, e.g. is maith liom, is cuma liom, etc. (see Lessons 20 and 23), go/nach clauses are frequently replaced by using the subject followed by a /ə/, which causes lenition, and the verbal noun, e.g. <strong>Ba mhaith liom an carr a bheith ag Cáit</strong> 'I would like Cáit to have the car', instead of <strong>Ba mhaith liom go mbeadh an carr ag Cáit</strong>. A negation is expressed by using gan /gNə/ before the subject, e.g. <strong>Ba mhaith liom gan an carr a bheith ag Cáit</strong> 'I would like Cáit not to have the car'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 271, grammar_rule_id: 130, label: "(ii) In double conditions", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.293381Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.296357Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Dá mbeadh Máirtín anseo agus carr a bheith aige, bheadh Cáit sásta</strong> — If Máirtín was here and if he had a car, Cáit would be content.</p><p><strong>Dá mbeadh fear ag Cáit agus gan airgead a bheith aige, ní bheadh sí sásta</strong> — If Cáit had a man and if he hadn't any money, she would not be pleased.</p><p>In the case of a second condition, instead of simply using another dá/mara clause, e.g. <strong>dá mbeadh Máirtín anseo agus dá mbeadh carr aige</strong> ... 'if Máirtín was here and if he had a car ..., the infinitive construction may be used: <strong>dá mbeadh Máirtín anseo agus carr a bheith aige</strong>...</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 272, grammar_rule_id: 131, label: "(i) Two principal clauses", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.301443Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.304362Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí Bríd ann agus í tinn</strong> — Bríd was there and she was sick.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ansin agus leabhar mór aici</strong> — Cáit is there and she has a big book.</p><p><strong>D'imigh Máirtín amach agus gan aon chóta air</strong> — Máirtín went out and he had no coat on.</p><p>When two principal clauses are connected by agus, the verb tá may be omitted in the second: (a) if the same subject is in both clauses, e.g. <strong>Bhí Bríd ann agus í tinn</strong>. (b) if the preposition is in a prepositional idiom, e.g. <strong>Tá leabhar agam</strong>, refers to the subject of the first principal clause, e.g. <strong>Tá Cáit ansin agus leabhar mór aici</strong>.</p><p>This construction may be used to imply a certain element of surprise: <strong>Bhí Bríd ann agus í tinn</strong>. 'Bríd was there even though she was sick.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 273, grammar_rule_id: 131, label: "(ii) Agus/is meaning 'when, since'", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.305036Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.306792Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí an bosca ansin is mé ag teacht abhaile</strong> — The box was there when I was coming home.</p><p><strong>Bhí an lá gearr is tú ag imeacht thart mar sin</strong> — The day was short since you were going round like that.</p><p>A circumstantial phrase ('when ...', 'while ...', 'since ...', 'as ...') can be introduced by agus/is and the verb tá is then omitted.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 274, grammar_rule_id: 131, label: "(iii) Use of agus/is in responses", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.307461Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.309950Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An bhfuil sé míle as seo go Carna? - Tá agus deich míle!</strong> — Is it six miles from here to Carna? - Indeed it is probably ten.</p><p><strong>Tá mé ag imeacht anois. - Tá agus mise.</strong> — I am going off now. - Yes, and so am I.</p><p><strong>Is maith liom an áit seo. - Is maith agus liomsa.</strong> — I like this place. - Yes, and so do I.</p><p>In responses of this sort, which while in unison with the statement or question, add to an amount (e.g. <strong>tá agus deich míle!</strong>) or add to the subject (e.g. <strong>tá agus mise</strong>), agus/is is used and the verb tá need not be repeated. Note the use of the contrast form of pronouns or prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>tá agus mise</strong>; <strong>is maith agus liomsa</strong>.</p><p>If the statement or question is in the negative níl /Na:/ can be used similarly, e.g. <strong>Níor cheannaigh Cáit trí chóta? - Níor cheannaigh ná dhá chóta!</strong> 'Cáit didn't buy three coats?' - 'No, she didn't and she didn't even buy two coats!'; <strong>Níl mé sásta. - Níl ná mise.</strong> 'I am not satisfied.' - 'No, indeed, and neither am I.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 275, grammar_rule_id: 132, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.317993Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.319706Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Rinne mé go maith. Rinne tú.</strong> — I did well. Indeed you did.</p><p><strong>Bhídís sásta leis. Bhídís muis!</strong> — They used to be pleased with it. They were, indeed.</p><p>Where a response is emphasised the subject pronouns may be repeated, e.g. <strong>Rinne tú</strong> 'indeed you did' and both receive equal stress. When a combined form is pronounced with a long final syllable it can be stressed equally with the first syllable, e.g. <strong>bhídís</strong> /'w'e'd'i:s7, <strong>íoc</strong> /arfi:'k :r/. The second syllable of the optional third person plural past form (see Lessons 7 and 26) can also be stressed, e.g. <strong>bhíodar</strong> /fw'i:'dor/, as opposed to nonstressed <strong>AV'i:drə</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 276, grammar_rule_id: 133, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.322577Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.324551Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Sin a bhfuil anseo</strong> — That is all of that which is here.</p><p><strong>Chaith sé a raibh d'airgead aige</strong> — He spent all the money he had.</p><p>A /ə/ which causes eclipsis (and is followed by the dependent form of an irregular verb) is used to express 'all of that which', e.g. <strong>Sin a bhfuil anseo</strong>.'That is all of that which is here.' The preposition de is used before a following noun, e.g. <strong>Chaith sé a raibh d'airgead aige</strong> (lit. He spent all of that which he had of money), 'He spent all the money he had'.</p><p>When the preposition de is used in a partitive sense (see Lesson 24) it combines with the relative a(r) to give dhár /yu:(r)/, e.g. <strong>Ní raibh duine dhár raibh ann sásta</strong>. 'No one of all those who were there was pleased'; <strong>Ní chreidim focal dhár dúirt sé</strong> 'I don't believe a word of all that he said'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 277, grammar_rule_id: 134, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.327931Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:02.329730Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Month</th><th>Irish</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Eanáir</td><td>/æ:Na:r'/</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Feabhra</td><td>/f'aurə/</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Márta</td><td>/ma:rtə/</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Aibreán</td><td>/aib'r' :N/</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Bealtaine</td><td>/b'axtnə 'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Meitheamh</td><td>/m'ehwə /</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Iúil</td><td>/u:l'/</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Lúnasa</td><td>/LU:NSƏ Ə/</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Meán Fómhair</td><td>/m'a:Nfu:wrə '/</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Deireadh Fómhair</td><td>/d'er'ə fu:wrə '/</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Samhain</td><td>/saun'/</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Nollaig</td><td>/NoLkə '/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The above are now accepted as the standardised names of the months. Some of them are traditional in the dialect, e.g. <strong>An Márta</strong> 'March', <strong>An tAibreán</strong> 'April', <strong>An Bhealtaine</strong> 'May', and these take the article, e.g. <strong>san Aibreán</strong> 'in April'. Others have been introduced through the school system, e.g. <strong>Meitheamh</strong> 'June', <strong>Iúil</strong> 'July'. <strong>Mí</strong> 'month' is often prefixed, e.g. <strong>Mí Eanáir</strong> 'the month of January', <strong>Mí Meán Fómhair</strong> 'September'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 278, grammar_rule_id: 135, label: "(i) The present subjunctive", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.130796Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.133031Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Go sábhála Dia sinn</strong> ! — God save us!</p><p><strong>Go gcuire Dia an t-ádh ort</strong>! — May God make you lucky!</p><p><strong>Go mbeannaí Dia dhuit</strong>! — May God bless you!</p><p><strong>Nár éirí sin leat</strong>! — May you not succeed with that!</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 279, grammar_rule_id: 135, label: "(ii) The Past Subjunctive", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.133866Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.136459Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The use of the past subjunctive, which is in form the same as the habitual past (see Lesson 24), is very limited. It may be used optionally instead of the conditional following <strong>dá</strong>, e.g. <strong>D'ólfainn deoch dá ngabhfainn ann</strong> 'I would have a drink if I went there' or <strong>D'ólfainn deoch dá dtéinn ann</strong> ... 'I would have a drink if I should go there'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 280, grammar_rule_id: 136, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.147279Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.149769Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glanadh sé an bord agus níodh Cáit</strong> — Let him clear the table and have Cáit wash the dishes.</p><p><strong>Léadh Máirtín an leabhar ach ná briseadh sé an chathaoir</strong> — Let Máirtín read the book but let him not break the chair.</p><p><strong>Coinniodh muid an cóta sin ach ná salaíodh muid é</strong> — Let us keep the coat but let us not dirty it.</p><p><strong>Osclaíodh Cáit an doras agus imriodh muide cluiche eile</strong> — Have Cáit open the door and let us play another game.</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds -(e)adh, -odh</td><td>adds -(a)iodh /i:x/</td></tr><tr><td>glanadh</td><td>salaíodh</td></tr><tr><td>briseadh</td><td>coinníodh</td></tr><tr><td>léadh</td><td>osclaíodh</td></tr><tr><td>níodh</td><td>imriodh</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 281, grammar_rule_id: 137, label: "(i) Use with indirect relative", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.156034Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.158273Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ní bhacfaidh mé leis</strong>. — I won't bother with it.</p><p><strong>Diabhal a mbacfaidh mé leis</strong>. — Indeed I won't bother with it.</p><p>Diabhal or dheamhan can be used with any verb in an indirect relative clause (see Lesson 18).</p><p class=\"note\">Diabhal and dheamhan can contain a partitive idea and be followed by the preposition de, e.g. <strong>Diabhal ar chuala mé de'n fhocal sin ariamh</strong>. 'Indeed I have never heard (anything of) that word'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 282, grammar_rule_id: 137, label: "(ii) Use with fronted emphasised subject in sentence containing the verb tá", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.158906Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.160495Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ní raibh duine ar bith ann ach thú féin</strong> — There was no one there but yourself.</p><p><strong>Diabhal duine ar bith a bhí ann ach thú féin</strong>. — Indeed no one was there but yourself.</p><p>Diabhal or dheamhan can be used to bring an emphasised subject to the front of a sentence containing the verb <strong>tá</strong>. In this case a direct relative (see Lesson 13) is used.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 283, grammar_rule_id: 137, label: "(iii) Assertive use before go/nach clause", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.161179Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.165208Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Diabhal go bhfuil an áit seo go deas</strong>. — Indeed this place is nice.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 284, grammar_rule_id: 137, label: "(iv) Use in responses", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.167071Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.170841Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>An bhfuil bosca ar bith ann?</strong> — Is there any box?</p><p>- <strong>Diabhal bosca!</strong> — Indeed no, there isn't!</p><p><strong>Ar léigh tú an leabhar seo ariamh?</strong> — Did you ever read this book?</p><p>- <strong>Diabhal léamh!</strong> — Indeed no I didn't!</p><p>In responses the topic of the question, e.g. <strong>bosca</strong>, or the verbal noun of the question verb, e.g. <strong>léamh</strong>, are used after diabhal or dheamhan, e.g. <strong>Diabhal bosca!</strong> - <strong>Diabhal léamh!</strong> Alternatively <strong>é</strong> can be used, e.g. <strong>An bhfuil bosca ar bith ann?</strong> - <strong>Dheamhan é</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 285, grammar_rule_id: 138, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.188445Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T22:54:38.191644Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glanfaidh tú an bord, an nglanfais?</strong> — You will clean the table, will you?</p><p><strong>Ar nigh tú na soithí?</strong> - <strong>Níos</strong>. — Did you wash the dishes? - Yes, I did.</p><p><strong>Nigh mé ar ball iad</strong>. — I washed them a while ago.</p><p><strong>Léigh an leabhar ach ná bris an chathaoir, nó má bhriseann tú</strong> ... — Read the book but do not break the chair, or if you do ...</p><p><strong>Ar choinnigh tú an cóta?</strong> — Did you keep the coat?</p><p>- <strong>Ar ndóigh, choinnigh</strong>. — Yes, of course I did.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 286, grammar_rule_id: 139, label: "(i) Indefinite predicate", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.951603Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.953892Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is dochtúir mé.</strong> — I am a doctor.</p><p><strong>Is múinteoir í sin.</strong> — That woman there is a teacher.</p><p>When the predicate is an indefinite noun, e.g. dochtúir 'a doctor', múinteoir 'a teacher', it directly follows is.</p><p><strong>Is sagart é Máirtín.</strong> — Máirtín is a priest.</p><p><strong>Ar múinteoir í do bhean?</strong> — Is your wife a teacher?</p><p><strong>Deir sé gur dochtúirí iad na daoine sin.</strong> — He says that those people are doctors.</p><p>The rule that in any copula sentence where the subject is definite, e.g. Máirtín, do bhean, na daoine sin, it is preceded by the appropriate pronoun é, í or iad (see Lesson 11) holds good.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 287, grammar_rule_id: 139, label: "(ii) Responses", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.954651Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.956620Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ar dochtúir mé/mise?</strong> — <strong>Is ea.</strong> /s'æ:/ Yes.</p><p><strong>Thú/tusa?</strong> — <strong>Ní hea.</strong> /N'i: 'hæ:/ No.</p><p>etc. <strong>Ab ea?</strong> /'əb'æ:/ Yes?</p><p><strong>Nach ea?</strong> /Nax 'æ:/ No?</p><p><strong>Deir sé gur ea.</strong> /gur'b'æ:/ He says yes.</p><p><strong>nach ea.</strong> /Nax 'æ:/ He says no.</p><p>In responses to sentences with an indefinite noun, ea (an old neuter third-person pronoun) is used. Is ea is often spelt 'sea, showing the stress and pronunciation.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 288, grammar_rule_id: 139, label: "(iii) Emphatic word-order", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.957303Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.959282Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Is duine deas é.</strong> — He is a nice person.</p><p><strong>Is deas an duine é.</strong> — He is a nice person.</p><p>When in copula sentences containing an adjective, e.g. <strong>Is duine deas é.</strong> 'He is a nice person', the adjective is emphasised, it precedes the noun and the article is used. This is the normal order in certain common expressions, e.g.</p><p><strong>Is mór an trua é.</strong> — It is a great pity</p><p><strong>Is maith an scéal é.</strong> — That is good news.</p><p><strong>Is bocht an rud é.</strong> — It is a miserable affair.</p><p>In responses the adjective is repeated, e.g. <strong>Is deas an duine é.</strong> 'He is a nice person.'- <strong>Is deas.</strong> 'Yes, he is.' Alternatively ea can be used, e.g. <strong>Is ea.</strong> 'Yes, he is.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 289, grammar_rule_id: 140, label: "(i) Singular", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.972616Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.974472Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glan an bord agus nigh na soithí!</strong> — Clear the table and wash the dishes!</p><p><strong>Léigh an leabhar ach ná bris an chathaoir!</strong> — Read the book, but don't break the chair!</p><p><strong>Coinnigh an cóta sin ach ná salaigh an caipín!</strong> — Keep that coat, but don't dirty the cap!</p><p><strong>Oscail an doras agus imir cluiche eile!</strong> — Open the door and play another game!</p><p>The second singular imperative, e.g. glan, bris is the root of the verb, that is, the form to which other endings are added. There are two basic types of regular verb; in the vocabularies, verbs are marked '1' or '2' according to whether they belong to the first or second type. The second singular of the first type has one syllable, e.g. glan, bris, nigh, léigh while that of the second type has two syllables, e.g. salaigh, coinnigh, oscail, imir. There are certain differences in the endings which are added to these two types.</p><p class=\"note\">Exceptionally, verbs with roots ending in (e)áil (pacáil, péinteáil etc.) belong to type 1; also verbs spelt with one syllable but pronounced with a helping vowel, e.g. dearg 'redden' (see Appendix 1.2) belong to type 1.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 290, grammar_rule_id: 140, label: "(ii) Plural", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.975074Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.976968Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Glanaigí an bord agus nígí na soithí!</strong> — Clear the table and wash the dishes!</p><p><strong>Léigí an leabhar ach ná brisigí an chathaoir!</strong> — Read the book, but don't break the chair!</p><p><strong>Coinnígí an cóta sin ach ná salaígí an caipín!</strong> — Keep that coat, but don't dirty the cap!</p><p><strong>Osclaígí an doras agus imrígí cluiche eile!</strong> — Open the door and play another game!</p><table><tr><th>Type 1</th><th>Type 2</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>adds-(a)igí /gə 'i:/</td><td>adds -(a)ígí /i:g'i:/</td></tr><tr><td>glan + aigí glanaigí</td><td>sal(aigh) + aígí salaígí</td></tr><tr><td>bris + igí brisigí</td><td>coinn(igh) + ígí coinnígí</td></tr><tr><td>lé(igh) + igí léigí</td><td>osc(ai)l + aígí osclaígí</td></tr><tr><td>ni(gh) + igí nígí</td><td>im(i)r+ígí imrígí</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Final -igh is dropped after a long vowel. When the ending has an initial vowel the last syllable is always lost: -(a)igh is dropped; (a)i- is dropped before a final l, n, r which will then be broad or slender according to the preceding consonant, e.g. oscail oscl- (broad), imir imr- (slender).</p><p>The endings -aigí/-aígí are added to final broad consonants, e.g. glan-, sal-, oscl-. The endings -igí/-ígí are added to final slender consonants, e.g. bris-, coinn-, imr-. All verbal endings are spelt with an initial 'broad' or 'slender' vowel according to whether the preceding consonant is spelt broad or slender.</p><p class=\"note\">1. pacáil: pacálaigí péinteáil: péinteálaigí Verbs with roots ending in -(e)áil are regular and behave exactly like glan, bris, except that the final l is normally made broad before any ending which does not begin with i.</p><p class=\"note\">2. rith /r'i:/ : rithigí /ri:g'i:/ bruith /bru/ : bruithigí /bri:g'i:/ In spelling, verbs with roots ending in -th are regular. In pronunciation, however, as th /h/ is dropped in this dialect (see App. 1.4), the syllable is lengthened just as in nígí.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 291, grammar_rule_id: 140, label: "(iii) Negative", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.977548Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.986996Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Ná hoscail an doras seo!</strong> — Don't open that door!</p><p>Ná prefixes h to any verb with an initial vowel.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 292, grammar_rule_id: 140, label: "(iv) Contrast or emphasis", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.987602Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:50.990005Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Ordinary form</p><p><strong>Glan é!</strong> — Clean it!</p><p><strong>Osclaígí é!</strong> — Open (pl.) it!</p><p>(b) Emphasis</p><p><strong>Glan féin é!</strong> — Clean it yourself!</p><p><strong>Osclaígí féin é!</strong> — Open it yourselves!</p><p>(c) Contrast</p><p><strong>Glan thusa é!</strong> — You clean it!</p><p><strong>Osclaígí sibhse é!</strong> — You (pl.) open it!</p><p>(d) Contrast + Emphasis</p><p><strong>Glan thusa thú féin é!</strong> — You clean it yourself!</p><p><strong>Osclaígí sibhse sibh féin é!</strong> — You open it yourselves!</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 293, grammar_rule_id: 141, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.001426Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.003752Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>an t-aon fhuinneog amháin</strong> — the only (one) window</p><p><strong>an dá fhuinneog</strong> — the two windows</p><p><strong>na trí fhuinneog</strong> — the three windows</p><p>An t- is used before aon, irrespective of gender, e.g. <strong>an t-aon bhord amháin</strong> 'the only (one) table', <strong>an t-aon fhuinneog amháin</strong> 'the only (one) window'. Dhá 'two', which is the lenited form, is generally used, but after the article dá, the unlenited form, is required, e.g. <strong>an dá fhuinneog</strong> 'the two windows'.</p><p>Na is always used with numbers over two, e.g. <strong>na trí fhuinneog</strong> 'the three windows', <strong>na ceithre shagart</strong> 'the four priests', and optionally before dá, e.g. <strong>na dá fhuinneog</strong> 'the two windows'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 294, grammar_rule_id: 142, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.006990Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.008564Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>aon fhear mór amháin</strong> — only one big man</p><p><strong>an dá fhuinneog mhóra</strong> — the two big windows</p><p><strong>na trí theach bheaga</strong> — the three little houses</p><p>After aon the adjective is as normal, e.g. <strong>aon fhear mór amháin</strong> 'only one big man', <strong>aon bhean mhór amháin</strong> 'only one big woman' (see Lesson 4). After the numbers dhá 'two' to deich 'ten' the adjective is in the plural and is lenited, e.g. <strong>an dá fhuinneog mhóra</strong> 'the two big windows', <strong>na trí theach bheaga</strong> 'the three little houses'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 295, grammar_rule_id: 143, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.012599Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:20:51.015660Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>Ceann 'one' (lit. 'head') can stand for any noun:</p><p><strong>An bhfuil mórán boird anseo?</strong> — Are there many tables here?</p><p><strong>Bord amháin / Ceann amháin.</strong> — One table / One.</p><p><strong>Dhá bhord / Dhá cheann / Péire.</strong> — Two tables / Two.</p><p><strong>Ceithre bhord / Ceithre cinn.</strong> — Four tables / Four.</p><p>Péire 'pair' is very often used in place of dhá cheann.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 296, grammar_rule_id: 144, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.083490Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.085401Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Imperative</th><th>Verbal Noun</th><th>Habitual Tenses</th><th>Future / Conditional</th><th>Past Tense</th><th>Verbal Adjective</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>déan</td><td>déanamh</td><td>déan-</td><td>déan-</td><td>rinne</td><td>déanta</td></tr><tr><td>feic</td><td>feiceáil</td><td>feic-</td><td>feic-</td><td>chonaic</td><td>feicthe</td></tr><tr><td>gabh</td><td>gabháil</td><td>té-</td><td>gabh-</td><td>chuaigh</td><td>goite</td></tr><tr><td>faigh</td><td>fáil</td><td>faigh-</td><td>gheobh-</td><td>fuair</td><td>faighte</td></tr></tbody></table><p class=\"note\">The particular pronunciation of té- as /t'ai/, e.g. téann sé /t'aiN s'e:/ 'he goes', of faigh as /fa:/, e.g. fhaigheann sé /fa:N s'e/ 'he gets', and of chonaic as /xan'ək/ should be carefully noted.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 297, grammar_rule_id: 145, label: "(i) Examples", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.091381Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.093600Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>blaosc</strong> — eggshell</p><p><strong>lán na blaoisce</strong> — the full of the eggshell</p><p><strong>seachtain</strong> — week</p><p><strong>deireadh na seachtaine</strong> — the end of the week</p><p><strong>bróg</strong> — shoe</p><p><strong>bonn mo bhróige</strong> — the sole of my shoe</p><p><strong>fuinneog</strong> — window</p><p><strong>dúnadh na fuinneoige</strong> — closing the window</p><p><strong>muic</strong> — pig</p><p><strong>cloigeann na muice</strong> — the pig's head</p><p><strong>ubh</strong> — egg</p><p><strong>blas na huibhe</strong> — the taste of the egg</p><p><strong>grian</strong> — sun</p><p><strong>solas na gréine</strong> — the light of the sun</p><p><strong>Gaillimh</strong> — Galway</p><p><strong>muintir na Gaillimhe</strong> — the people of Galway</p><p><strong>bainis</strong> — wedding</p><p><strong>lucht na bainse</strong> — those associated with the wedding</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 298, grammar_rule_id: 146, label: "(i) Formation", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.100730Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.102948Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>bán</strong> — white</p><p><strong>(níos/is) báine</strong> — whiter</p><p><strong>glic</strong> — cunning</p><p><strong>glice</strong> — more cunning</p><p><strong>bog</strong> — soft</p><p><strong>boige</strong> — softer</p><p><strong>gorm</strong> — blue</p><p><strong>goirme</strong> — bluer</p><p><strong>tobann</strong> — sudden</p><p><strong>tobainne</strong> — more sudden</p><p><strong>fairsing</strong> — plentiful</p><p><strong>fairsinge</strong> — more plentiful</p><p><strong>bodhar</strong> — deaf, dumb</p><p><strong>bodhaire</strong> — deafer, dumber</p><p><strong>sleamhain</strong> — slippery</p><p><strong>sleamhaine</strong> — more slippery</p><p><strong>láidir</strong> — strong</p><p><strong>láidire</strong> — stronger</p><p><strong>dílis</strong> — faithful</p><p><strong>dilse</strong> — more faithful</p><p><strong>cothrom</strong> — level</p><p><strong>coithrime</strong> — leveller</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 299, grammar_rule_id: 146, label: "(ii) Further examples with regular vowel changes", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.103545Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:24:39.105563Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>tinn</strong> — sick</p><p><strong>níos tinne</strong> — sicker</p><p><strong>cam</strong> — crooked</p><p><strong>níos caime</strong> — more crooked</p><p><strong>trom</strong> — heavy</p><p><strong>níos troime</strong> — heavier</p><p>Similar to trom is lom 'bare', níos loime 'barer'; cúng 'narrow', níos cuinge 'narrower'.</p><p>In some few adjectives the middle syllable is dropped with resulting regular vowel changes:</p><p><strong>domhain</strong> — deep</p><p><strong>níos doimhne</strong> — deeper</p><p><strong>soibhir</strong> — rich</p><p><strong>níos soibhre</strong> — richer</p><p><strong>milis</strong> — sweet</p><p><strong>níos milse</strong> — sweeter</p><p>Similar to domhain is reamhar 'fat', níos reimhe 'fatter'.</p><p>Exceptionally, the vowel does not change in donn /dauN/ 'brown', níos doinne /dauN'ə/ 'browner', and teann/t' :N/ 'firm', níos teáinne /t'a:N'ə/ 'firmer'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 300, grammar_rule_id: 147, label: "(i) Comparative", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.443927Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.446184Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an seomra seo níos dorcha.</strong> — This room is darker.</p><p><strong>Is dorcha an seomra seo.</strong> — This room is darker.</p><p>A comparison is expressed:</p><p>(a) by <em>níos</em> (lit. 'a thing which is'; <em>ní</em> 'a thing' written together with <em>is</em>, the relative form of the copula, 'which is') before a form of the adjective, e.g. <em>dorcha</em> 'dark', <em>níos dorcha</em> 'darker'.</p><p>(b) by the copula <em>is</em> (see 'Lesson 11) before the adjective, e.g. <em>Is dorcha</em> 'is darker'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 301, grammar_rule_id: 147, label: "(ii) Superlative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.446932Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.448755Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá an seomra is dorcha anseo.</strong> — The darkest room is here.</p><p>(lit. The room which is darkest is here)</p><p><strong>Sin í an deoch is blasta.</strong> — That is the tastiest drink.</p><p>To form a superlative, <em>is</em> (relative form of the copula) is used before the adjective.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 302, grammar_rule_id: 147, label: "(iii) Form of adjective", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.449422Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.451598Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The form of the adjective (or adverb) used in the comparative and in the superlative is always the same.</p><p>Two-syllable adjectives which end in a vowel, e.g. <em>dorcha</em>, <em>blasta</em>, and all verbal adjectives, e.g. <em>briste</em> 'broken', <em>péinteáilte</em> 'painted', do not change.</p><p class=\"note\">*When used with prepositions ending in a vowel, <em>n</em> /N/ may optionally be inserted: <em>le chéile</em> or <em>lena chéile</em> 'with each other'. Normally when <em>n</em> is not inserted, the actual pronunciation is shown by omitting the <em>a</em>: <em>le chéile</em>, <em>ó chéile</em> (see Appendix 1.5).</p><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative/Superlative</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>beo</td><td>níos/is beocha</td></tr><tr><td>buidhe</td><td>níos/is buíoch</td></tr><tr><td>breá</td><td>níos/is breácha</td></tr><tr><td>crua</td><td>níos/is crua</td></tr><tr><td>rua</td><td>níos/is rua</td></tr><tr><td>luath</td><td>níos/is luath</td></tr><tr><td>liath</td><td>níos/is liath</td></tr><tr><td>réidh</td><td>níos/is réidh</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In two or three adjectives the syllable is lengthened:</p><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative/Superlative</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>tiubh</td><td>níos/is tiúcha also tiubhe</td></tr><tr><td>dubh</td><td>níos/is dúcha duibhe</td></tr><tr><td>te</td><td>níos/is teocha</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Other regular types will be dealt with in later lessons.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 303, grammar_rule_id: 147, label: "(iv) Adjectives with irregular comparative form", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.452241Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.454114Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative/Superlative</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>maith</td><td>níos/is fearr</td></tr><tr><td>dona</td><td>measa</td></tr><tr><td>mór</td><td>mó</td></tr><tr><td>beag</td><td>lú</td></tr><tr><td>álainn</td><td>áille</td></tr><tr><td>gránna</td><td>gránna</td></tr><tr><td>fada</td><td>faide</td></tr><tr><td>tanaí</td><td>tanaí</td></tr><tr><td>minic</td><td>minicí</td></tr><tr><td>tirim</td><td>trioma</td></tr><tr><td>furasta</td><td>fusa</td></tr></tbody></table><p>All these forms are pronounced regularly in accordance with the table in Appendix I (with the exception of <em>furasta</em> /frustə/ in which the first syllable is dropped.</p><p>The forms <em>níos dona</em>, <em>níos beag</em>, <em>níos furasta</em> are also used, though probably less frequently than the forms given above.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 304, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(i) With le", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.458979Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.460587Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit chomh mór le Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit is as big as Máirtín.</p><p><em>Chomh... le</em> is used before a noun to express 'as ... as', e.g. <em>chomh mór le</em> 'as big as'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 305, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(ii) With is followed by a direct relative", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.461196Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.462743Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl Cáit chomh sásta is a bhí Máirtín.</strong> — Cáit is not as pleased as Máirtín was.</p><p><em>Chomh ... is</em> followed by a direct relative (see Lesson 13) is used before a verb to express 'as ... as', e.g. <em>chomh sásta is a bhí</em> 'as pleased as ... was'. This <em>is</em> can be taken to be the shortened form of <em>agus</em>, which may also occur in this construction.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 306, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(iii) With sin", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.463341Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.464920Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Níl sé chomh maith sin.</strong> — He is not all that good.</p><p><strong>Bhí Máirtín chomh sásta sin go mbeidh Cáit sásta freisin.</strong> — Máirtín was so pleased that Cáit will also be pleased.</p><p><em>Chomh ... sin (go/nach)</em> is used to express 'all that...', 'so that...', e.g. <em>chomh maith sin</em> 'all that good', <em>chomh sásta sin</em> 'so pleased that...'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 307, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(iv) With céanna", position: 4, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.465513Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.466996Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Máirtín chomh beag céanna.</strong> — Máirtín is just as small.</p><p><em>Chomh ... céanna</em> is used to express 'just as ...', e.g. <em>chomh beag céanna</em> 'just as small'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 308, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(v) As exclamation", position: 5, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.467556Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.469026Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Chomh ard le Cáit!</strong> — How tall Cáit is!</p><p><em>Chomh ... le</em> can be used to express 'how ... !'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 309, grammar_rule_id: 148, label: "(vi) In questions", position: 6, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.469598Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.471052Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Cé chomh ard le Cáit?</strong> — How tall is Cáit?</p><p><em>Cé chomh ...</em> can be used to express 'how ... is ?'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 310, grammar_rule_id: 149, label: "(i) Introduction and table", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.476211Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.478207Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag goil suas.</strong> — Cáit is going up(wards).</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit thuas.</strong> — Cáit is up (above).</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag teacht anuas.</strong> — Cáit is coming down (from above).</p><p>The use of these adverbs depends on the position of the speaker. <em>Suas</em> 'up, upwards' implies motion or direction away from the speaker, <em>thuas</em> 'up, above', a position above, while <em>anuas</em> 'down from above' indicates movement or direction downwards from above the speaker.</p><table><tr><th>Motion or direction to</th><th>Stationary</th><th>Motion or direction from</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>Tá Cáit ag goil suas</td><td>Tá Cáit thuas</td><td>Tá Cáit ag teacht anuas</td></tr><tr><td>suas /su:sə/</td><td>thuas /hu:sə/</td><td>anuas /'ənu:sə/</td></tr><tr><td>up, upwards</td><td>up, above</td><td>down from above</td></tr><tr><td>síos /s'i:s/</td><td>thíos /hi:s/</td><td>aníos /'ən'í:s/</td></tr><tr><td>down, downwards</td><td>down, below</td><td>up from below</td></tr><tr><td>soir /ser'/</td><td>thoir/her'/</td><td>anoir /'ən'er'/</td></tr><tr><td>east, eastwards</td><td>in the east</td><td>from the east</td></tr><tr><td>siar /s'i:rə/</td><td>thiar /hi:rə/</td><td>aniar /'ən'i:rə/</td></tr><tr><td>west, westwards</td><td>in the west</td><td>from the west</td></tr><tr><td>back, backwards</td><td>back (there)</td><td>from back (there)</td></tr><tr><td>sal /sa:l/</td><td>thall/h:l/</td><td>anall 'n/əcu l/</td></tr><tr><td>i bhus /'əwus/</td><td>anonn /d'nu:n/</td><td>over there, from over there, over here</td></tr><tr><td>over, across</td><td>across there</td><td>from across there</td></tr><tr><td>isteach /'əs't'æ:x/</td><td>istigh /'əs't'i/</td><td>in, inwards</td></tr><tr><td>in, within</td><td>amach /'əma:x/</td><td>amuigh /'əmu/</td></tr><tr><td>out, outwards</td><td>out, without</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 311, grammar_rule_id: 149, label: "(ii) The adverbs ó thuaidh and ó dheas", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.478797Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.480442Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><em>Ó thuaidh</em> /o: hu:ə/ 'northwards, in the north, from the north', and <em>ó dheas</em> 'southwards, in the south, from the south' normally have only one form, e.g. <strong>Tá Cáit ag goil ó thuaidh;</strong> <strong>Tá Cáit ó thuaidh;</strong> <strong>Tá Cáit ag teacht ó thuaidh.</strong> However, when they function as adjectives in set phrases, <em>aduaidh</em> /'ədu:ə/ and <em>aneas</em> /ə'væs/ are used:</p><p><strong>siar/thiar/aniar aduaidh</strong> 'northwestward, in the northwest, from the northwest'</p><p><strong>siar/thiar/aniar aneas</strong> 'southwestward' etc.</p><p><strong>soir/thoir/anoir aduaidh</strong> 'northeastward' etc.</p><p><strong>soir/thoir/anoir aneas</strong> 'southeastward' etc.</p><p>Also: <strong>an ghaoth aduaidh</strong> 'the north wind', <strong>an ghaoth aneas</strong> 'the south wind'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 312, grammar_rule_id: 149, label: "(iii) Usage", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.481004Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.491783Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) <strong>Tá Cáit ag goil síos an bóthar.</strong> — Cáit is going down the road.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag goil soir an bóthar.</strong> — Cáit is going eastwards on the road.</p><p>In local directions <em>soir</em>, etc. 'east ...', <em>siar</em>, etc. 'west ...', <em>síos</em> etc. 'down ...' (geographically north) and <em>suas</em>, etc. 'up ...' (geographically south) are used where English loosely employs 'up' or 'down'.</p><p>(b) <strong>an fear istigh</strong> — the man inside</p><p><strong>na fir thall</strong> — the men over there</p><p><strong>an fear isteach</strong> — the man who is just coming/going in</p><p>In this type of phrase the relative of the verb <em>tá</em>, as well as the notion of movement ('going/coming'), can be understood, e.g. <em>an fear (atá) istigh</em> 'the man (who is) inside', <em>an fear (atá ag goil/teacht) isteach</em> 'the man (who is coming/going) in'.</p><p>(c) <strong>Tá Cáit ag goil siar amach.</strong> — Cáit is going away off to the west.</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit thiar amach.</strong> — Cáit is away in the west.</p><p><em>Amach/amuigh</em> can be combined with <em>siar/thiar</em> to indicate greater distance.</p><p>(d) <strong>Beidh mé aniar amárach.</strong> — I will be (coming) from the west tomorrow.</p><p>When an adverb implying motion or direction is used, a verb of motion can be understood.</p><p>(e) <strong>an taobh istigh</strong> — the inside</p><p><strong>an taobh ó thuaidh</strong> — the northside</p><p><em>Taobh</em> /ti:w/'side' can be used with stationary forms of these adverbs. When so used the final /w/ is not pronounced.</p><p>(f) <strong>Tá sé in ann Cáit a bhualadh amach.</strong> — He can completely beat (= surpass) Cáit.</p><p><strong>Tá sé ag glanadh suas.</strong> — He is cleaning up.</p><p><em>Amach</em> and <em>suas</em> can be used with certain verbs to express the completion of an action.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 313, grammar_rule_id: 150, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.499020Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:28:12.500457Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>'x boats'</th><th>'x coats'</th><th>'x years'</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>aon bhád déag</td><td>aon chóta dhéag</td><td>aon bhliain déag</td></tr><tr><td>dhá bhád déag</td><td>dhá chóta dhéag</td><td>dhá bhliain déag</td></tr><tr><td>trí bhád déag</td><td>trí chóta dhéag</td><td>trí bliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>ceithre bhád déag</td><td>ceithre chóta dhéag</td><td>ceithre bliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>cúig bhád déag</td><td>cúig chóta dhéag</td><td>cúig bliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>sé bhád déag</td><td>sé chóta dhéag</td><td>sé bliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>seacht mbád déag</td><td>seacht gcóta dhéag</td><td>seacht mbliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>ocht mbád déag</td><td>ocht gcóta dhéag</td><td>ocht mbliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>naoi mbád déag</td><td>naoi gcóta dhéag</td><td>naoi mbliana déag</td></tr><tr><td>fiche bád</td><td>fiche cóta</td><td>fiche bliain</td></tr><tr><td>dhá fhichead bád</td><td>dhá fhichead cóta</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 314, grammar_rule_id: 151, label: "(i) Forms", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.622808Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.624677Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Imperative</th><th>Verbal Noun</th><th>Habitual Tense</th><th>Future/Conditional</th><th>Past Tense</th><th>Verbal Adjective</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>clois</td><td>cloisteáil</td><td>clois-</td><td>clois-</td><td>chuala</td><td>cloiste</td></tr><tr><td>ith</td><td>ithe</td><td>ith-</td><td>íos-</td><td>d'ith</td><td>ite</td></tr><tr><td>tabhair</td><td>tabhairt</td><td>tug-</td><td>tabhar-</td><td>thug</td><td>tugtha</td></tr><tr><td>beir</td><td>breith</td><td>beir-</td><td>béar-</td><td>rug</td><td>beirthe</td></tr><tr><td>abair</td><td>rá</td><td>deir-</td><td>déar-</td><td>dúirt</td><td>ráite</td></tr><tr><td>tar</td><td>teacht</td><td>tag-</td><td>tioc-</td><td>tháinig</td><td>tagtha</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 315, grammar_rule_id: 152, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.634799Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.636218Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá spás idir an bord agus an balla.</strong> — 'There is a space between the table and the wall.'</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 316, grammar_rule_id: 152, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.636785Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.638503Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>idir mé...</td><td>between me ...</td></tr><tr><td>idir thú...</td><td>between you ...</td></tr><tr><td>idir é/í...</td><td>between him/her/it...</td></tr><tr><td>eadrainn</td><td>between us /ætrNə '/</td></tr><tr><td>eadraibh</td><td>between you (pl.) /a:tri:/</td></tr><tr><td>eadarthu</td><td>between them /a:trbə /</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Idir does not combine with the singular personal pronouns, e.g. idir mé, idir mise, idir mé féin, idir mise mé féin. The contrast forms and general usage of plural forms are the same as those of ag and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. eadrainn, eadrainne, eadrainn féin, eadrainne muid féin; see Lesson 16.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 317, grammar_rule_id: 152, label: "(iii) Meaning of idir", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.639171Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.640982Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) Basic meaning 'between'</p><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo idir an dó agus an trí.</strong> — 'Cáit will be here between two and three.'</p><p>(b) Secondary meaning 'both'</p><p><strong>Bhí idir sean agus óg ann.</strong> — 'There were both old (people) and young (people) there.'</p><p><strong>Tá idir balla agus ceann briste.</strong> — 'Both wall(s) and roof are broken.'</p><p>No article is required after idir 'both' and the singular can be used in a general sense. Also idir 'both' may lenite following nouns (though not usually those with initial t or s), e.g. <strong>idir fhear agus bhean</strong> — 'both men and women', <strong>idir bheithigh agus chaoirigh</strong> — 'both cows and sheep', <strong>idir mhagadh is dháiríre</strong> — 'half in mocking (lit. both joking and serious)'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 318, grammar_rule_id: 153, label: "(i) ea to i", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.646915Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.648446Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>leanbh</strong> — child, <strong>caipín an linbh</strong> — 'the child's cap'</p><p><strong>ceann</strong> — head, <strong>mullach mo chinn</strong> — 'the top of my head'</p><p>Similar to leanbh are: <strong>fear</strong> 'man', <strong>cóta an fhir</strong> 'the man's coat', <strong>breac</strong> 'fish', <strong>ag marú an bhric</strong> 'catching (lit. killing) the fish'. Similar to ceann is <strong>greann</strong> 'humour', <strong>údar grinn</strong> 'a source of humour'.</p><p>Note also <strong>feall</strong>, <strong>buille feill</strong> 'a treacherous blow', in which ea changes to ei.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 319, grammar_rule_id: 153, label: "(ii) a to o", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.656889Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.658760Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>glas</strong> — lock, <strong>poll an ghlas</strong> — 'the key (lit. lock) hole'</p><p><strong>crann</strong> — tree, <strong>barr an chroinn</strong> — 'the top of the tree'</p><p><strong>ball</strong> — place, <strong>i lár boill</strong> — 'right in the middle (lit. in the middle of the place)'</p><p>Similar to glas are: <strong>tarbh</strong> 'bull', <strong>cloigeann an toirbh</strong> 'the bull's head', <strong>sac</strong> 'sack', <strong>béal an tsoic</strong> 'the mouth of the sack'.</p><p>The following nouns are entirely irregular: <strong>muineál</strong> 'neck', <strong>greim cúl muiníl</strong> 'a grip of (the) back of (the) neck', <strong>Mícheál</strong>: <strong>cóta Mhichíl</strong> 'Mícheál's coat'; <strong>mac</strong> 'son', <strong>bean mo mhic</strong> 'my son's wife'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 320, grammar_rule_id: 154, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.669046Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.671660Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>A Pheadair!</strong> — Peadar!</p><p><strong>A Cháit Mhór!</strong> — Cáit Mhór!</p><p><strong>A Thomáis Mhóir!</strong> — Tomás Mór!</p><p><strong>A shaighdiúir!</strong> — Soldier!</p><p><strong>A dheabhail bhradaigh!</strong> — You sly devil!</p><p>(i) Nouns ending in -(e)anach</p><p><strong>A chluasán!</strong> — You fat-head!</p><p>Nouns ending in -(e)anach, (and <strong>stócach</strong> 'young man', <strong>madadh</strong> 'dog') have -aí/i:/ in place of the final syllable.</p><p><strong>A stór!</strong> — Oh darling! (lit. treasure)</p><p><strong>A bhéal mór!</strong> — You big-mouth!</p><p><strong>A chloigeann pota!</strong> — You pothead!</p><p>When inanimate nouns are used as terms of endearment or abuse, the final consonant is not made slender and a following adjective is not lenited.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 321, grammar_rule_id: 155, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.679411Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:07.680893Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Word</th><th>Diminutive</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>bád</td><td>báidín</td></tr><tr><td>fuinneog</td><td>fuinneogín</td></tr><tr><td>cró</td><td>cróin</td></tr><tr><td>geata</td><td>geaitín</td></tr><tr><td>gob</td><td>gobín</td></tr><tr><td>siopa</td><td>siopín</td></tr><tr><td>hata</td><td>haitín</td></tr><tr><td>teach</td><td>teachín</td></tr><tr><td>coileach</td><td>coileachán</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The suffix -ín may be added to all nouns. A final unstressed vowel /ə/ is dropped; see Appendix I, 5(i). Final broad consonants (except ch, e.g. teach: teachín) become slender. The gender of a noun remains the same when the basic meaning of the noun is unaltered. The pronunciation of all these forms is regular and in accordance with the tables in Appendix I. Examples of how the vowel changes are shown in the above table.</p><p class=\"note\">1. doras, doirísín 'a little door'; in a few words the medial consonant is also made slender (see Lesson 27).</p><p class=\"note\">2. coileach, coileachán, coilín; two-syllable words with the ending /ox/ can optionally drop it before the -ín. In this case a broad medial consonant is made slender.</p><p class=\"note\">3. cliabh, cliaibhín, cléibhín; fear, feairín, firín; glas, glaiseán, gloiseán; ia may optionally become é (see Lesson 27) and similarly where there is an irregular vowel change (see 3. above) the change may optionally take place.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 322, grammar_rule_id: 156, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.562800Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.564895Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo roimh Máirtín</strong> — Cáit will be here before Máirtín.</p><p><strong>Beidh Cáit anseo roimh an gCéadaoin</strong> — Cáit will be here before Wednesday.</p><p>Roimh /riw'/ causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>roimh Máirtín</strong> 'before Máirtín'. When used with the singular article it causes eclipsis to a following noun, e.g. <strong>roimh an gCéadaoin</strong> 'before Wednesday'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 323, grammar_rule_id: 156, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.565616Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.567462Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>romham</td><td>/ru:m/</td></tr><tr><td>romhat</td><td>/ru:t/</td></tr><tr><td>roimhe</td><td>/riw'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>roimpi</td><td>/ri:m'p'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>romhainn</td><td>/ru:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>romhaibh</td><td>/ru:b'/</td></tr><tr><td>rompu</td><td>/ru:mpə/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as that of <em>ag</em> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>romham, romhamsa, romham féin, romhamsa mé féin</strong>; see Lesson 16. <strong>Rompu</strong> /ru:mpə/ 'before them' has an alternative form <strong>romhab</strong> /ru:b/ which is not normally written.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 324, grammar_rule_id: 156, label: "(iii) Meaning of roimh", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.568162Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.570263Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The basic meaning is 'before, ahead of, in front of'.</p><p><strong>Bhí Máirtín anseo roimh na daoine eile</strong> — Máirtín was here before the other people.</p><p><strong>Bí ag breathnú romhat!</strong> — Look ahead of you!</p><p>More idiomatically roimh is used:</p><p>(a) to express 'on the arrival of'</p><p><strong>Bhí Cáit romham ag an traein</strong> — Cáit was at the train on my arrival.</p><p>Cáit was at the train to meet me.</p><p>(b) with <em>fáilte</em> 'welcome (for)', <em>faitíos</em> 'afraid (of)'</p><p><strong>Tá fáilte agam romhat.</strong> — I have a welcome for you.</p><p><strong>Tá faitíos agam roimh Cháit.</strong> — I am afraid of Cáit.</p><p class=\"note\">Although <em>tá faitíos orm</em> is 'I am afraid' (see Lesson 17), the preposition <em>ag</em> is now generally used in connection with <em>roimh</em>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 325, grammar_rule_id: 157, label: "(i) Introduction", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.576721Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.578353Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag breathnú trí fhuinneog eile</strong> — Cáit is looking through another window.</p><p><strong>Tá Máirtín ag breathnú tríd an bhfuinneog seo</strong> — Máirtín is looking through this window.</p><p>Trí /hri:/ 'through' causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>trí fhuinneog</strong> 'through a window'. When used with the singular article it takes the form <strong>tríd</strong> and a following noun is eclipsed, e.g. <strong>tríd an bhfuinneog</strong>.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 326, grammar_rule_id: 157, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.578963Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.580605Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>trím</td><td>/hri:m'/</td></tr><tr><td>trít</td><td>/hri:t'/</td></tr><tr><td>tríd</td><td>/hri:d'/</td></tr><tr><td>tríthi</td><td>/hri:/</td></tr><tr><td>trínn</td><td>/hri:N'/</td></tr><tr><td>tríbh</td><td>/hri:b'/</td></tr><tr><td>tríothu</td><td>/hri:b/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as that of <em>ag</em> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>trím, trímse, trím féin, trímse mé féin</strong>; see Lesson 16. Note, however, that the contrast form for the third person singular masculine is <strong>trísan</strong> 'through him'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 327, grammar_rule_id: 157, label: "(iii) Meaning of trí", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.581205Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.582792Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The basic meaning is 'through'.</p><p><strong>Feicim tríd an éadach sin</strong> — I see through that cloth.</p><p>More idiomatically, trí is used in the expressions <em>trí thine</em> 'on fire', <em>tríd is tríd</em> 'all things considered, generally speaking, on the whole', <em>trína chéile</em> 'in a mess'; <em>trí gach scéal</em> 'finally, in the end, the upshot of the matter is that...'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 328, grammar_rule_id: 158, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.585470Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.588020Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>candáil</strong> auctioning — <strong>fear na candála</strong> the auctioning man</p><p><strong>feadaíl</strong> whistling — <strong>do chuid feadaíola</strong> your whistling</p><p><strong>altóir</strong> altar — <strong>os comhair na haltóra</strong> in front of the altar</p><p>Nouns ending in <em>-áil</em>/-<em>(a)il</em> and non-agent nouns ending in <em>-óir</em>/<em>-eoir</em> form their genitive by making the final consonant broad and by adding <em>-a</em> /ə/, e.g. <strong>candáil</strong> 'auctioning', <strong>na candála</strong> 'of the auctioning'.</p><p>Agent nouns ending in <em>-óir</em>/<em>-eoir</em> frequently have no special genitive form, e.g. <strong>hata an bhádóra</strong> 'the boatman's hat'. In Official Standard Irish (and more rarely in this dialect) they are treated like <strong>altóir</strong>, e.g. <strong>hata an bhádóra</strong>, <strong>teach an mhúinteora</strong>, etc.</p><p>A limited number of nouns have similar genitive forms:</p><p><strong>bliain</strong> year — <strong>tús na bliana</strong> the beginning of the year</p><p><strong>bainríon</strong> queen — <strong>ainm na bainríona</strong> the queen's name</p><p><strong>buachaill</strong> boy — <strong>airgead an bhuachalla</strong> the boy's money</p><p><strong>deartháir</strong> brother — <strong>teach mo dhearthára</strong> my brother's house</p><p><strong>móin</strong> turf — <strong>baint mhóna</strong> cutting turf</p><p><strong>maitheas</strong> good(ness) — <strong>mórán maitheasa</strong> much good</p><p><strong>leathshúil</strong> one eye — <strong>ag dúnadh na leathshúile</strong> shutting an eye</p><p><strong>sáil</strong> heel — <strong>barr na sála</strong> the top of the heel</p><p><strong>loch</strong> lake — <strong>bruach locha</strong> a bank of a lake</p><p><strong>táilliúr</strong> tailor — <strong>bean an táilliúra</strong> the tailor's wife</p><p><strong>Samhain</strong> Hallowtide — <strong>Lá Samhna</strong> All Saints' Day</p><p><strong>Cáisc</strong> Easter — <strong>aimsir na Cásca</strong> Eastertime</p><p><strong>feoil</strong> meat — <strong>blas na feola</strong> the taste of meat</p><p>There are also the following nouns in which the vowel changes regularly according to the tables in Appendix I:</p><p><strong>gleann</strong> /æ:/ valley — <strong>muintir an ghleanna</strong> /æ:/ the people of the valley</p><p><strong>am</strong> /a:l/ time — <strong>go leor ama</strong> /a:/ plenty of time</p><p>Similar to <strong>gleann</strong> is <strong>leann</strong> /la:/ 'ale', <strong>teach leanna</strong> /æ:/ 'alehouse'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 329, grammar_rule_id: 159, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.597507Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.599404Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The genitive of some few nouns is formed by making the final consonant broad, by adding <em>-a</em> /ə/ and by changing the spelling from:</p><p>(i) <strong>oi</strong> to <strong>a</strong></p><p><strong>droim</strong> /i:/ back — <strong>lár an droma</strong> /a:/ the middle of the back</p><p>(ii) <strong>oi</strong> to <strong>o</strong></p><p><strong>troid</strong> /d/ fight — <strong>fonn troda</strong> /o/ a desire to fight</p><p>(iii) <strong>ui</strong> to <strong>o</strong></p><p><strong>fuil</strong> /l/ blood — <strong>ag cur fola</strong> /o/ bleeding</p><p>Somewhat similar to <strong>fuil</strong> is <strong>muir</strong> 'sea' which occurs in set phrases, e.g. <strong>ar an muir mhór</strong> 'on the great sea', <strong>cuan mara</strong> 'sea urchin'.</p><p>(iv) <strong>io</strong> to <strong>ea</strong></p><p><strong>sioc</strong> /u/ frost — <strong>ag cur sheaca</strong> /tail/ frosting</p><p>(v) <strong>ei</strong> to <strong>ea</strong></p><p><strong>greim</strong> /l:/ bite — <strong>de bharr an ghreama</strong> /æ:/ on account of the bite</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 330, grammar_rule_id: 160, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.611690Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.613848Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>spéisiúil</strong> interesting — <strong>níos spéisiúla</strong> more interesting</p><p><strong>socair</strong> steady — <strong>níos socra</strong> steadier</p><p><strong>deacair</strong> difficult — <strong>níos deacra</strong> more difficult</p><p><strong>cóir</strong> fair — <strong>níos córa</strong> fairer</p><p>All adjectives ending in <em>-úil</em>, and the adjectives <em>socair</em>, <em>deacair</em> and <em>cóir</em> form their comparative by making the final consonant broad and by adding <em>-a</em> /ə/.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 331, grammar_rule_id: 161, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.618066Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:29:54.619420Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Bhí(odh) an seomra seo ní ba dorcha.</strong> — This room was/used to be/would be darker.</p><p><strong>Bheadh an seomra ba dorcha anseo.</strong> — The darkest room was/used to be/would be here.</p><p>When a sentence is in a past tense or in the conditional, <em>ní ba</em> /N'i: bə/ frequently replaces <em>níos</em> in a comparison, and <em>ba</em> replaces <em>is</em> in a superlative. (<em>Ba</em> is the relative past/conditional of the copula; see Lesson 31.) <em>Níos</em> is in fact made up of <em>ní</em> 'thing' and the present relative of the copula.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 332, grammar_rule_id: 162, label: "(i)", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.095602Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.097570Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>Shiúil Cáit thar theach eile.</strong> — Cáit walked past another house.</p><p><strong>Shiúil Cáit thar an bhfear sin.</strong> — Cáit walked past that man.</p><p>Thar /ha:r/, /hæ:r/ 'past' causes lenition to a directly following noun, e.g. <strong>thar theach</strong> 'past a house', except in certain adverbial phrases, e.g. <strong>thar sáile</strong> (lit. 'over brine') 'beyond the sea, overseas'; <strong>thar barr</strong> (lit. 'past top') 'outstanding'; <strong>thar cionn</strong> 'excellent'. When used with the singular article a following noun is eclipsed, e.g. <strong>thar an bhfear</strong> 'past the man'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 333, grammar_rule_id: 162, label: "(ii) Prepositional pronouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.098216Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.100113Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinary form</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>tharam</td><td>past me /ha:rmə/</td></tr><tr><td>tharat</td><td>past you /ha:rdə/</td></tr><tr><td>thairis</td><td>past him/it /hæ:r'əs'/</td></tr><tr><td>thairti</td><td>past her/it /ha:rt'ə/</td></tr><tr><td>tharainn</td><td>past us /ha:rNə'/</td></tr><tr><td>tharaibh</td><td>past you (pl.) /ha:ri:/</td></tr><tr><td>thartu</td><td>past them /ha:rtbə/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contrast forms and general usage are the same as that of <strong>ag</strong> and most other prepositional pronouns, e.g. <strong>tharam, tharamsa, tharam féin, tharamsa mé féin</strong>; see Lesson 16. Note, however, that the contrast form of <strong>thairis</strong> is <strong>thar san</strong> /ha:rsNə/.</p><p>Tharat, thartu have the alternative forms <strong>thartat</strong> /hairtdə/ and <strong>tharu</strong> /hairbə/ resp., though these are not normally written. <strong>Tharaibh</strong> has the alternative pronunciation /ha:rbə'/.</p><p>In the impersonal use of the third person singular <strong>thairis</strong> is replaced by <strong>thart</strong> 'over, around'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 334, grammar_rule_id: 162, label: "(iii) Meaning of thar", position: 3, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.100739Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.102643Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>The basic meaning is 'past, beyond, over':</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit ag siúl thar an ngeata.</strong> — Cáit is walking past the gate.</p><p><strong>Tá an madadh ag caitheamh léim thar an mbosca.</strong> — The dog is jumping over the box.</p><p><strong>Chaith Cáit thar bhliain ansin.</strong> — Cáit spent over a year there.</p><p>More idiomatically <strong>thar</strong> is used:</p><p>(a) with <strong>a bheith</strong> to express 'outstandingly, extremely'</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit thar a bheith go maith.</strong> — Cáit is extremely good.</p><p>(b) with <strong>mar</strong> to express 'compared with how'</p><p><strong>Tá Cáit go maith thar mar a bhí sí inné.</strong> — Cáit is well compared with how she was yesterday.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 335, grammar_rule_id: 163, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.109170Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.111526Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p><strong>céir</strong> wax — <strong>baladh na céarach</strong> the smell of wax</p><p><strong>cathair</strong> city — <strong>muintir na cathrach</strong> the people of the city</p><p><strong>glúin</strong> knee — <strong>caipín na glúnach</strong> the kneecap</p><p><strong>súil</strong> eye — <strong>os cionn na súlach</strong> above the eye</p><p>A limited number of feminine nouns form their genitive by making the final consonant broad and by adding -ach /xə/, e.g. <strong>céir</strong> 'wax', <strong>na céarach</strong> 'of the wax'. It will be noticed that most of these nouns are pronounced with one long syllable and a final slender r, l, n. If the noun is spelt with two short syllables, e.g. <strong>cathair</strong> 'city', the second syllable is dropped when -ach is added: <strong>na cathrach</strong> 'of the city'.</p><p>Further examples are:</p><p><strong>lair</strong> mare — <strong>searrach lárach</strong> a female foal</p><p><strong>cathaoir</strong> chair — <strong>tóin na cathaorach</strong> the seat of the chair</p><p><strong>traein</strong> train — <strong>doras na traenach</strong> the door of the train</p><p><strong>tóin</strong> bottom — <strong>póca tónach</strong> hip pocket</p><p><strong>coróin</strong> crown — <strong>ar shon na corónach</strong> for the sake of the crown</p><p><strong>cráin</strong> sow — <strong>cloigeann na cránach</strong> the sow's head</p><p><strong>stail</strong> stallion — <strong>cloigeann na stalach</strong> the stallion's head</p><p><strong>caora</strong> sheep — <strong>craiceann na caorach</strong> sheepskin</p><p>The medial r also becomes broad in <strong>caoireoil</strong> 'mutton': <strong>ceathrú caorólach</strong> 'a quarter (pound) of mutton'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 336, grammar_rule_id: 164, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.119026Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.120690Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>These feminine nouns form their genitive by making the final consonant broad and by adding -ach /xə/, and by changing the spelling from:</p><p>(i) <strong>oi</strong> to <strong>o</strong></p><p><strong>toil</strong> /i/ will — <strong>in aghaidh mo tholach</strong> against my will</p><p>(ii) <strong>ui</strong> to <strong>o</strong></p><p><strong>cuid</strong> /i/ share — <strong>ag iarraidh a chodach</strong> wanting his share (of food)</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 337, grammar_rule_id: 165, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.122992Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.124940Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>A few feminine nouns form their genitive by adding -n /N'/:</p><p><strong>comharsa</strong> neighbour — <strong>teach na comharsan</strong> the neighbour's house</p><p><strong>lacha</strong> duck — <strong>cois na lachan</strong> the duck's foot</p><p><strong>ceathrú</strong> thigh — <strong>os cionn na ceathrún</strong> above the thigh</p><p><strong>An Cheathrú Rua</strong> (place-name) — <strong>muintir na Ceathrún Rua</strong> the people of 'Carraroe'</p><p>We should further note: <strong>Éire</strong> 'Ireland', <strong>muintir na hÉireann</strong> 'the people of Ireland', <strong>in Éirinn</strong> 'in Ireland'. The form <strong>Éirinn</strong> is used after prepositions and also optionally as nominative.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 338, grammar_rule_id: 166, label: "(i) Abstract nouns formed from adjectives", position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.130123Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.133051Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><p>(a) with -(e)acht</p><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Comparative</th><th>Noun</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>bán</td><td>níos báine</td><td>báineacht</td></tr><tr><td>dearg</td><td>níos deirge</td><td>deirgeacht</td></tr><tr><td>oilte</td><td>níos oilte</td><td>oilteacht</td></tr><tr><td>barúil</td><td>níos barúla</td><td>barúlacht</td></tr><tr><td>deacair</td><td>níos deacra</td><td>deacracht</td></tr><tr><td>aibí</td><td>níos aibí</td><td>aibíocht</td></tr></tbody></table><p>To form an abstract noun the ending -(e)acht /xət/ is normally added to the comparative form of an adjective. The ending -eacht follows a slender consonant, e.g. <strong>báine</strong>, <strong>báineacht</strong>; the ending -acht follows a broad consonant, e.g. <strong>deacra</strong>, <strong>deacracht</strong>, or by spelling convention (see Appendix 1.6) the ending -ocht follows í, e.g. <strong>aibí</strong>, <strong>aibíocht</strong>.</p><p>A few adjectives with no special comparative form add -iocht /i:əxt/, e.g. <strong>éasca</strong> 'easy, quick', <strong>éascaíocht</strong> 'easiness, quickness'; <strong>cliste</strong> 'clever', <strong>clisteacht</strong> 'cleverness'; <strong>sásta</strong> 'satisfied', <strong>sástaíocht</strong> 'satisfaction'.</p><p>(b) with -(e)as -(e)adas</p><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Noun</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>maith</td><td>maitheas</td></tr><tr><td>buíoch</td><td>buíochas</td></tr><tr><td>tinn /i:/</td><td>tinneas /i/</td></tr><tr><td>beo</td><td>beos</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Some adjectives of one syllable form an abstract noun by adding -(e)as; -eas follows a slender consonant, e.g. <strong>maith</strong>, <strong>maitheas</strong>; the ending -as follows a broad consonant, e.g. <strong>buíoch</strong>, <strong>buíochas</strong>; by spelling convention s follows o: <strong>beo</strong>, <strong>beos</strong>.</p><p>Somewhat similar are <strong>te</strong> /t'e/ 'hot', <strong>teas</strong> /t'æ:s/ 'heat'; <strong>soibhir</strong> /səw'ər'/ 'rich', <strong>soibhreas</strong> /saiw'r'əs/ 'richness'.</p><p>(c) with -(e)adas</p><table><tr><th>Adjective</th><th>Noun</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>ciúin</td><td>ciúineadas</td></tr><tr><td>binn /vJ</td><td>binneadas / i/</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Similar to the above are: <strong>úr</strong> 'fresh', <strong>úireadas</strong> 'freshness' (the final r becomes slender); <strong>dorcha</strong> 'dark', <strong>dorchadas</strong> 'darkness'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 339, grammar_rule_id: 166, label: "(ii) Abstract nouns formed from agent nouns", position: 2, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.133702Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.142843Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Agent noun</th><th>Abstract noun</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>bádóir</td><td>bádóireacht</td></tr><tr><td>feilméara</td><td>feilméaracht</td></tr><tr><td>sclábhaí</td><td>sclábhaíocht</td></tr><tr><td>siopadóir</td><td>siopadóireacht</td></tr><tr><td>cabaire</td><td>cabaireacht</td></tr></tbody></table><p>To form an abstract noun the ending -(e)acht is often added to an agent noun; for the selection of the variants -eacht, -acht, -ocht see (a) above. Agent nouns in -úr become slender and add -eacht, e.g. <strong>táilliúr</strong>, 'tailor', <strong>táilliúireacht</strong> 'tailoring'.</p><p>Abstract nouns of this sort can be used as verbal nouns, e.g. <strong>ag feilméaracht</strong> 'farming'.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
gs = GrammarSection.create!(id: 340, grammar_rule_id: 167, label: nil, position: 1, created_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.144095Z", updated_at: "2026-04-11T23:30:55.147167Z")
gs.body = "<!-- BEGIN app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><div class=\"lexxy-content\">\n  <!-- BEGIN /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb\n--><table><tr><th>Ordinal</th><th>Pronunciation</th></tr><tbody><tr><td>1st an chéad cheann</td><td>/ə x'e:d/</td></tr><tr><td>2nd an dara ceann</td><td>/ə da:rə/</td></tr><tr><td>3rd an tríú ceann</td><td>/ə t'r'i:wu:/</td></tr><tr><td>4th an ceathrú ceann</td><td>/ə k'æ:ru:/</td></tr><tr><td>5th an cúigiú ceann</td><td>/ə ku:g'u:/</td></tr><tr><td>6th an séú ceann</td><td>/ə s'e:u:/</td></tr><tr><td>7th an seachtú ceann</td><td>/ə s'æ:xtu:/</td></tr><tr><td>8th an t-ochtú ceann</td><td>/ə toxtu:/</td></tr><tr><td>9th an naoú ceann</td><td>/ə Ni:u:/</td></tr><tr><td>10th an deichiú ceann</td><td>/ə d'eu:/</td></tr><tr><td>11th an t-aonú ceann déag</td><td>/ə ti:Nu: ... d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>12th an dóú ceann déag</td><td>/ə do:u: ... d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>13th an tríú ceann déag</td><td>/ə t'r'i:u: ... d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>19th an naoú ceann déag</td><td>/ə Ni:u: ... d'e:g/</td></tr><tr><td>20th an fichid ceann</td><td>/ə f'i:u:/</td></tr><tr><td>21st an t-aonú ceann fichead</td><td>/ə ti:Nu: ... f'i:d/</td></tr><tr><td>22nd an dóú ceann fichead</td><td>/ə do:u: ... f'i:d/</td></tr><tr><td>23rd an tríú ceann fichead</td><td>/ə t'r'i:u: ... f'i:d/</td></tr><tr><td>30th an deichiú ceann fichead</td><td>/ə d'eu: ... f'i:d/</td></tr><tr><td>31st an t-aonú ceann déag is fiche</td><td>/ə ti:Nu: ... d'e:g əs fi/</td></tr><tr><td>32nd an dóú ceann déag is fiche</td><td>/ə do:u: ... .../</td></tr><tr><td>33rd an tríú ceann déag is fiche</td><td>/ə t'r'i:u: ... .../</td></tr><tr><td>39th an naoú ceann déag is fiche</td><td>/ə Ni:u: ... .../</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The ordinal numbers can be seen in the above table. <strong>An chéad</strong> 'the First' lenites a following noun (except those beginning with t, d), e.g. <strong>an chéad bhord</strong> 'the first table', <strong>an chéad duine</strong> 'the first person'. <strong>An chéad</strong> used with <strong>eile</strong> means 'the next', e.g. <strong>an chéad uair eile</strong> 'the next time', <strong>an chéad bhliain eile</strong> 'next year'. All the ordinal numbers except <strong>an chéad</strong> prefix h to a following vowel, e.g. <strong>an dara halt</strong> 'the second place', <strong>an tríú húlla</strong> 'the third apple'.</p><p>Ordinals above thirty-nine are generally avoided by the use of circumlocution, e.g. <strong>sin (anois) dhá fhichead leabhar</strong> 'that there (now) is forty books', <strong>sin (anois) leabhar is dhá fhichead</strong> 'that there (now) is forty-one books'; or by using the older system.</p>\n<!-- END /Users/seosamh/.rbenv/versions/ruby-3.4.2/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/actiontext-8.1.3/app/views/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb --></div>\n<!-- END app/views/layouts/action_text/contents/_content.html.erb -->"; gs.save!
